{"wave":"infrastructure","provider":"linear","initiative":"218967b6-a760-4b7c-9a46-11d9d61a42c2","project":null,"synced_at":1790171715,"projects":[{"id":"03c59a52-dbd4-4e11-a6ac-f6d6359c8e08","slug":"reliability","name":"Reliability","summary":"Maintainers can keep Loopflow advancing its own work and delivering verified releases without repeatedly repairing its coordination machinery. Scheduled delivery reaches truthful, recoverable outcomes; planning and execution remain usable across credentia","definition":"Maintainers can keep Loopflow advancing its own work and delivering verified releases without repeatedly repairing its coordination machinery. Scheduled delivery reaches truthful, recoverable outcomes; planning and execution remain usable across credential expiry, process failure, and upgrades. Recovery preserves work, history, and exact control authority.","flows":{"first":null,"loop":null,"finally":null},"krs":[{"text":"Throughout the chapter, every configured scheduled release opportunity is durably accounted for as on-time, caught up, deferred with a recorded reason and continuation, or failed with an actionable cause. Two consecutive opportunities settle through the configured path as a truthful no-op or complete exact-tag publication; at least one publishes artifacts available to users, all required verification and exact-tag smoke checks pass, and neither settlement requires manual repair. Required scheduled verification failures remain visible and receive an owning repair disposition within one day.","holds":false},{"text":"Across fourteen consecutive days of real Loopflow self-hosting, every attempted core planning or execution operation reaches observable progress, an intentional declared wait, or one bounded, truthful, actionable failure. Ordinary credential expiry with a usable refresh grant does not interrupt planning; recoverable process or controller failures resume without manual state repair. There are no hidden outages, duplicate advancements, lost durable input, terminal-truth regressions, or false availability claims. Recovery preserves assigned-worktree isolation, caller edits, durable history, and exact process authority.","holds":false}],"initiative_ids":["218967b6-a760-4b7c-9a46-11d9d61a42c2"],"team_ids":["3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3"]},{"id":"62b73cde-5057-4959-8ad8-fca96b9e80b5","slug":"technical-architecture","name":"Technical Architecture","summary":"Loopflow architecture is legible from the top down: key data structures, authorities, process boundaries, and public APIs explain the system; implementation follows that map; obsolete concepts do not linger as alternate designs.","definition":"Loopflow architecture is legible from the top down: key data structures, authorities, process boundaries, and public APIs explain the system; implementation follows that map; obsolete concepts do not linger as alternate designs.","flows":{"first":null,"loop":null,"finally":null},"krs":[{"text":"A top-down architecture map names every durable control owner, truth source, process boundary, and public API; four consecutive weekly drift checks find zero unnamed owner, mirror, or shim.","holds":false},{"text":"For 30 consecutive days, every landed PR maps cleanly to documented architecture concepts or updates the map in the same PR.","holds":false},{"text":"Stale pre-Run, pre-Home, and pre-native-PM language reaches zero outside migrations and historical release material and stays zero for 30 days.","holds":false}],"initiative_ids":["218967b6-a760-4b7c-9a46-11d9d61a42c2"],"team_ids":["3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3"]},{"id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","slug":"performance-efficiency","name":"Performance & Efficiency","summary":"Ordinary Loopflow work is fast and economical for humans, agents, and machines. Setup, builds, verification, navigation, worktrees, landing, and resource use stay within explicit budgets and require no avoidable manual work.","definition":"Ordinary Loopflow work is fast and economical for humans, agents, and machines. Setup, builds, verification, navigation, worktrees, landing, and resource use stay within explicit budgets and require no avoidable manual work.","flows":{"first":null,"loop":null,"finally":null},"krs":[{"text":"Task launch-to-first-progress, pre-land verification, and land-to-merge each have named p50 and p95 budgets and stay within them for 30 days.","holds":false},{"text":"Thirty consecutive landings report every local pre-land verification stage against its budget.","holds":false},{"text":"Seven consecutive days of normal development require zero avoidable setup or repair steps and zero manual Git surgery.","holds":false},{"text":"Build artifacts, disk use, CPU, and agent spend remain within named budgets for 30 days without disrupting work.","holds":false}],"initiative_ids":["218967b6-a760-4b7c-9a46-11d9d61a42c2"],"team_ids":["3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3"]}],"items":[{"id":"15172e70-1ec8-4cb0-a15c-f84d49361381","identifier":"LOO-279","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-279/make-linear-oauth-recovery-survive-unattended-project-runs","name":"Make Linear OAuth recovery survive unattended Project runs","description":"Observed 2026-09-22: Technical Architecture, Performance & Efficiency, and Stability & Security Project runs all stopped before plan refresh because the stored Linear access token expired and automatic refresh was rejected. Restore unattended refresh so headless Project planning continues across token expiry; persist rotated refresh credentials atomically; prove the installed path across expiry and a rejected refresh response; keep reconnect guidance Doppler-aware when user action is genuinely required.","rank":0,"completed":false,"project_id":"03c59a52-dbd4-4e11-a6ac-f6d6359c8e08","project":"reliability","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"7d5302d8-c861-41f0-8996-5b52312cc2f2","identifier":"LOO-277","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-277/make-mutable-release-worktrees-re-enter-without-losing-preparation","name":"Make mutable release worktrees re-enter without losing preparation state","description":"Audit from LOO-259 found a separate restart gap. release_run and finish_release_pr both materialize stable release-<target>-<version> paths through create_named_worktree without holding a path lease. The initial tree is mutated by manifest bumps, repository hooks, and release-note generation before any remote PR head exists; an interruption leaves potentially unique dirty or unpushed bytes that exact-source publisher recovery cannot safely discard. Once an OPEN PR exists, GitHub head_ref_oid is authoritative and reintegration already fetches the exact head, hard-resets to current main, and reruns preparation, but a stale local path or branch makes generic creation fail first. A live release owner also cannot exclude lf wt remove because the lease is acquired only by cleanup.\n\nOutcome: mutable release preparation and reintegration define an evidence-preserving restart policy under an exact path lease. Before a remote PR head exists, preserve unknown dirty or unpushed bytes and report an actionable recovery boundary; never force-remove them. After GitHub supplies an exact PR head, safely converge matching clean partial state into the existing rebuild-from-current-main path, while dirty, divergent, differently registered, and live-owned state remains unchanged. Do not reuse the immutable publisher helper or rebase prepared release artifacts.\n\nProof: production-shaped interruption fixtures cover pre-commit dirty state, post-push clean state, stale branch-only or missing-body registration, dirty or divergent state, and concurrent removal during both initial preparation and reintegration. Exact-head rebuild still reruns every preparation step on current main, and retries neither lose hook output nor publish stale manifests or notes. Start with a scratch design; commit 0683cfe6, PR #1187, and v0.12.6 are the preserved evidence for rebuild-over-rebase.","rank":1,"completed":true,"project_id":"03c59a52-dbd4-4e11-a6ac-f6d6359c8e08","project":"reliability","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"38644d0a-7a30-4127-8caf-2d76c13db015","identifier":"LOO-273","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-273/make-pr-landing-repair-observable-and-terminating","name":"Make PR landing repair observable and terminating","description":"Make PR landing repair observable and terminating\n\n<!-- loopflow-task-start:18ed9d63e826489a7c6a68f704954b771aef3706d3e2c81edca369a5f2edc8e6 -->","rank":2,"completed":true,"project_id":"03c59a52-dbd4-4e11-a6ac-f6d6359c8e08","project":"reliability","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"bb8c6014-279a-4028-be3d-e01ea38ceb78","identifier":"LOO-266","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-266/restore-caller-edits-after-incomplete-release-synchronization","name":"Restore caller edits after incomplete release synchronization","description":"Observed 2026-08-22 while resuming v0.12.14 through the scheduled release-run. sync_main auto-stashed overlapping caller edits, including Infrastructure Wave memory. Artifact download then timed out on nightly-x86_64-apple-darwin; the target process exited successfully, publication remained incomplete, and the exact caller memory file was back at its pre-edit content. This is distinct from LOO-259 publisher-worktree ownership and LOO-261 clean-host candidate validation.\\n\\nOutcome: release synchronization never consumes or strands caller worktree changes. Use a dedicated release worktree or a scoped restoration guard so every terminal path—published, resumable failure, transport timeout, or validation failure—returns the caller branch, index, and working bytes exactly as found while preserving tagged release state. A restoration conflict fails with durable, actionable recovery evidence and never silently drops the stash.\\n\\nProof: a release-shaped fixture starts with tracked and untracked caller edits, resumes an existing tag, and injects failure after sync at artifact download and candidate validation. Each exit leaves caller bytes and status identical, retains one resumable release identity, and a later retry publishes once. The success path has the same caller-state guarantee. Start with a scratch design because this changes release recovery ownership.","rank":3,"completed":true,"project_id":"03c59a52-dbd4-4e11-a6ac-f6d6359c8e08","project":"reliability","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"52c7cbaf-0139-4a0e-b1e3-d277f63295ba","identifier":"LOO-262","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-262/read-historical-ci-incident-runs-during-local-promotion","name":"Read historical CI-incident Runs during local promotion","description":"Read historical CI-incident Runs during local promotion\n\n<!-- loopflow-task-start:08e05448e16ab4d475dae1ee292d3cb5d7b9fdcd4f776d0fc856169bc9d010e0 -->","rank":4,"completed":true,"project_id":"03c59a52-dbd4-4e11-a6ac-f6d6359c8e08","project":"reliability","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"c7288e7e-ba26-4a40-81a4-ab9cf4296456","identifier":"LOO-263","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-263/settle-merged-task-work-atomically-from-after-merge-policy","name":"Settle merged Task Work atomically from after-merge policy","description":"Observed 2026-08-22 on LOO-226 after PR #1229 merged. The PR record carries `after_merge=complete_task` and Linear is complete, but durable Task Work remains Running with its process alive; the latest durable event is only `pr_merged`, and status recommends a later manual `complete`. A bounded terminal wait did not settle it. Completed LOO-66 corrected impossible action recommendations, not this controller transition.\n\nOutcome: observing the exact merged PR under `after_merge=complete_task` atomically persists the merge fact, terminal Task lifecycle, terminal Work Epoch, Run/Invocation settlement, and exactly one completion event. Process liveness cannot turn the controller-owned transition into an agent follow-up. Reconciliation is idempotent and never mints a synthetic Run.\n\nProof: a production-shaped fixture begins with a live Task Run and an exact merged PR carrying `complete_task`; one reconciliation leaves Task and Work terminal, settles the existing Run/Invocation, records one completion event, and exposes no manual completion action. Repeated reconciliation changes no counts. A stale or non-exact PR cannot settle the Task. Fault injection at event persistence leaves the pre-transition state intact and recoverable. Start with a scratch design because this changes the worker/controller lifecycle boundary.","rank":5,"completed":true,"project_id":"03c59a52-dbd4-4e11-a6ac-f6d6359c8e08","project":"reliability","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"9a805e26-0dcf-4f69-bad4-d2eeba141df6","identifier":"LOO-265","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-265/make-task-promotion-recovery-depend-on-live-process-truth","name":"Make Task promotion recovery depend on live process truth","description":"## Objective\n\nRestore Task availability by making Task-only execution, recovery, and local promotion depend on the current Task Run and direct owned-process observation. Provider invocation, trace, journal, and Exec receipts remain diagnostic metadata and cannot veto that decision.\n\n## Scope\n\n* Remove `prove_absent_run_owners` and both control-path calls. When current Run containment is absent, use the existing cascading recovery to settle the Run and dependent Invocation/Turn state as interrupted or unknown and clear the Task slot.\n* Missing journal context, trace-capture initialization, or later capture writes must not prevent the Task body from running or settling. Preserve a partial-capture warning; remove `trace_capture_blocker` as a non-resumable control outcome.\n* Keep provider resume behavior. Keep exact-owner signaling fail-closed: an unprovable or reused process is never signaled.\n* Limit installed Task promotion to Task/Run process truth. Project/Wave lifecycle adoption and validation are advisory or out of scope for this slice.\n\n## Proof\n\n* An absent Task process with an open Invocation and missing terminal trace or Exec receipts settles and promotion continues.\n* A Task with missing journal context or failed capture initialization/write still executes and settles; status reports partial capture.\n* A foreign, reused, or unprovable live process is never signaled.\n* Existing resume-token behavior remains unchanged.\n* Production-shaped regressions based on LOO-262 and LOO-265 no longer strand a Task on telemetry completeness.\n\n## Not in this slice\n\n* No new supervisor or ProcessOwner schema.\n* No control/trace storage split.\n* No broad liveness or Invocation/Turn type deletion beyond code made dead by this change.\n* No Project/Wave control redesign.\n* No distributed lease or compatibility shim.\n\n<!-- loopflow-task-start:f99d824c429be756188577b2195175f5f7e7cafc2356b490671156c7e7972f4b -->","rank":6,"completed":true,"project_id":"03c59a52-dbd4-4e11-a6ac-f6d6359c8e08","project":"reliability","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"3e92e091-4e2b-4b9e-848c-28b741bfcab8","identifier":"LOO-264","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-264/make-home-upgrade-promotion-retries-idempotent","name":"Make Home-upgrade promotion retries idempotent","description":"Observed 2026-08-22 while using the supported published refresh from lf 0.12.12 to v0.12.13. Promotion preflight proved the candidate could read 94 persisted lifecycles and safely advance the store, then activation failed with UNIQUE constraint failed: home_upgrade_work.upgrade_id, home_upgrade_work.work_kind, home_upgrade_work.work_id. The installer preserved the 0.12.12 binary and 0.12.12.001_release store frontier. An earlier refused 0.12.14 local promotion had inspected the same active Work set, so a preflight or retry may have left or reused an upgrade identity unexpectedly.\\n\\nOutcome: local and published promotion reserve one exact HomeUpgrade transaction and attach each Work at most once. A refused preflight writes no upgrade rows. Re-entry after any interruption adopts the exact transaction or creates a new non-colliding one, without deleting evidence, bypassing lifecycle checks, or requiring operator database repair.\\n\\nProof: a production-shaped fixture runs a refused candidate preflight followed by a compatible published promotion over the same active Work set; the second promotion completes without a uniqueness error. Repeat at every reservation/attachment interruption boundary and prove one upgrade receipt, one row per Work, unchanged state after refusal, and idempotent same-candidate re-entry. Start with a scratch design because this changes the Home upgrade transaction boundary.","rank":7,"completed":true,"project_id":"03c59a52-dbd4-4e11-a6ac-f6d6359c8e08","project":"reliability","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"2e903b54-a62e-4e3b-b747-3d1c8e95f7a5","identifier":"LOO-261","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-261/make-release-candidate-validation-work-from-a-clean-host","name":"Make release candidate validation work from a clean host","description":"Observed 2026-08-21 during the scheduled v0.12.14 release. The release PR merged and the exact tag existed, but final publication failed after artifact download when scripts/publish_release.py ran the downloaded arm64 lf candidate as install preflight --json with LF_CONTROL_DB_PATH pointing at an uninitialized scratch database. The command exited 1, and the wrapper surfaced only CalledProcessError, hiding the candidate diagnostic.\n\nOutcome: release candidate validation succeeds on a clean publisher with no pre-existing Loopflow control database, never opens or mutates the live Home, and preserves the exact candidate failure reason when validation is genuinely unsafe. Adapt the validation context or provide a stateless candidate check; do not weaken install safety or add a live-store bypass.\n\nProof: a release-shaped downloaded artifact passes candidate validation in an empty temporary Home and proceeds to publication; an incompatible candidate fails before publication with a sanitized actionable reason; the live control database remains byte-untouched; same-tag re-entry resumes the tagged v0.12.14 attempt through the publisher-state ownership contract in LOO-259. This is a successor to terminal LOO-215, not a reopening of it.","rank":8,"completed":true,"project_id":"03c59a52-dbd4-4e11-a6ac-f6d6359c8e08","project":"reliability","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"52591bd3-0d34-4125-902c-c58ed786386b","identifier":"LOO-255","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-255/tests-never-touch-the-live-loopflow-home-or-inherit-ambient-lf-state","name":"Tests never touch the live Loopflow home or inherit ambient LF_* state","description":"STANDING QUALITY FRONTIER, not a one-off. Tests must run against their own state — never the live `~/.lf` control-plane DB, and never with ambient `LF_*` / writer-authority env inherited from the Run that launched them. When they leak, the local gate passes but CI (or another machine) fails, and it looks like a flake instead of a missing boundary.\n\nEVIDENCE (recurring):\n\n* #1003 fixed one slice: ambient `LF_*` via EnvGuard's `AMBIENT_TASK_ENV`. Treated as a point fix, so the frontier has no owner.\n* ci-fix on PR #1230 surfaced THREE more in one run: (1) a `git_operation` test saves the ambient writer token but never clears it; (2) a provider-broker test's spawned broker drops `LF_HOME` and reaches the live home; (3) a `wave_context` test opened this Run's live SQLite DB (0.12.13 draft) instead of its fixture.\n\nINTENT — smaller, simpler, and more FLEXIBLE, so we get in our own way less. This is NOT 'lock every test in a rigid sandbox' and NOT more guards/allowlists. The leaks exist because the boundary is rigid in the wrong way: tests assume a fixed ambient home and each one opts out by hand. The fix is to make the system ADAPT to however it's launched — from a live Run, clean CI, or another machine — and resolve the right state for that context automatically, so a test just works wherever it runs. Flexibility over preconditions: fewer hard rules to satisfy, less ambient coupling, a smaller surface where a leak is even possible. Same through-line as 'accept any context, then adapt' (see the main-agents-move-off-canonical work).\n\nPROOF that closes it: the full Rust suite run from inside a live Run/Task Session yields identical results to clean CI, with LESS isolation code than today — per-test scrubbing and the growing `AMBIENT_TASK_ENV` allowlist shrink or disappear because the system adapts to context instead of demanding tests conform to it.\n\nSee memory: cargo-test-inside-task-session.","rank":9,"completed":true,"project_id":"03c59a52-dbd4-4e11-a6ac-f6d6359c8e08","project":"reliability","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"1d6723a8-2fe5-465d-b7f5-04586033dbff","identifier":"LOO-243","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-243/carry-run-execution-context-across-async-work-runners","name":"Carry Run execution context across async Work runners","description":"Observed 2026-08-20 across LOO-226, LOO-229, and LOO-240: each Task runner launches a harness, then records a non-resumable failure because trace_capture_context() finds no active RunContext. The CLI establishes journal::RUN_CONTEXT as thread_local state, while WorkRunner uses Runtime::new().block_on(run_work); Task execution may therefore continue on a Tokio worker thread where the process's context is absent. Project runner currently drops the same missing capture silently. Outcome: pass one explicit, exact Run execution context through Project and Task Work runners across async thread changes; establish it before any provider launch; preserve Run/process identity and authority without inferring from environment or minting a second context; and give Project and Task runners the same fail-closed semantics. Proof: a production-shaped multithreaded __work fixture runs Project and Task bodies with trace capture bound to the supervising Run, records no duplicate Exec/Turn receipts, and demonstrates that a genuinely absent or mismatched context fails before harness launch with an actionable error. This is distinct from LOO-227, which owns durable Project-to-child control Basis.","rank":10,"completed":true,"project_id":"03c59a52-dbd4-4e11-a6ac-f6d6359c8e08","project":"reliability","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"815e7193-54b5-4ff3-8d58-020923795e86","identifier":"LOO-240","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-240/restore-roadmap-and-status-when-persisted-task-values-are-unknown","name":"Restore roadmap and status when persisted Task values are unknown","description":"## Observed failure\n\nOn 2026-08-20, `lf status product --json` exited without a snapshot because one durable row failed validation: `invalid Task: merge request requires reviewer-facing PR copy`. `lf roadmap --json` returned the Wave envelope but marked the entire Project projection unavailable for the same reason. In the same run, `lf pm show --no-sync --json` successfully returned the current three-Project Product snapshot and its Tasks.\n\nCompleted [LOO-202](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-202/expose-one-fleet-snapshot-from-wave-to-raw-trace) established that one stale Project must not erase current Work. Invalid Task validation is a second failure mode at the same product boundary.\n\n## Outcome\n\nFocused status and fleet roadmap remain useful when one durable Task cannot decode. Preserve every valid Wave, Project, Task, Run, and attention row; surface the exact invalid Work as scoped integrity evidence with a legal recovery action. Do not silently coerce the invalid directive, invent defaults, query raw SQLite from clients, or hide the failure.\n\n## Proof\n\nA shared contract fixture contains valid current Work beside one Task whose directive fails validation. `lf status <wave> --json` and `lf roadmap --json` both succeed, retain the valid hierarchy, identify the offending Task and reason, and expose the same recovery semantics. Add a regression test for the exact `merge request requires reviewer-facing PR copy` case.\n\n## Consolidation\n\nThis Task is the sole owner of the invalid persisted-Task resilience fix. It absorbs LOO-233 and duplicate incident placeholder LOO-239. LOO-229 retains the broader current-truth versus historical-state contract; this Task owns degradation of mixed-version or invalid Task rows in roadmap and status readers.","rank":11,"completed":true,"project_id":"03c59a52-dbd4-4e11-a6ac-f6d6359c8e08","project":"reliability","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"3a72d757-b396-4af1-83d6-d5b6a8ecda6b","identifier":"LOO-241","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-241/make-ledger-continuity-failures-match-scheduled-obligations","name":"Make ledger continuity failures match scheduled obligations","description":"Evidence: the 2026-08-20 telemetry-daily run on release 0.12.10 failed doctor continuity for eight gap days, 2026-08-04 through 2026-08-11, before the telemetry scorecard could run. Existing LOO-219 covers historical capture loss, not cadence continuity.\n\nOutcome: doctor judges continuity against durable scheduler obligations. A day before cadence activation or without an expected run is not silently treated as an outage; a genuinely missed scheduled run names the cron, Home, expected interval, and missing receipt actionably. Unrecoverable historical gaps do not keep every future telemetry run permanently red.\n\nProof: on a copied production-shaped store, explain whether each August 4-11 gap was expected from durable activation and receipt evidence; prove an expected missing day fails with the exact owning cron and Home; prove a pre-activation day is outside the denominator; prove a later successful receipt restores the current window without editing historical observations; and prove telemetry-daily reaches its scorecard once only terminal historical gaps remain.","rank":12,"completed":true,"project_id":"03c59a52-dbd4-4e11-a6ac-f6d6359c8e08","project":"reliability","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"9500cecb-4e4a-4c0d-88b1-0186c5ddeb69","identifier":"LOO-226","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-226/install-local-builds-without-destroying-live-work","name":"Install local builds without destroying live work","description":"User outcome\n\nA developer can promote a locally built main or branch through the protected installation transaction used by releases. The first local install uses a disposable clone of the reliable published store; compatible local builds reuse that clone. The installed CLI, daemon, keeper, macOS app, and helpers agree on the selected artifact and Home. A later published release returns to the reliable store without importing dev data. Promotion never destroys live work to make progress.\n\nOne promotion contract\n\n* Route local and published artifacts through one machine-global promotion lock, exact preflight, activation, recovery, keeper restart, and health proof. Never copy or relink installed binaries around that transaction.\n* Keep one reliable published store. A first local install clones it; compatible local installs reuse the dev Home; explicit fresh archives the old dev Home and reclones reliable data. Development binaries never receive reliable-store authority.\n* Treat an exact complete artifact, app/helper, selected-store, migration-frontier, and settled-receipt match as a true no-op before any upgrade receipt, generation change, pause, signal, or reconciliation. Print already installed; fleet untouched.\n* Before a real transition, settle absent owners only from exact Run-event and process-receipt evidence, then classify every live Invocation. Direct provider sessions may stop only with a proved native resume route. Same-lineage headless Work may stop only with a proved lifecycle relaunch. Captured first-fork Work may instead use the confirmed published-return or fresh parking disposition. Any other live or unprovable body defers the entire transition before pause or signal.\n* Preserve typed HomeUpgrade causality. Same-lineage Work relaunches with RunTrigger HomeUpgrade and no retry lineage; confirmed rewind Work stays parked. Neither disposition creates a work-failure event or consumes Task or Project recovery.\n* Use existing provider-session artifacts and command builders to reopen eligible conversations. Link old and replacement Invocations, preserve conversation evidence, and add no second inventory or handoff format.\n* Record exact artifact, store, recovery, first-fork Work, and safe-stop disposition in machine-level promotion authority. Retain the complete published artifact set and reliable store as failback.\n* A published return selects and migrates only the reliable store, parks surviving forked Work before reliable reconciliation, archives the dev Home for inspection, and never imports dev schema or rows.\n\nProof\n\nOne staged integration history proves: an exact reinstall leaves fleet identity untouched; local promotion clones but never mutates the reliable store; a live TUI or ambiguous owner defers promotion without receiving a signal; direct Claude and Codex conversations reopen from existing session artifacts; a headless Task relaunches across same-lineage local upgrades without spending recovery and is durably parked on published return; the 2026-08-19 stale Invocation shape settles exactly once with evidence preserved; keeper and app surfaces retain one artifact/store identity; failure injection leaves valid receipt-owned recovery; and the next published release migrates the reliable store once with no dev-data import.\n\nThis Task absorbs LOO-228 and its 2026-08-19 evidence. Implement one promotion mechanism and preserve the combined acceptance cases in scratch/install-unpublished-local-builds-through.md.\n\n<!-- loopflow-task-start:c4baea4f50bb8eb893840d28ef77483ed740993c3625514fce864cb85b1336a1 -->","rank":13,"completed":true,"project_id":"03c59a52-dbd4-4e11-a6ac-f6d6359c8e08","project":"reliability","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"5f974026-f793-47c1-b499-89f68517a445","identifier":"LOO-224","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-224/wave-resident-replays-stale-playhead-step-names-after-a-skill-rename","name":"Wave resident replays stale playhead step names after a skill rename, wedging the loop","description":"Wave resident replays stale playhead step names after a skill rename, wedging the loop\n\nThe product wave (repo /Users/jack/src/loopflow) is stuck loop:failed with '3 consecutive wave failures: failed to prepare wave / wave_mutate: skill not found: wave_mutate'. The catalog registers the skill as wave/mutate (lf list shows it; builtin flow wave.yaml lists wave/clarify -> wave/pursue -> wave/mutate), but the wave's journal playhead pins the pre-rename step name wave_mutate — 5,744 entries in .lf/journal/waves/product/journal.jsonl, replayed across every resident restart including two home upgrades today (2026-08-19). The resident resumes from the persisted playhead instead of re-expanding the flow against the current catalog, so it can never heal. finish_failed_pass fires from run_harness_pass (flowloop/wave.rs:852) when prepare() cannot resolve the stale name.\n\nTask-level flow pins got exactly this repair in #1151 (task runtime: repair stale flow pins and bound recovery); the wave resident needs the same: on prepare failure for a persisted step name, re-expand the flow from the catalog and reconcile the playhead (wave_mutate -> wave/mutate is a rename, index 2 of 3 is unchanged) instead of counting toward consecutive-failure shutdown. Blast radius today: product has 4,317 replays and is wedged; engbot/list have 3 stale refs each; infrastructure 129; all currently idle but will wedge if they resume into a stale pin.\n\nDone when: a wave whose journal pins a renamed step name recovers on the next pass without manual journal surgery (prove with a unit test around the playhead reconciliation, plus the live product wave leaving loop:failed after deploy); consecutive-failure counting distinguishes unresolvable-step (repairable) from body failures.\n\n<!-- loopflow-task-start:a7f376582cd1bb7bd0b467dcae85e593f9d5139e995e5cfa7bf715287e37d468 -->","rank":14,"completed":true,"project_id":"03c59a52-dbd4-4e11-a6ac-f6d6359c8e08","project":"reliability","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"17edc7a0-5e01-444a-8c6b-ccaf2ead7250","identifier":"LOO-228","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-228/absorbed-home-upgrades-must-not-destroy-work-they-cannot-resume","name":"[absorbed] Home upgrades must not destroy work they cannot resume","description":"Absorbed into LOO-226. The survivor now contains the committed 2026-08-19 incident evidence, exact reinstall no-op, fail-closed pre-signal liveness classification, typed HomeUpgrade causality outside the Work recovery budget, exact dead-owner settlement, provider-session continuity, and the combined local-promotion proof. The older leave-unresumable-processes-behind proposal is resolved to deferral before pause or signal. Do not implement this Task independently.","rank":15,"completed":true,"project_id":"03c59a52-dbd4-4e11-a6ac-f6d6359c8e08","project":"reliability","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"cf5f19cd-731b-4f5d-81c4-989eaaa00a20","identifier":"LOO-229","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-229/status-surfaces-must-distinguish-last-recorded-event-from-current","name":"Status surfaces must distinguish last recorded event from current truth","description":"Status surfaces must distinguish last recorded event from current truth\n\nEvidence from 2026-08-19, three instances of one defect: (1) lf status showed 'project runner failed: no eligible managed codex account ... credential is missing' for every infrastructure project all day, replaying terminal project_events from Jul 22 / Aug 14 — the account store had been healthy since Aug 16 and concurrent runners were selecting accounts fine; (2) wave headers showed loop:failed replayed from journals across restarts even while every project and task under the wave ran normally; (3) agent_invocations kept invocation_74115449 as outcome='running' for hours after its process died in an upgrade, and lf invocation status could not resolve the id it was still reporting as live.\n\nScope: every read surface (lf status, lf ls, invocation list, Mac app equivalents) must either verify a claimed-live fact against liveness evidence or label it as historical ('last failure at <time>', not 'failed'). Startup/reconcile should settle records whose backing process provably did not return, in the spirit of #1151 and LOO-224 but as a general rule for run/invocation records rather than a per-noun patch. Stale-state incidents today all shared the shape: durable record outlives reality and is replayed as present tense.\n\nDone when: a terminal failure record older than the newest successful run of the same work renders as history, not as the current state (fixture-level proof plus one live lf status demo); an invocation whose process is gone is settled by reconcile within one startup cycle; the Jul-22 credential ghost cannot recur — reproduce it in a test store and show the new rendering.\n\n<!-- loopflow-task-start:39d177a93bf486c5cbd6820097b6836af50dd4f75a9355c34ffe4dc0dd381fbe -->\n\n## Ownership boundary — 2026-08-20\n\nLOO-240 owns degradation when one persisted Task row cannot decode, including the exact `merge request requires reviewer-facing PR copy` fixture. This Task retains the broader contract: status surfaces must distinguish historical records from current liveness and reconcile exact dead owners. Depend on LOO-240 for mixed-version row availability; do not implement a second partial-reader path here.","rank":16,"completed":true,"project_id":"03c59a52-dbd4-4e11-a6ac-f6d6359c8e08","project":"reliability","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"db77bbf0-2ea0-4c47-9512-3fda3a963f34","identifier":"LOO-227","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-227/let-project-runs-resume-child-tasks-with-durable-control-authority","name":"Let Project runs resume child Tasks with durable control authority","description":"Observed 2026-08-19 in Stability & Security iteration 36: the authoritative roadmap recommended resuming parked LOO-224, but `lf task resume LOO-224 --json` failed closed before mutation with `invalid control authority: Run run_8ee1a67cc30a4bc9b70905b92cb0d68d has no durable Turn Basis for child control`. LOO-224 retained its worktree, passing PR #1202, and unresolved wait; no duplicate Run was created. User outcome: every Project phase permitted to supervise child Tasks carries durable, exact control authority across phase transitions and provider/process recovery, so a recommended resume cannot strand the Task. Done when a real Project/pursue Run can resume an existing parked child Task; the authority is bound to the exact Project Work and Turn Basis; a retry is idempotent; an unrelated Project or stale Turn remains denied; and a missing basis surfaces as actionable Project failure before pursuit rather than leaving the child parked. Add a deterministic behavior fixture for phase transition plus process-loss recovery. Do not weaken task authority or bypass the existing resume path.","rank":17,"completed":true,"project_id":"03c59a52-dbd4-4e11-a6ac-f6d6359c8e08","project":"reliability","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"aad2d373-8f99-41e5-9ddf-79996266fceb","identifier":"LOO-219","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-219/keep-daily-telemetry-actionable-after-historical-capture-loss","name":"Keep daily telemetry actionable after historical capture loss","description":"Observed 2026-07-21: the due telemetry-daily flow failed because lf doctor reports 20,703 partial capture records. Representative provider and conversation traces record No space left on device on 2026-07-14 and 2026-07-15. Current evidence contradicts an active capacity outage: the Home volume has 169 GiB available, .lf/traces uses 11 GiB, and .lf uses 23 GiB. Outcome: preserve historical partial-capture facts while letting telemetry distinguish recovered current health from new active loss. Done when historical partials remain visible and immutable; a healthy post-recovery window can pass; any new capture loss fails immediately with time, owner, and storage context; text and JSON doctor output agree; deterministic fixtures cover historical loss plus current health and a newly recurring failure. Do not delete or rewrite trace history, add an unbounded ignore list, or weaken release verification.","rank":18,"completed":true,"project_id":"03c59a52-dbd4-4e11-a6ac-f6d6359c8e08","project":"reliability","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"776e020e-c2e6-4b3c-b320-802494c59ce1","identifier":"LOO-17","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-17/subsumed-by-loo-102-make-the-development-mac-app-use-the-shared","name":"[subsumed by LOO-102] Make the development Mac app use the shared registry safely","description":"scripts/loopflow-dev.py run and run-debug currently bundle a development-provenance lf, which defaults to an empty per-worktree database. The app launches successfully but cannot see the real Waves, Tasks, or interactive handoffs it is supposed to demo. Build the bundled demo CLI from the current checkout with release store selection and validation-only migration authority, so it reads the shared registry without gaining permission to migrate it. Prove the installed Dev app bundled lf ls --json matches the installed CLI Wave identities and that run-debug still refuses any required production migration.","rank":19,"completed":true,"project_id":"03c59a52-dbd4-4e11-a6ac-f6d6359c8e08","project":"reliability","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"c794339e-4e49-48d1-ab82-7322d78c9f6d","identifier":"LOO-214","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-214/bootstrap-the-infrastructure-cron-host-and-its-durable-receipts","name":"Bootstrap the infrastructure cron host and its durable receipts","description":"Install the infrastructure Wave's declared telemetry-daily and release-run jobs on its placed Home using the supported lf cron path. Preflight host-local/Doppler authority without exposing secrets. Prove lf cron list matches GOAL.md, each flow can run through the configured path, and scheduled success/failure leaves durable, actionable evidence sufficient to start the nightly/weekly and host-drift observation windows. Do not build a generic deployment platform or alter completed Task work.","rank":20,"completed":true,"project_id":"03c59a52-dbd4-4e11-a6ac-f6d6359c8e08","project":"reliability","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"ae4775c7-a2a6-4a91-a3f3-65153dae363a","identifier":"LOO-29","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-29/subsumed-by-loo-55-report-the-authoritative-pr-base-without-false","name":"[subsumed by LOO-55] Report the authoritative PR base without false contamination","description":"Found by W2-306 while diagnosing the re-mint base incoherence, and correctly left out of its scope. Filed by the Project because a Task session does not file its own follow-ups.\n\n## The defect\n\nops/task.rs:1497-1511 classifies merge_base < base as:\n\n'PR range is contaminated: recorded base carries commit(s) not on origin/main'\n\nThat is the #877/#882 foreign-ancestry case. It is FALSE for the mint bug. W2-300's row hits this arm — is_ancestor(9f6bdd498, 3e9df0677) is true — yet base 3e9df0677 IS origin/main's tip and carries nothing foreign. The message accuses the base of exactly the property it does not have, which sends a reader hunting foreign ancestry that is not there.\n\nThe arm conflates two distinct causes:\n\n* (a) base is ON the upstream but AHEAD of the branch's fork point — the mint bug W2-306 fixes;\n* (b) base is OFF the upstream carrying foreign commits — genuine contamination (#877/#882).\n\nis_ancestor(base, upstream) separates them cleanly: true => (a), false => (b).\n\n## Why this survives W2-306\n\nW2-306 makes cause (a) unrepresentable going forward at the mint, and heals existing rows on adopt. So after it lands this message should become unreachable for (a) — but 'unreachable' is an assumption worth testing, not a reason to leave a lying diagnostic in the tree. Any future path that records a base ahead of the fork point lands here again and gets misdiagnosed.\n\n## Done when\n\n* The M<B arm distinguishes (a) from (b) via is_ancestor(base, upstream) and each names its real cause. A base that is the upstream's own tip must never be described as carrying commits not on the upstream.\n* A test pins each arm separately with a fixture that actually has the property the message asserts. A single test over one shape passes with the conflation fully present — this is the same trap that hid the mint bug (a first-mint-only test is green while the defect is live).\n* Do NOT change the fail-closed behaviour; only the classification and the text. Both arms still refuse.\n\n## Context\n\nDepends on nothing; W2-306 (#1059) may land first and is expected to. Read W2-306's design (scratch/a-re-minted-successor-pairs.md) for the base/merge_base model before touching this: base_commit == merge_base(upstream, branch) is the invariant the tree already states at ops/task.rs:1492 and 3455-3458.","rank":21,"completed":true,"project_id":"03c59a52-dbd4-4e11-a6ac-f6d6359c8e08","project":"reliability","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"9ddb3775-2ea0-40c1-b88c-c7ac623c6dd5","identifier":"LOO-32","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-32/subsumed-by-loo-37-bound-release-notes-and-release-queue-metadata","name":"[subsumed by LOO-37] Bound release notes and release-queue metadata","description":"Observed while recovering the v0.11.3 release on 2026-07-15. The deterministic notes fallback generated a 140,834-byte RELEASE_NOTES.md by copying full historical PR bodies. `lf release run patch` then passed that entire document as the squash commit body, and GitHub rejected auto-merge at its 16,383-character commit-message limit.\\n\\nUser story\\n\\nAs the release operator, deterministic fallback notes stay reviewable and the release PR can always enter the merge queue regardless of historical PR-body size.\\n\\nDone when\\n\\n- The PR set is exactly the commits after the previous release tag; already-released history is excluded.\\n- Mechanical notes summarize or omit PR bodies under an explicit documented byte budget.\\n- PR presentation and squash commit metadata have separate bounded copy.\\n- A fixture with many very large PR bodies produces useful notes and successfully reaches the queue call.\\n- Truncation is visible and deterministic, never silent corruption.\\n\\nSource: release PR #987 and W2-257. Recovery manually reduced the v0.11.3 PR body from about 140 KB to 2.1 KB.","rank":22,"completed":true,"project_id":"03c59a52-dbd4-4e11-a6ac-f6d6359c8e08","project":"reliability","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"7572c986-583a-4876-878d-6c3d491980fb","identifier":"LOO-212","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-212/bootstrap-the-release-homes-doppler-authority","name":"Bootstrap the release Home's Doppler authority","description":"Restore patch-release readiness after the 2026-07-21 release-run failed before mutation. Evidence: doppler run -- uv run python scripts/publish_release.py check reported missing secret names CARGO_REGISTRY_TOKEN, FLY_API_TOKEN, NOTARY_ISSUER, NOTARY_KEY, NOTARY_KEY_ID, R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID, R2_ACCOUNT_ID, and R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY on Home home_39860354aaca640c2ccb50bf6ca609d8. Inspect Home placement and the configured Doppler project/config without printing values. If values require new human authority, create one durable Ask naming the exact destination and secret names. Otherwise repair configuration. Done when the same check exits zero. Do not publish a release as part of this Task.","rank":23,"completed":true,"project_id":"03c59a52-dbd4-4e11-a6ac-f6d6359c8e08","project":"reliability","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"7150136b-c614-4ea4-8a0d-f89d45a8ffd6","identifier":"LOO-37","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-37/make-releases-finish-predictably-when-automation-is-degraded","name":"Make releases finish predictably when automation is degraded","description":"Outcome: A release remains bounded, useful, and safely resumable when the notes agent is unavailable, rate-limited, unauthenticated, or otherwise degraded. Contract: mechanical release context and queue metadata have explicit size bounds; agent notes use bounded source material; every provider unavailability class selects the supported deterministic fallback; retries neither duplicate release state nor grow context without bound; status distinguishes degraded notes from an unsafe release gate. Proof: tests cover missing CLI, cooldown, quota, authentication failure, oversized PR bodies, interrupted retry, and a successful resumed release that produces concise notes and the exact intended artifact once.","rank":24,"completed":true,"project_id":"03c59a52-dbd4-4e11-a6ac-f6d6359c8e08","project":"reliability","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"998ea80e-790c-4039-b7b8-5555af56159c","identifier":"LOO-39","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-39/subsumed-by-loo-102-synchronize-wave-wakes-on-events","name":"[subsumed by LOO-102] Synchronize Wave wakes on events","description":"Observed in merge-group CI for migration PR #986 on 2026-07-15. The ordinary PR run and the full local `cargo test -p loopflow` passed, but merge-group rust-test failed `flowloop::wave::tests::say_wakes_the_loop_and_is_consumed_by_the_next_pass` after 3.137s with `condition not met in time: pass spawned`; 552 tests had passed and the remaining suite was canceled. The same test passed in the prior runs.\n\nUser-visible outcome\n\nWave runtime tests prove wake/consume ordering deterministically under loaded CI. They wait on an owned event or state transition, not a three-second scheduling race, and a real failure still reports which transition never occurred.\n\nProof\n\nReproduce the failure under parallel nextest load, replace wall-clock polling with deterministic synchronization at the runtime boundary, and run the test repeatedly plus the full nextest profile. Follow the repository rule: do not add retries or merely extend the timeout; delete a test if it cannot prove user behavior deterministically.","rank":25,"completed":true,"project_id":"03c59a52-dbd4-4e11-a6ac-f6d6359c8e08","project":"reliability","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"910993d2-0c1e-4b34-b8cd-2343d9109854","identifier":"LOO-55","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-55/let-a-task-ship-without-corrupting-neighboring-work-or-needing-manual","name":"Let a Task ship without corrupting neighboring work or needing manual repair","description":"Outcome: A Task can move from launch to merged PR without writing outside its assignment, leaving provider processes behind, falsely rejecting its own branch, or requiring a human to reconstruct CI state. Contract: one writer owns the assigned worktree and provider write tools cannot modify main or another Task even when those paths are readable; normal exit reaps provider descendants and lfd maintenance reaps only registry-proven orphans; PR range diagnostics use the authoritative upstream base and never call origin main foreign contamination; one bounded ci-fix turn owns repair of the authoritative failed head and settles truthfully. Proof: an end-to-end lifecycle covers worktree escape attempts, provider exit and forced orphan recovery, rebased publication, failed CI repair, and terminal settlement with zero manual Git or process cleanup.","rank":26,"completed":true,"project_id":"03c59a52-dbd4-4e11-a6ac-f6d6359c8e08","project":"reliability","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"5efbfd37-8d3a-417d-8e20-d8f2fc59e3df","identifier":"LOO-57","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-57/keep-wave-status-readable-when-a-historical-project-leaves-the-pm","name":"Keep Wave status readable when a historical Project leaves the PM snapshot","description":"Reproduced on 2026-07-15 after `lf pm show --wave infrastructure --sync` succeeded. Both `lf roadmap --wave infrastructure --json` and `lf status infrastructure --json` still fail because historical Project Session `release-stability` (6c053501-f727-42cf-a1a3-8fc788e82410) is present in the registry but absent from the current Linear PM snapshot. This was previously reported during the migration fire and remains red.\n\nUser-visible outcome\n\nWave status always renders current work even when the durable registry contains Sessions for a renamed, retired, archived, or otherwise absent Project. Historical work remains visible as historical/degraded evidence and cannot make the entire Wave map unavailable.\n\nProof\n\nCreate a registry with current Tasks plus a historical Project Session omitted from the PM snapshot. `lf status` and `lf roadmap` must render current projects, classify the orphaned historical session explicitly, and give an actionable reconciliation owner. Preserve history; do not delete the Session or synthesize a fake PM project.","rank":27,"completed":true,"project_id":"03c59a52-dbd4-4e11-a6ac-f6d6359c8e08","project":"reliability","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"9e3c71ba-78aa-4da2-986a-bef5c0130409","identifier":"LOO-62","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-62/subsumed-by-loo-55-reap-provider-subprocesses-when-a-body-exits","name":"[subsumed by LOO-55] Reap provider subprocesses when a body exits","description":"Observed on 2026-07-15 after W2-225 generation 10 failed because its historical lf binary disappeared. The lf Task body and tmux session exited, but an orphaned `opencode serve` process remained under PPID 1 in the Task worktree for more than 90 minutes. `lf top` still reported it even though `lf task status W2-225` correctly said no process was alive.\n\nUser-visible outcome\n\nWhen a Project or Task body completes, fails, is interrupted, or disappears, every provider subprocess it owns is terminated within a bounded grace period. A provider must not outlive its durable body lease, retain ports, or make a dead worktree look occupied. Cleanup must target the owned process group and never kill unrelated provider sessions.\n\nProof\n\nCover normal completion, wrapper failure before terminal outcome, interrupt, and lost-body reconciliation. Start a provider child, terminate the body, and prove the child/process group is reaped while an unrelated provider stays alive. `lf top` and Task status must agree after cleanup.","rank":28,"completed":true,"project_id":"03c59a52-dbd4-4e11-a6ac-f6d6359c8e08","project":"reliability","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"fe0b2e61-7a11-4ddc-a627-bbed371bc921","identifier":"LOO-102","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-102/make-every-wave-start-and-wake-reliably-from-supported-surfaces","name":"Make every Wave start and wake reliably from supported surfaces","description":"Outcome: A Wave starts from the CLI, development Mac app, or a fresh Home and becomes responsive without registry confusion, partial registration, or polling-dependent luck. Contract: first start either publishes a live lfd endpoint or rolls back and names the actionable failure; the Mac app and CLI use the intended shared registry without replacing a promoted binary unsafely; wake behavior synchronizes on durable events rather than wall-clock sleeps; one Wave startup failure does not kill unrelated Home work. Proof: end-to-end tests cover fresh Home, installed lfd, development app, already-registered Wave, concurrent wake, and failed preflight, and every path reaches a truthful live or failed state within a bounded interval.","rank":29,"completed":true,"project_id":"03c59a52-dbd4-4e11-a6ac-f6d6359c8e08","project":"reliability","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"3cc1493f-a29f-4f9d-80b8-fb378340ba9d","identifier":"LOO-104","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-104/subsumed-by-loo-55-reap-registry-proven-orphan-providers-from-lfd","name":"[subsumed by LOO-55] Reap registry-proven orphan providers from lfd maintenance","description":"Extend lfd's startup and fifteen-minute Home maintenance sweep to terminate provider process groups whose exact Loopflow owner registry entry points to an absent owner. Send TERM, wait a bounded grace, then KILL the verified group and persist a reap receipt. Never terminate stalled, unobservable, PID-reused, or merely unclaimed processes. This complements the existing exit-time provider-subprocess cleanup task: exit cleanup is the normal path; lfd is the crash/reboot recovery path. Done when a registered orphan disappears without a Wave restart, a live owner vetoes reaping, repeated sweeps are idempotent, and lf top can explain the recent cleanup from its receipt.","rank":30,"completed":true,"project_id":"03c59a52-dbd4-4e11-a6ac-f6d6359c8e08","project":"reliability","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"761827cc-cee7-4a77-9e93-3bfef6825535","identifier":"LOO-105","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-105/subsumed-by-loo-55-prove-deterministic-ci-fix-recovery-on-the-run","name":"[subsumed by LOO-55] Prove deterministic ci-fix recovery on the Run spine","description":"## Context\n\nPRD-8 (product wave, \"Wake a waiting Task into ci-fix when its PR fails\") depends on four Infrastructure substrate tasks whose merged evidence was meant to satisfy its done-whens. All four are marked done in Linear:\n\n* ENG-88 / W2-229 — Prove failed-PR ci-fix recovery as one deterministic lifecycle (#1009)\n* ENG-20 / W2-230 — Route automatic ci-fix wakes through the durable Task command ledger (#1024)\n* ENG-87 / W2-231 — Make ci-fix infrastructure failures block the Task actionably (#1001)\n* ENG-19 / W2-232 — Settle one bounded ci-fix turn without entering the generic Task gate (#1063)\n\n## The gap (found 2026-07-18 while auditing PRD-8 directive v4)\n\nThe architecture refactor #1073 (\"establish the durable Run and Steer control spine\") swept away two of the four implementations AFTER they merged:\n\n* ENG-88's 2817-line deterministic lifecycle harness (`rust/loopflow/src/task/runner/ci_fix_lifecycle_tests.rs`) was **deleted** in #1073 and **not replaced**. Current ci-fix test coverage on main is only unit tests: `ci_fix_restart_bar_permits_only_a_failing_open_pr_wake` (1), `merge_gate*` (3), and `autonomy*` (4). No test exercises the full wake → fix → green → rearm path deterministically.\n* ENG-20's durable command ledger (`ChildCommand` aggregate + `queue_command`) was **deleted** in #1073 via migration `0.11.035_drop_child_commands.sql`. The wake is now recorded through the `ci_incidents` store (`claim_ci_incident`, `mark_ci_incident_repaired`, `mark_ci_incidents_blocked`) on the Run/Launch/Basis spine. Whether that satisfies the \"durable audited ci-fix command ledger\" done-when needs a deliberate decision, not a silent replacement.\n\nENG-87 (blocked semantics) and ENG-19 (bounded settlement) survive in refactored form (`settle_ci_fix_turn`, `mark_ci_incidents_blocked` in `rust/loopflow/src/task/runner.rs`).\n\n## What this task owns\n\nRe-establish the deterministic lifecycle proof on the current Run/Launch/Basis architecture so PRD-8's original done-when #1 holds again:\n\n> A deterministic integration harness opens a PR and proves: pending checks consume no body; one required failure starts exactly one ci-fix turn with canonical metadata; duplicate and multi-check deliveries coalesce; the fix pushes the same branch/PR and rearms observation for the new SHA; green CI does not wake a body; and an infrastructure failure produces an actionable blocker without code churn.\n\nAnd confirm whether the `ci_incidents` store on the Run spine satisfies ENG-20's \"durable audited command ledger\" intent, or whether a dedicated audit record is still warranted.\n\n## What this task does NOT own\n\nThe ci-fix wake mechanism itself (PR 967, product) and the blocked-semantics / bounded-settlement behavior (ENG-87, ENG-19). This is proof-and-audit restoration only, on the architecture that replaced the originals.\n\n## Why PRD-8 stays open\n\nPRD-8 directive v4 says: keep PRD-8 open and visibly dependent on the Infrastructure tasks until their merged evidence satisfies the original done-whens. The merged evidence no longer satisfies them because #1073 removed the proof. PRD-8 cannot close until this task (or equivalent proof) lands.","rank":31,"completed":true,"project_id":"03c59a52-dbd4-4e11-a6ac-f6d6359c8e08","project":"reliability","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"d4af661c-669f-4fc7-88d9-dea5005f275f","identifier":"LOO-270","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-270/make-architecture-drift-evidence-consistent-across-local-and-hosted","name":"Make architecture drift evidence consistent across local and hosted trees","description":"Outcome: scripts/check_architecture.py judges the reviewed repository identically in a clean hosted checkout and a normal Loopflow checkout containing ignored runtime archives. Current counterexample on 2026-08-23: all 167 discovered boundaries map, but the local check fails only on stale vocabulary inside ignored .lf/prompts and .lf/tmp artifacts that hosted CI never sees. Contract: exclude non-reviewed generated/runtime material by one principled source-of-truth boundary, not a growing stale-term or path allowlist; preserve coverage of reviewed .lf configuration and every existing architecture category. Proof: focused tests create ignored runtime artifacts containing retired vocabulary and remain green, while equivalent tracked/reviewed vocabulary still fails; the real local check and hosted workflow use the same behavior.","rank":0,"completed":true,"project_id":"62b73cde-5057-4959-8ad8-fca96b9e80b5","project":"technical-architecture","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"15ea26b7-0b66-4c79-94c1-a846bef80b1a","identifier":"LOO-253","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-253/main-agents-move-off-canonical-main-into-a-worktree","name":"Main agents move off canonical main into a worktree","description":"PRINCIPLE: a main agent (one that runs on the canonical checkout, not a Task body) must not do work directly on main — it moves into a worktree. Two subjects, shipped in ONE diff sharing a worktree-move core:\n\nSUBJECT 1 — the WAVE agent (resident). Runs the wave loop with cwd = canonical main, so memory curation commits onto local main, drifting it ahead of origin/main and jamming Task placement. Fix: run the resident from a dedicated worktree; commit curation there; reach main via a CI-gated PR; canonical becomes read-only to the resident. This half is fully parent-reviewed — see scratch/main-agent-in-worktree.md on branch jack-heart/main-agent-in-worktree. (Project agent is a likely third subject; keep the core general enough to cover it.)\n\nSUBJECT 2 — default `lf`. The user's main/only agent interface when they run bare `lf` in a repo. Meet them in whatever context (plain repo, on main, in a worktree) and don't refuse; as soon as real work happens on main, transparently MOVE to a worktree and carry the in-flight work along. Scoped to bare/default `lf` — `lf code` and other specialized subcommands keep their assumptions and do NOT get the auto-move.\n\nNOT the fix: branch-identity verification, ownership-collision guards, wt-remove warnings — that hardens the model in the wrong direction. The move is accept-the-context, then relocate off main.\n\nAbsorbs cd07e2e2 (the default-lf framing).","rank":1,"completed":true,"project_id":"62b73cde-5057-4959-8ad8-fca96b9e80b5","project":"technical-architecture","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"cd07e2e2-65c0-43f3-ae59-859c634e7859","identifier":"LOO-254","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-254/absorbed-into-15ea26b7-default-lf-accept-context-move-to-worktree","name":"[absorbed into 15ea26b7] Default lf: accept context, move to worktree","description":"Vision: a new user runs `lf` inside their repo and that IS their main/only agent interface. That agent may itself choose to run a Wave or Project agent directly. Default lf should meet the user in whatever context it finds — plain repo, sitting on main, or already in a worktree — and NOT refuse.\n\nLoosening intake is coupled with absorbing the user into the workflows that work best. Keystone behavior: as soon as possible, notice we are doing real work directly on the canonical/main checkout and transparently MOVE to a worktree — create or switch into one and carry the in-flight work along. Permissive intake + automatic promotion into the good pattern, rather than (a) rejecting the context or (b) letting work dirty main.\n\nSCOPE — this belongs to bare/default `lf` specifically (the empty invocation that is the user's main agent door). Specialized subcommands do NOT get the auto-move: `lf code` and friends can keep assuming they're in a worktree and just work outside one too. Don't make this a global CLI policy; it's the behavior of the default entrypoint.\n\nThis unifies two problems that are the same problem:\n\n* The wave resident dirtying canonical main (task 15ea26b7) is a special case of 'work is happening on main; move to a worktree.'\n* A user/agent running default lf on main and starting real work should be lifted into a worktree early and quietly.\n\nToday the machinery hardens against these contexts instead:\n\n* 'Wave and Project turns require the canonical main checkout' — refuses.\n* `lf task run` refuses a pre-existing un-owned worktree/branch — refuses.\n  Both are the wrong reflex: refuse-the-context instead of accept-then-move.\n\nDo NOT fix this by adding branch-identity verification, ownership guards, or wt-remove warnings — that hardens the model in the wrong direction.\n\nExplore, don't over-specify: the concrete surface — what counts as 'real work on main', when exactly to move, how the move carries uncommitted/committed state, how the default agent decides to escalate — is for design to work out.","rank":2,"completed":true,"project_id":"62b73cde-5057-4959-8ad8-fca96b9e80b5","project":"technical-architecture","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"28836de1-0ac0-46da-981e-5961efe225ed","identifier":"LOO-73","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-73/make-project-planning-changes-take-effect-without-restarting-or","name":"Make Project planning changes take effect without restarting or stranding work","description":"Outcome: A human can change a Project definition or KRs in Linear and active Project Work adopts the authoritative plan at a safe boundary without losing direction, observations, or identity. If adoption is unsafe, status says exactly why a restart is required; it never continues silently on stale planning. Contract: the Project seed carries the complete PM definition and KR set, including more than four KRs; one authoritative representation owns the plan; refresh occurs at a bounded phase boundary; removal or archival produces a truthful state rather than a crash or stale loop. Proof: tests start Project Work with five or more KRs, edit the definition and KR set while it is active, and show the next bounded turn receives the exact new plan once while prior direction and evidence remain intact.","rank":3,"completed":true,"project_id":"62b73cde-5057-4959-8ad8-fca96b9e80b5","project":"technical-architecture","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"0194b0da-1465-471a-9fed-a0c470f89851","identifier":"LOO-208","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-208/make-loopflow-architecture-explainable-from-one-map","name":"Make Loopflow architecture explainable from one map","description":"Outcome: A maintainer can start at one top-down map and understand the durable control owners, truth sources, process boundaries, public APIs, and provider edges that explain Loopflow. The implementation and vocabulary follow that map instead of preserving alternate historical models. Contract: name each core real-world concept once; link it to its authoritative data structure and public surface; identify persistence and process ownership; name allowed historical-only vocabulary; add a bounded drift check that finds unnamed owners, mirrors, shims, and stale pre-Run, pre-Home, or pre-native-PM language. Proof: every landed change for four consecutive weekly checks maps to an existing concept or updates the map in the same change, and each check reports zero unexplained owner, mirror, or shim.","rank":4,"completed":true,"project_id":"62b73cde-5057-4959-8ad8-fca96b9e80b5","project":"technical-architecture","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"52beae61-e799-455c-b8dc-25548d259457","identifier":"LOO-127","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-127/let-every-repository-own-and-safely-move-its-waves","name":"Let every repository own and safely move its Waves","description":"Outcome: Independent repositories can use the same Wave names without cross-repository attachment, and an existing Wave can be renamed or rehomed without losing its identity or history. Contract: Wave resolution is scoped by canonical repository plus slug; ambiguous bare-name lookup fails closed; rename and rehome preserve the Wave UUID, PM binding, Work and Run history, journal, and authored paths; stale repository paths are repaired transactionally. Proof: deterministic tests register, launch, query, and display an Infrastructure Wave in two repositories, exercise rename and rehome, and prove neither repository can resolve or mutate the other Wave.","rank":5,"completed":true,"project_id":"62b73cde-5057-4959-8ad8-fca96b9e80b5","project":"technical-architecture","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"ad59fb8f-837d-4763-8582-213b8e4a7bf8","identifier":"LOO-6","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-6/measure-the-local-pre-land-verification-path-against-its-budgets-on","name":"Measure the local pre-land verification path against its budgets on every landing","description":"## Problem\n\nKR4 requires that 'the full local pre-land verification path has **measured** budgets and stays inside them for a month of landings.' It is the only KR in this Project with **no work behind it** — verified against all 29 open tasks on 2026-07-16.\n\n`54f17e72` ('Make the full local release gate trustworthy and bounded', done) established that the gate is bounded. What is missing is the other half: nothing **measures** an actual landing's verification duration against those bounds. Without that, the KR cannot be evidenced — not 'is unmet', but *unmeasurable*, which is worse. A month of landings could pass inside budget and nobody could say so.\n\n## Approach hint, not a design\n\nThe dogfood idiom already exists: `lf rebase` writes local-only ops telemetry (strategy/class/counts, no diffs or secrets) and the wave reviews it weekly against named metrics (agent-rebase rate, avoidable rebase-agent rate, median land->merged). Pre-land verification wants the same shape — a duration per named stage against a named budget — rather than a new subsystem.\n\nTwo traps to check before relying on that path:\n\n* The telemetry path **moved**: W2-233 (PR #982, merged) routed ops metrics from tracked `.lf/metrics/ops.jsonl` to git-ignored `.lf/tmp/metrics/ops.jsonl` through a single `ops_metrics_path()`. Use the helper; do not hardcode.\n* `.lf/tmp` is **reapable, and got reaped**: W2-271 was filed because loopflow's own `/tmp` trees filled this host to 100% (117Mi free of 926Gi). A metric that lives only under a path the OS or a cleanup can delete cannot carry a month-long endurance claim. Decide explicitly where a month of evidence lives.\n\n## Done when\n\n* Every local pre-land verification run records its duration per stage against a **named budget** — not a wall-clock number a human has to interpret.\n* A landing that exceeds budget is visible without anyone timing it by hand.\n* A month of landings can be read off to judge KR4 — the evidence survives `/tmp` reaping and a machine restart.\n* The budgets themselves are stated somewhere a reader can find, not implicit in a passing test.\n\n## KR\n\nThis is the sole work behind 'The full local pre-land verification path has measured budgets and stays inside them for a month of landings.' Note the KR's clock **cannot start** until this lands: there is no month of evidence to accumulate while nothing measures.\n\n## Sequencing\n\nFiled, not started. As of 2026-07-16 the Project holds 10 running Sessions, 4 waiting, and 14 open PRs against 20 completed — the constraint is review/merge throughput, not task supply. Starting an 11th body adds to the queue rather than the rate. Ready to pick up when the merge queue drains.","rank":0,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"e266a7e4-9130-4845-b820-21c79ad47420","identifier":"LOO-7","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-7/settle-ci-fix-against-the-authoritative-repaired-head","name":"Settle CI-fix against the authoritative repaired head","description":"## Outcome\n\nOne failed PR head wakes at most one bounded repair body. After that body pushes, settlement judges the authoritative remote head, or a PR observation provably newer than the repair turn, before declaring that the body did not repair anything.\n\n## Live evidence: W2-311 / PR #1065\n\nOn 2026-07-17, system command cc_25913faa6aa94ba29a664c5588553cc2 woke Codex generation 3 for failed head 709046f64477aebff648211daf2957e0455e993d. The autonomous body diagnosed the Rust stack overflow and pushed 02527e291885ad6305921bff54c23dc69065502b. Every substantive hosted check passed on the new head; only structural scratch-clear and its tests-result roll-up remained red.\n\nSettlement still used the pre-turn PR observation at 709046f. It marked the original wake failed, blocked the incident, and proposed that the body did not repair the head even though GitHub and the Task PR publication both held 02527e291. This is a settlement observation race, not an agent repair failure. Preserve system command cc_25913faa6aa94ba29a664c5588553cc2 and generation 3 as the autonomous repair actors; generation 4 only reduced and reviewed W2-311.\n\nENG-19 shipped its prior bounded CI-fix work in PR #1018 and its Loopflow Task Session is terminal completed. Linear later regressed ENG-19 to incomplete when this new evidence was added. Do not reopen, resume, or add work to ENG-19; this fresh Task owns only the settlement race.\n\n## Done when\n\n* After a CI-fix body returns, settlement either reads the authoritative remote PR head directly or waits for a PR observation known to be newer than the repair turn before evaluating head advancement.\n* If the remote head differs from the incident head, settlement records the response against that new head, rearms hosted CI observation, and never blocks the old incident as unrepaired.\n* Only an authoritative read proving the remote head is still the original failed head may produce the did-not-repair outcome.\n* Duplicate, stale, concurrent, and restart deliveries for one failed head still launch at most one repair body.\n* Durable evidence ties the incident, system command, generation, old head, and repaired head together so attribution remains automatic.\n* A deterministic regression holds a stale pre-turn observation while the fake body pushes a new remote head, then proves no false block and no second body.\n\n## Boundary\n\nKeep the fix at CI-fix settlement: authoritative remote head or a provably newer observation. Do not add another PR observation cache or a generic gate body.\n\n## Sequencing\n\nStart now. W2-319 remains filed and starts only after this settlement fix settles.","rank":1,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"d6d9be55-3c1b-4fa4-961f-241d5ac55468","identifier":"LOO-8","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-8/complete-the-per-wave-linear-team-migration","name":"Complete the per-wave Linear team migration","description":"## Outcome\n\nInfrastructure, Intelligence, and Product each use their already-created Linear team as the only team for new Projects and Tasks. New identifiers carry the wave-owned prefixes ENG, SCI, and PRD instead of the shared W2 prefix.\n\nThe migration completes only when repo bindings, Linear Project ownership, open issue ownership, local Task Session identifiers, and PM diagnostics agree.\n\n## Root cause\n\nPer-wave team support and reteam mechanics shipped, and the three correct GOAL.md binding commits were authored on origin/jack-heart/ux, but those commits never merged to main. Main still binds all three waves to shared team 60558c53-2169-49f8-a76a-1f4586705aa9 (W2), so new work continues to receive W2 identifiers and lf pm reteam currently points in the wrong direction.\n\n## Source of truth\n\nWave GOAL.md frontmatter owns each stable Linear team id:\n\n* infrastructure -> 08c8d501-791d-4db0-a14c-15599d365955 (ENG)\n* intelligence -> 7de894bd-17da-403a-9d71-93c40afe9367 (SCI)\n* product -> e894ffa1-bc38-4382-af89-2e1d89884f4e (PRD)\n\nLinear owns Project teamIds and Issue team/identifier after apply. The local SQLite PM snapshot and Task Sessions derive from those provider records.\n\n## Work\n\nPR phase:\n\n* Change only the three wave GOAL.md team bindings to the existing wave-owned teams.\n* Preserve initiative ids and every other wave definition.\n* Verify PM parsing/tests and show that dry-run reteam targets ENG, SCI, and PRD rather than W2.\n* Do not copy unrelated origin/jack-heart/ux history; reproduce the three minimal binding changes on a fresh branch.\n\nApply phase after the binding PR is merged:\n\n* Run lf pm sync and lf pm doctor.\n* Dry-run each wave and inspect every move/defer/skip.\n* Apply Product and Intelligence when their protected-writer sets are empty.\n* Apply Infrastructure only after W2-319 and W2-320, plus this binding Task, are terminal so no body can write an identifier being renumbered.\n* Re-sync and prove each active Project belongs to exactly one wave team, every open issue is in its wave team, and doctor has no stranded-team diagnostic.\n* Preserve completed W2 issues as historical references. Do not rewrite shipped PRs or commits.\n\n## End-to-end proof\n\nCreate one fresh no-implementation probe Task per wave only if a non-mutating provider operation can prove the creation target; otherwise use the provider snapshot plus the next real Task creation. No disposable Linear issues solely for testing.\n\nThe final evidence must show:\n\n* Infrastructure creation resolves ENG.\n* Intelligence creation resolves SCI.\n* Product creation resolves PRD.\n* Existing open issues were renumbered with prior identifiers recorded by reteam.\n* Local Task Sessions reconcile to new display identifiers without losing PR/session history.\n* Re-running each reteam is a no-op.\n* lf pm doctor reports no Project or open issue stranded on W2.\n\n## Absent and error states\n\nMissing team binding, ambiguous team identity, a Project spanning teams, an issue with a live writing body, failed provider mutation, or stale post-apply snapshot fails closed. Never force-move beneath a live body and never edit Task Session identifiers manually.\n\n## Operational boundary\n\nThe binding PR may proceed while W2-319/W2-320 run. The provider apply is explicitly blocked behind their terminal settlement and any other protected writers reported by reteam. Use the supported lf pm reteam path only; no direct Linear GraphQL mutations.\n\n## Exclusions\n\nDo not rename historical completed W2 issues. Do not create new Linear teams. Do not change Initiative or Project definitions. Do not weaken the live-writer fence to finish faster.","rank":2,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"057c2efc-b389-4fd5-9c03-9e6659182b80","identifier":"LOO-9","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-9/keep-local-development-inside-a-predictable-resource-envelope","name":"Keep local development inside a predictable resource envelope","description":"Outcome: Dogfooding Loopflow cannot fill the disk, monopolize CPU, or make the local verifier unusable without an actionable warning and bounded recovery. Contract: name budgets for build artifacts, caches, disk growth, CPU, and verification; attribute growth to the owning source and clean disposable artifacts safely; distinguish product test failure from host security or verification pressure such as the observed syspolicyd burn; surface remediation before resource exhaustion rather than documenting manual cleanup as routine. Proof: repeated local builds and full verification stay inside the budgets, deliberate over-budget fixtures produce actionable diagnostics, and a constrained host recovers without deleting source, active worktrees, or durable state.","rank":3,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"a81327f3-d85f-4ad4-a683-16ec3d38e0df","identifier":"LOO-10","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-10/enforce-one-provider-turn-per-task-generation","name":"Enforce one provider turn per Task generation","description":"## Problem\n\nOne Task generation can run multiple provider turns concurrently in the same assigned worktree. This violates the one-writer-by-dispatch doctrine inside the runner itself: no external dispatch or worktree discipline can prevent two children of the same lf Task process from editing together.\n\n## Live evidence\n\nOn 2026-07-17, W2-308 generation 3 had two live Claude provider children under the same lf __task parent, started one second apart. One carried an interaction-review completion prompt; the other carried a task clarification seed. lsof confirmed both used the same Task worktree. After the shorter turn exited, the runner spawned another seed turn while the older review turn was still alive, so yielding burned another body instead of restoring single ownership. The overlap also made two Cargo invocations in that worktree contend on its private target lock.\n\n## Done when\n\n* A Task generation has at most one active provider turn for its assigned worktree.\n* A review completion, wake, seed, or follow-up arriving during an active turn is retained durably and delivered only after that turn owns a terminal boundary.\n* Completing a shorter sibling cannot launch another turn while an older sibling remains active.\n* A deterministic regression presents the review-plus-seed race, proves one provider process and one writer at every instant, then proves both instructions are delivered exactly once in order.\n* The fix lives at runner scheduling and ownership, not a filesystem lease or a force override.\n\n## KR\n\nAdvances zero avoidable repair and zero manual git surgery: concurrent writers can corrupt the worktree and make a healthy local build appear locked.","rank":4,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"753fbb15-e1f0-41eb-8cc0-731053881992","identifier":"LOO-11","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-11/authorize-global-lf-promotion-against-the-shared-migration-frontier","name":"Authorize global lf promotion against the shared migration frontier","description":"## Outcome\n\nBranch-local builds stay isolated by default. An explicit global promotion succeeds only through one authorized boundary that proves the candidate can safely operate on the shared Loopflow store and cannot strand live bodies.\n\nKeep `scripts/install.py` as the local build, bundle, and codesign entry point. Remove its authority to directly unlink, copy, or symlink the machine-global CLI or app helper. Python stages artifacts; Rust owns promotion policy because the Rust migration registry and shared Task/Project ledger are the sources of truth.\n\n## User-visible behavior\n\n`uv run python scripts/install.py local` builds into the worktree's `local-bin/` without changing the active command or app.\n\n`local --use` remains an explicit human-selected promotion capability, but it first previews and validates the candidate. The same gate applies to `refresh`, `refresh --no-pull`, `--install-dir`, the installed app, and its bundled `lf` helper. No supported path can replace a global consumer directly.\n\nThe preview names:\n\n* candidate worktree or release identity and exact stamped source revision\n* current CLI and app targets\n* candidate migration authority and latest known migration\n* shared store's applied migration frontier and lineage compatibility\n* pending, unknown, divergent, or checksum-mismatched migrations\n* live Loopflow Task and Project body count\n* rollback target retained if promotion succeeds\n\n## Promotion policy\n\n* Missing or unreadable evidence fails closed before any replacement.\n* A validation-only branch candidate may promote only when its migration history exactly recognizes the shared store and it has no pending production migration to apply.\n* An older candidate that does not know an applied migration, a divergent candidate, or any checksum mismatch is rejected with the exact database and candidate evidence.\n* A canonical main or tagged candidate with published migration authority may apply a compatible pending migration only through the existing backed-up migration path and only when no Loopflow bodies are live.\n* Any live Task or Project body makes a global replacement unsafe and is named in the refusal.\n* CLI, app, bundled helper, and skill sync publish as one accepted operation. The executable switch is atomic and the previous known-compatible binary remains the rollback candidate.\n* A failed promotion leaves every current target byte-for-byte or link-for-link unchanged.\n\n## Source of truth\n\nThe Rust migration registry, migration provenance stored in `schema_migrations`, build provenance/source revision, and the shared Task/Project ledger decide compatibility and liveness. The Python installer must not parse migration files, infer schema compatibility, or maintain a second registry.\n\nThe promotion boundary exposes a structured read-only preflight that the installer renders. Global mutation routes through that boundary rather than duplicating its checks in each Python command.\n\n## Affected surfaces\n\n* `scripts/install.py local --use`\n* `scripts/install.py refresh`\n* `refresh --no-pull` and custom `--install-dir`\n* `~/.local/bin/lf` / resolved active CLI target\n* `/Applications/Loopflow.app` and its bundled `lf`\n* post-install skill synchronization\n* migration authority and store-opening diagnostics\n* documentation describing local versus global builds\n\n## End-to-end proof\n\nA deterministic two-worktree regression creates:\n\n1. a shared store advanced by a newer published build,\n2. an older or divergent validation-only branch candidate,\n3. a compatible validation-only branch candidate at the exact frontier,\n4. a canonical published candidate with one pending migration,\n5. a live Task or Project body.\n\nThe test proves every global entry point rejects the incompatible candidate and live-body case before changing CLI or app targets; the exact-frontier branch candidate promotes atomically; the canonical candidate migrates only with no live bodies and retains a rollback binary. Sabotaging any supported path to call the old direct copy/symlink operation must make the regression fail.\n\n## Operational boundary\n\nUse one exclusive promotion lock across preflight and publication. Re-check store frontier and live-body state while holding the lock so the decision cannot race a migration or body launch. Do not stop live bodies as part of promotion.\n\n## Exclusions\n\nThis is supported-flow safety, not an OS security boundary against arbitrary same-user programs or stale pre-change scripts that deliberately unlink files in a user-writable bin directory. Do not add immutable filesystem flags or change permissions on the user's whole bin directory. The supported installer must have no direct bypass, and old worktrees should be directed to update before global promotion.\n\nDo not weaken, reorder, rename, or add compatibility shims around shipped migrations. Do not duplicate migration knowledge in Python.\n\n## Sequencing\n\nStart now by explicit human direction. Coordinate by file boundary with W2-320: this Task owns install/promotion and migration authorization surfaces; W2-320 owns CI-fix settlement.","rank":5,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"dc94fd8f-b93d-4fed-acf5-bd4d88adca2a","identifier":"LOO-12","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-12/an-evaporated-interrupt-parks-a-session-on-a-human-instruction-nobody","name":"An evaporated interrupt parks a Session on a human instruction nobody reads","description":"Split from W2-267 (GAP B), which deliberately excludes it. W2-267 recovers a Session whose durable status CLAIMS a body that does not exist. A Waiting Session claims no body, so it is out of that predicate by construction — but one Waiting shape is a strand wearing a different status.\n\n## The shape\n\nrecord_interrupt_of_inactive_process (ops/child.rs:364) parks a Session at Waiting with the durable reason:\n\n```\n'interrupted while no process was running; resume the Session to start one'\n```\n\nThat is a human instruction with no reader — the same family as ops/task.rs:1806 (fixed in W2-267) and 'manual cleanup is required' (filed as f39e37f0). In a headless wave with no human watching, nobody types it and the Session sits forever.\n\nLive instance: W2-230 (2026-07-16) — kickoff step 2, PR still 'working' (never opened), no live body, status=waiting on exactly this reason.\n\n## Why W2-267 did NOT simply auto-resume it\n\nAn Interrupt is an explicit command to STOP. Auto-resuming would overrule an explicit human command — a strictly worse failure than the strand it fixes.\n\nThe asymmetry with :1806 is what decides it, and it is NOT PR phase (which is lagging and mutable — see W2-267's W2-135 deduction — and must not be reintroduced as a discriminator):\n\n* :1806 shape — Active intent, no body. observe() reports BodyCategory::Stopped with owner BodyOwner::Loopflow and the reason 'a wake will adopt or start one'. The read model says Loopflow owns it while status_reason told a human to fix it. A contradiction, so recovery is right.\n* interrupt-Waiting shape — observe() reports NeedsInput with owner BodyOwner::Human, and a human genuinely just commanded a stop. No contradiction, so recovery is wrong.\n\n## The real question\n\nShould an interrupt that lands on an ALREADY-DEAD process park the Session at Waiting at all? The interrupt did not stop anything; it evaporated. Its docstring says the status exists so 'an operator cannot tell an interrupt that landed from one that evaporated' — that is a notification concern, and parking on an unread instruction is a costly way to notify.\n\nThis is interrupt semantics, not recovery dispatch. Deciding it needs an owner who can change what an evaporated interrupt means.\n\n## Done when\n\nA Session interrupted while no process was running either resolves without a human, or records a state whose owner is real. No durable field invites a resume that nobody will type.\n\n## Note\n\nW2-230 is currently blocked by BOTH this and f39e37f0 (its lease is stuck at 'revoked', so even a manual 'lf task resume' fails). Fixing this alone will not revive it.","rank":6,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"f39e37f0-5894-4f13-82b9-6649865d91ce","identifier":"LOO-13","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-13/release-a-body-lease-stuck-at-revoked-when-its-process-group-is-gone","name":"Release a body lease stuck at revoked when its process group is gone","description":"A failed reap deadlocks a Session's lease permanently, and no command can break it. Split from W2-267; this is reaping/classification territory (sibling of 'reap orphaned processes, classify children accurately' / b9843f04, and adjacent to #1000 'reap orphaned opencode process groups on resident boot'), not recovery-dispatch territory.\n\n## The deadlock\n\nreap_revoked_child_body (ops/child.rs:66-84) calls reap_child_process with '?'. When the kill fails (EPERM), it returns early and finish_revoked_task_process NEVER runs, so the lease stays 'revoked' forever.\n\nreserve_task_process (store/sqlite/child_sessions.rs:503-504) CASes on:\n\n```\nAND COALESCE(process_generation, 0) = ?16\nAND (process_lease_state IS NULL OR process_lease_state = 'finished')\n```\n\nA 'revoked' lease satisfies neither branch. The Session can never reserve another generation. Every 'lf task resume' fails; it is not a race, it is permanent.\n\n## Live instance (2026-07-16)\n\nW2-230: process_generation=2, process_lease_state='revoked', status=waiting, outcome=superseded ('body generation 2 stalled after 1804s without durable progress; recovering the same Task Session').\n\nstatus_reason: 'body generation 2 lease was revoked after a stall but its process group could not be reaped: failed to signal Group(27084): Operation not permitted (os error 1); manual cleanup is required' (written by ops/task.rs:2123).\n\nThe parent Project Session verified pid/process group 27084 does NOT exist — ps shows no members. The lease is pinned by a corpse, and the durable field sends a human chasing a ghost. 'manual cleanup is required' is a third human-instruction-with-no-reader, alongside ops/task.rs:1806 (fixed in W2-267) and ops/child.rs:365.\n\nScale: 1 of 117 Sessions. Lease states across the registry: active=41, finished=75, revoked=1. Rare, permanent, unaided.\n\n## Done when\n\nA lease stuck at 'revoked' whose process group is verifiably absent is released to 'finished' so the Session can reserve again, without a human. A group that genuinely still exists is not silently released.\n\n## Notes\n\n* W2-267 classifies this state honestly rather than fixing it: plan_stranded_recovery returns Surface with the true cause ('pinned by a lease stuck at revoked; its reap never completed... this needs the lease released, not a resume') instead of burning its 3-attempt budget on a CAS that cannot pass. It does NOT release the lease.\n* Consider also: the CAS miss is reported as 'task W2-230 changed from waiting to waiting during process reservation; retry the command' — the message blames status when the lease is what missed. Misleading; worth fixing alongside.\n* Consider whether an unreapable-but-already-dead group should block anything at all.","rank":7,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"7e7be305-83fb-4689-ac7e-c1260d962924","identifier":"LOO-14","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-14/recover-a-task-session-whose-parent-project-session-is-dead","name":"Recover a Task Session whose parent Project Session is dead","description":"Split from W2-267 (decision 6), which recovers a stranded Task body on the parent Project runner's 5s control tick. That removes the *flow-turn* dependency but not the *live-parent* dependency: recovery runs on the parent's tick, so a Task whose Project Session is itself dead is observed by nothing and still strands.\n\n## Why this is filed separately\n\nW2-267 deliberately refuses to reach for the wave tier. Reasons, which still hold:\n\n* The wave resident is the only self-reviving tier, so recovering from it crosses a tier boundary.\n* It would be a *second* re-dispatcher. Two competing relaunchers are worse than the gap.\n* Removing the flow-turn dependency is a real, shippable win on its own.\n\n## Evidence this may be the LARGER half\n\nCarried from the W2-267 review (parent Project Session, 2026-07-16), and the reason this task must be real rather than a scratch note:\n\nThe harm actually observed was Sessions frozen for HOURS. That is more consistent with the dead-parent case than the flow-turn case — a parent that is not running observes nothing, and this wave sits idle with no live listener between iterations. W2-267's fix may therefore address the smaller half of the problem.\n\nThe 2026-07-16 sweep found 13 Sessions stranded at 'failed' with frozen generations across 57 tasks (~23%). One manual resume revived 13/13, including a gen-9 Session.\n\n## Design constraints inherited from W2-267\n\n* Do NOT add a second dispatcher. If the wave tier drives this, it must route through the same classifier (plan_stranded_recovery) and the same relaunch path (relaunch_inactive_process via ChildSession::launch).\n* Recovery keys on durable status ('the Session's status claims a body that does not exist'), never on the 'lost' label alone and never on PR phase (which is lagging and mutable — see W2-267's W2-135 deduction).\n* Safety rests on the reserve_task_process fencing CAS (store/sqlite/child_sessions.rs:477-530), which CASes on generation + a 'finished' lease, so a losing dispatcher gets Ok(None).\n* The 'deliberately parent-driven: Sessions do not grow a second watchdog process' invariant (ops/task.rs:1810-1815) is the doctrine this task must either honour or explicitly overturn with a design.\n\n## Done when\n\nA Task Session whose parent Project Session is dead is recovered without a human, or the design records a reasoned decision that it should not be.","rank":8,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"b24f0e5e-aa61-42c9-9339-73d1437bf8e3","identifier":"LOO-15","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-15/reconcile-a-completed-linear-task-whose-session-still-needs-directive","name":"Reconcile a completed Linear Task whose Session still needs directive acknowledgement","description":"## Problem\n\nW2-308 shipped PR #1064 and Linear is complete, but its Task Session remains waiting because a later control interrupted generation 5 after directive v5 was applied and before its acknowledgement was recorded. The terminal recovery command cc_a172ed28a946463d893b84865d3fc292 remains Persisted with no generation.\n\nDeveloper Efficiency incorporated directive v22 and attempted recovery, then failed with: Task W2-308 is waiting; terminal Task Sessions cannot start a process. Direct completion refuses because directive v5 is unincorporated. The only acknowledgement surface refuses outside the owning Task Session. Current main retains both boundaries in ops/task.rs: terminal Sessions cannot start a process, while directive acknowledgement requires LF_TASK_SESSION_ID to match the owner. No actor can close the cycle.\n\n## Done when\n\n* A merged, Linear-complete Task can reconcile its Session after the final directive acknowledgement is interrupted, without impersonating the Task or starting another provider process.\n* Reconciliation neither creates another PR nor supersedes the existing terminal recovery with a duplicate command.\n* A regression covers: final directive applied, PR merged, Linear completed, body interrupted before acknowledgement, Project recovery attempted, then terminal Session settles truthfully.\n* W2-308 can reach durable terminal status and its recovery receipt no longer remains indefinitely Persisted.\n\n## KR\n\nAdvances the no-stranded-Task KR: this is a durable command orphaned against a dead generation with every supported owner barred from acting.","rank":9,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"910fdd72-b276-42fa-b1fd-384ca1d83fdb","identifier":"LOO-16","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-16/recover-a-stranded-task-session-without-human-intervention","name":"Recover a stranded Task Session without human intervention","description":"## Problem\n\nWhen a Task body dies, the Session goes `failed` and **stays there indefinitely**. Nothing observes it and nothing re-dispatches it. It waits for a human or parent Session to type `lf task resume`.\n\n## Evidence (developer-efficiency Project Session, 2026-07-16)\n\nA sweep of all 57 Project tasks found **13 Sessions stranded at** `failed` **with frozen generations** — no retry activity at all. Each needed exactly one manual resume; **13/13 revived**, including a gen-9 Session (W2-229). Sampling missed 7 of them, among them W2-230 (parked at a Human review gate) and W2-235. Nothing was defective — they just needed someone to ask.\n\n**Shipped fixes do not reach stranded Sessions.** W2-210 was pinned to an ephemeral `/tmp/loopflow-compat-<date>/target/debug/lf` that temp-reaping deleted. That defect was already fixed and merged (W2-225, resolve Session bodies through the current Home lf) — a fresh resume re-resolved it correctly. The Session had simply frozen at a pre-fix failure mode and stayed dead. The longer a Session strands, the more shipped fixes it silently misses.\n\n## Why existing tasks do not cover this\n\n* `b9843f04` (reap orphaned processes, classify children) and `9e3c71ba` (reap subprocesses on body exit) **classify and clean up**; neither re-dispatches.\n* `bd3d4f6d` / W2-249 resolves the current attempt **after** recovery — it presupposes recovery happened.\n* `73581397` / W2-230 and `7d540011` / W2-229 are **ci-fix**-specific: their trigger is a PR failing CI, not a body dying.\n\n## Triage the recovery must respect\n\nRecovery is not one action. `outcome.kind` splits it, and a Task already `completed` needs nothing:\n\n* `lost` **+ Task not terminal** = stranded -> resume. Reliable (13/13).\n* `lost` **+ Task** `completed` = a body reaped after success. **Not a failure** — W2-171 (#913), W2-226 (#965), W2-227, W2-233 (#982) all show `completed` with a `lost` body. Auto-recovery must not touch these. (This misclassification is `b9843f04`'s subject and is a hard dependency for measuring anything by `lost`.)\n* `failed` **+ terminal error** = read `status_reason` first; a blind resume re-fails. Revoked provider token (W2-235, codex) needs a **provider handoff** (`lf task resume --model`), not a resume.\n\n## Done when\n\n* A Session whose body dies is re-dispatched automatically, without a human or parent typing `lf task resume`.\n* Auto-recovery never touches a Session whose Task is already terminal (completed/abandoned).\n* A terminal `failed` outcome that a resume cannot fix (revoked credential, missing binary) is not retried blindly — it either routes to a healthy provider or surfaces as actionable, once.\n* Retry is bounded and observable: a Session that cannot recover stops and says why, rather than minting dead generations.\n* Proof: a killed body under a live Task returns to `running` with no human action, and a completed Task's reaped body triggers nothing.\n\n## KR\n\nAdvances 'Avoidable human-in-the-loop setup or repair steps found in agent runs fall to zero' and 'No Task strands on a dead body'.","rank":10,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"d7fed8dc-1df1-4d32-bab3-45574a3f87cc","identifier":"LOO-218","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-218/bring-agent-turn-context-inside-the-named-performance-budgets","name":"Bring agent Turn context inside the named performance budgets","description":"Observed by lf performance --json on 2026-07-21. Across 1,416 eligible Turns, measured total-input p95 is 5,414,085 tokens against a 5,000,000 budget. Claude measures p50 1,026,989 and p95 13,367,871 against 1,000,000/5,000,000; Claude output p95 is 48,853 against 20,000. Codex input p95 is 5,270,416 against 5,000,000. Missing receipts remain UNKNOWN and must not be rewritten as zero.\n\nOutcome: ordinary Task, Project, and Wave Turns stay within the named input/output budgets without dropping required direction, evidence, or durable authority.\n\nDone when:\n\n* Attribute over-budget Turns to concrete context contributors and lifecycle paths, preserving provider and Work kind.\n* Remove or summarize only redundant context at its source; do not weaken Steers, current Task evidence, or Wave identity.\n* A deterministic fixture proves repeated iterations do not resend unbounded historical material.\n* A fresh cohort of at least 20 measured Turns per changed path stays within the existing p50/p95 budgets.\n* Missing provider receipts remain UNKNOWN and conflicting receipts remain partial.","rank":11,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"f91b03dd-43a0-419a-af00-9bb5097fdb56","identifier":"LOO-216","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-216/make-every-performance-scorecard-denominator-authoritative","name":"Make every Performance scorecard denominator authoritative","description":"Observed by lf performance --json on 2026-07-21 after LOO-45 shipped. The 14-day scorecard cannot judge core Project KRs: task_first_progress_seconds has 0 eligible because Runs do not persist the first material provider event; land_to_merge_seconds has 0 eligible because Task PRs lack authoritative GitHub mergedAt; avoidable_repairs and manual_git_repairs have no durable landing or incident denominator.\n\nOutcome: lifecycle timing and zero-repair scorecard rows distinguish complete measured zero from missing authority and can accumulate the 7-day and 30-day Project evidence without manual reconstruction.\n\nDone when:\n\n* Every eligible Task persists its first material provider event with the owning Task and terminal-time window authority.\n* Every supported land persists request time and authoritative mergedAt without inferring from observer timestamps.\n* Avoidable repair and manual Git repair use typed durable incidents plus a complete eligible denominator; absent authority remains UNKNOWN.\n* Deterministic fixtures prove missing, measured zero, and real incident are distinct, restart-safe facts.\n* The affected lf performance --json rows report complete eligible/measured coverage on a constructed mixed-history store.\n* Preserve the existing performance model: first accepted evidence wins, conflicts become partial, and budgets judge evidence rather than rewriting correctness.","rank":12,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"cf326bc3-d69e-468f-a318-f82543eb02c4","identifier":"LOO-217","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-217/capture-complete-pre-land-stage-and-resource-evidence-on-every-landing","name":"Capture complete pre-land stage and resource evidence on every landing","description":"Observed by lf performance --json on 2026-07-21. The 14-day scorecard has changed gates 29/46 measured, full gates 7/13, rust 45/48, swift-boundaries 30/32, and build-disk plus child-CPU only 3/59. These gaps prevent the 30-consecutive-landing and 30-day resource KRs from starting.\n\nOutcome: every eligible pre-land run durably records the stages it was required to execute and its build-disk/child-CPU evidence, including failed or interrupted runs, so scorecard missingness reflects a real unsupported boundary rather than dropped capture.\n\nDone when:\n\n* Gate completion writes one atomic durable receipt containing plan, per-stage status/duration, finished_at, build disk bytes, and child CPU seconds.\n* Interrupted and failed gates preserve partial evidence and explicitly name unsupported fields; they never silently disappear.\n* Every landing links to exactly one eligible gate receipt.\n* A deterministic mixed success/failure/interruption fixture produces complete eligible/measured coverage for supported stage and resource rows.\n* Existing source, active worktrees, and durable state are never deleted to satisfy resource accounting.","rank":13,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"25178a6b-4979-4e1b-b08a-dd0790c05d24","identifier":"LOO-18","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-18/make-concurrent-local-ledger-writes-deterministic","name":"Make concurrent local ledger writes deterministic","description":"Reproduction: run multiple read-only lf pm reteam or status commands concurrently against the shared store. At least one process emits ledger insert failed with sqlite database is locked even though the product query itself succeeds. This silently drops execution evidence.\n\nEscalated 2026-07-17: the same SQLITE_BUSY is no longer confined to lost receipts. Under ~51 concurrent Loopflow/provider processes it terminated live Session bodies. In one Developer Efficiency iteration W2-284, W2-285, W2-287 and ENG-18 generation 3 all ended with 'task process failed: sqlite error: database is locked', and the supervising Project Session itself failed the same way twice with nine observations unconsumed. Failures are marked resumable, so recovery is manual resume per Session -- an avoidable human-in-the-loop repair step, which is the first Developer Efficiency KR. Scope the fix to whatever write path a Session body takes at these fanouts, not only the lf pm read commands in the original reproduction.\n\nDone when concurrent local commands never lose ledger records to SQLITE_BUSY: writes use a bounded shared retry or serialization policy, read-only command results remain prompt, failure is explicit if the bound is exhausted, a Session body never dies from contention alone, and a deterministic concurrency test proves every requested execution receipt is recorded exactly once at a fanout at least as large as the observed fleet.","rank":14,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"7a4a384e-440b-4070-a097-3d318e6c288a","identifier":"LOO-19","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-19/review-ready-bars-task-review-on-every-pr-that-carries-its-own-design","name":"review_ready bars Task review on every PR that carries its own design doc","description":"## Problem\n\n`review_ready()` (rust/loopflow/src/task/mod.rs:584, added by #1054 `9f6bdd498`) gates the review handoff on the raw CI discriminant:\n\n```rust\npub fn review_ready(&self) -> bool {\n    self.phase() == PrPhase::Open\n        && self.fresh_ci().is_some_and(|observation| observation.state == CiState::Passing)\n}\n```\n\n`scratch-clear` fails on any PR whose `scratch/` holds more than `.gitkeep`, and `ci.yml` gives `tests-result` `needs: scratch-clear`. So a design-carrying PR is `CiState::Failing` by construction, and only `lf pr land` greens it -- by clearing `scratch/` as its first act. Review is the step that gates land, so the PR can never become review_ready. The condition is unsatisfiable by any action the Task is allowed to take.\n\nLive: W2-294 / PR #1063 sits `waiting` with status_reason \"pull request #1063 is waiting for fresh passing required checks before Task review\" (emitted at [runner.rs:1021](<http://runner.rs:1021>), behind the `!pr.review_ready()` guard at :1008), with 8 leaves green and scratch-clear the only real red. Its Project review cannot be requested. The Task then parks Blocked via `decide_open_pr_status` (\"the Task body did not repair the head. Needs a new directive or human review\") -- the same shape W2-283 recorded on #1061.\n\n## Cause\n\nThis is the SIXTH asker of \"is this failing head actionable by a Task body?\", and W2-309 (#1062, merged `15e441e69`) fixed only the first. `land_time_precondition` now appears 7x in task/mod.rs, and `review_ready` is not one of them -- it re-derives the question as `state == CiState::Passing`.\n\nThe primitive already exists; this is the known half-covered-primitive shape (cf. `heal_task_pr_base`, whose only caller covered one drift direction). Do not mint a second classifier.\n\n## Fix\n\n`review_ready` should treat a Failing observation whose every named failure is a land-time precondition as review-ready, reusing `land_time_precondition`. Preserve the freshness requirement (the head must be current) and keep an unnamed/unclassified failure barring review -- a filter that swallows unknown failures is a mute button.\n\n## Done when\n\n* A design-carrying PR whose only failure is scratch-clear reaches Task review.\n* A PR with any actionable leaf failure (e.g. rust-lint) still does NOT reach review.\n* A Failing observation with an EMPTY failing_checks set still bars review.\n* Sabotage: revert review_ready to `state == Passing` -> the scratch-clear case goes red.\n* The existing `review_ready_requires_current_head_passing_checks` freshness assertions still hold.\n\n## Notes\n\nDo not fix by deleting scratch-clear or by weakening the check: scratch-clear is a correct land-time gate. Fix the asker, not the check. Directly serves the KR \"Avoidable human-in-the-loop setup or repair steps found in agent runs fall to zero\" -- today every design-carrying PR requires a human or Project directive to get past this.","rank":15,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"d33a3fb4-2cfa-4dc2-945f-4282c0c91623","identifier":"LOO-20","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-20/recommend-and-route-only-ci-failures-a-body-can-actually-repair","name":"Recommend and route only CI failures a body can actually repair","description":"W2-309 (#1062, merged 15e441e69) stopped the ci-fix WAKE arming on a land-time precondition. It deliberately left three other askers of the same question wrong. This task owns two of them.\n\n## The shared question, and who asks it\n\n\"Is this failing head actionable by a Task body?\" FIVE consumers ask it; every one computes it as `ci.state == Failing`, which conflates \"red\" with \"a body can fix this\". On a design-carrying PR — every Task PR pre-land — all five are wrong the same way.\n\n1. current_ci_incident -> wake_legal (ops/task.rs) — FIXED on main by #1062\n2. ci_fix_restart_bar -> wake_legal (task/mod.rs) — FIXED on main by #1062\n3. **open_pr_model (task/actions.rs \\~256)** — recommends Resume (\"fix failing required checks\") and BLOCKS Review. THIS TASK.\n4. **next_move (lf/commands/waves.rs \\~1805,1817)** — routes NextMoveOwner::Ci and labels a live body \"fixing CI\". THIS TASK.\n5. decide_open_pr_status (ops/task.rs ~2663) — \"failing && !head_advanced\" -> Blocked, \"the Task body did not repair the head. Needs a new directive or human review\". Defused in practice by #1062 (no wake -> no turn with nothing to push); reopen only if a scratch-clear Blocked is measured.\n\n## The predicate already exists — adopt it, do not re-derive it\n\n`CiCheck::land_time_precondition()` is on main (task/mod.rs:317), with `LAND_TIME_PRECONDITION_CHECK` pinned against .github/workflows/ci.yml by `land_time_precondition_names_a_real_ci_job`. #1062 deliberately put it on CiCheck rather than inside wake_legal so 3 and 4 adopt it in one line each. A second opinion re-derived locally is the defect, not the fix.\n\n## Live evidence (2026-07-17, W2-309s own PR #1062)\n\n`lf task status W2-309` printed, on a head whose only red was scratch-clear:\n\n```\naction: resume  (required checks failed: scratch-clear)\n  blocked: review  (required checks failed)\n```\n\nSo the action model recommended a doomed Resume on the very Task built to stop doomed wakes, and the Waves supervision surface read owner=Ci while the true owner was the reviewer — the Wave chat had to reason around this by hand for several iterations.\n\n## The product call this task must make explicitly\n\n**Should a design-carrying PR be reviewable while scratch-clear is red?** Wave doctrine says yes (\"state the approval condition as *every check green except scratch-clear*\"; tests-result needs scratch-clear so the merge-gating roll-up is RED BY CONSTRUCTION pre-land and only goes green at land, which clears scratch/). Today open_pr_model says no. #1062 deliberately did not smuggle this into a wake fix. Decide it in the open.\n\n## Done when\n\n* A head whose only failures are land-time preconditions: open_pr_model does NOT recommend Resume and does NOT block Review; next_move does not route owner=Ci or label the body \"fixing CI\".\n* A head with a real leaf (rust-test) still recommends Resume and still routes owner=Ci. Both, or it is a mute button.\n* Sabotage: revert each change independently and the corresponding first test goes red. The real-leaf test passes with the bug fully present — that is why this class survived repeatedly.\n* Do NOT touch scratch/, scratch-clear, or tests-result needs: scratch-clear. The check is correct.\n\n## Method note that found this\n\nThe #1062 design first asserted \"wake_legal has exactly the two callers that matter\" — FALSE. That greps a SYMBOL; the question was WHO ASKS THE QUESTION. One `grep -rn \"CiState::Failing\"` found the other three in seconds. When a fix classifies a value, grep every reader of the values discriminant before claiming a caller list is complete: a shared enum arm IS the coupling whether or not anyone calls your function.","rank":16,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"b9236512-6eb8-4ecd-a8cb-dfa02b4e0553","identifier":"LOO-21","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-21/a-rebased-successor-keeps-a-stale-base-so-upstream-only-commits-read","name":"A rebased successor keeps a stale base, so upstream-only commits read as authored work","description":"`lf rebase` can reset an unpublished successor's branch without moving its persisted `base_commit`, and the resulting pair fabricates authored work out of commits that are purely upstream. Measured live on W2-290, 2026-07-17.\n\n## The measurement\n\nW2-290's sequence 2 row, read from `~/.lf/loopflow.db`:\n\n| field | value |\n| -- | -- |\n| `branch` | `jack-heart/recommend-only-executable-task-actions-2` |\n| `base_commit` | `f19f41aab` |\n| branch tip after `lf rebase` | `6232569e4` (current main) |\n\n`git merge-base --is-ancestor f19f41aab origin/main` → **true**. So the recorded base sits *behind* the branch tip, and the interval `base..branch` is `f19f41aab..6232569e4` — **entirely upstream commits, none of them Task work**. `unpublished_work` (ops/task.rs:3455-3458) reads that interval and classifies it as an authored `Range`, while `git diff origin/main...HEAD` on the branch is empty.\n\nThe Task therefore looks like it has work to publish when it provably has none.\n\n## This is NOT c4515194 — check the direction before folding them together\n\n`c4515194` (\"A re-minted successor pairs a fresh base with a stale branch\") is the **M<B** case: base is a *descendant* of the branch, `is_ancestor` is false forever, and emptiness reads **Unprovable**. Symptom: the Task can never settle.\n\nThis is the **B<M** case: base is an *ancestor*, the interval is non-empty but upstream-only, and emptiness reads as a false **Range**. Symptom: the Task fabricates work it never authored.\n\nSame underlying `(base, branch)` incoherence, opposite directions, opposite failure modes, and a fix for one does not touch the other. Do not merge these two Tasks — the distinction is the thing the fix turns on.\n\n## The primitive exists and is half-covered\n\n`heal_task_pr_base` (store/sqlite/child_sessions.rs:3927) exists precisely to move the \"otherwise-immutable base_commit\", and its **only** caller is the publish path (ops/task.rs:1518-1523) — which covers exactly this B<M direction. So the capability is present and simply never reached by rotation or by `lf rebase`.\n\n**Do not \"fix\" this by publishing an empty PR to trigger the heal.** That is using a side effect as a store repair, it mints a head, and on a design-carrying branch it arms another scratch-clear wake. The heal must be reachable without publishing.\n\n## Done when\n\n* A rebase or reset that moves an unpublished successor's branch either moves the persisted base atomically with it, or the classifier reads against upstream authority so upstream-only drift is never authored work. Prefer the shape that makes the incoherent pair unrepresentable over a rule a caller must remember — that is what W2-304 proved.\n* Reuse `heal_task_pr_base`; do not write a second base-mutation path.\n* Regression, and this is the part that matters: **rebase the successor after main advances, then assert the range is empty.** A test that only exercises a first mint — where the branch is created at base — passes with this bug fully present. That is exactly why it survived W2-300, W2-304, and W2-306.\n* Sabotage check: revert the fix and confirm the new test goes red. If it stays green it is pinning the fixture.\n\n## Scope\n\nUnder KR4 (\"A week of normal development — branches, stacks, worktrees, wave homes, landings — requires zero manual git surgery\"). An ordinary `lf rebase` silently corrupting a Task's own record of what it authored is that KR's subject exactly.\n\nRelated, do not conflate: `c4515194` (M<B, Unprovable) and `ae4775c7` (the publish M<B arm reporting \"foreign contamination\" for a base that is origin/main's own tip).","rank":17,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"5f6e8c87-54a6-4cbb-b8b7-1c450b8b90fb","identifier":"LOO-22","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-22/no-surface-reports-that-the-running-lf-is-older-than-merged-main","name":"No surface reports that the running lf is older than merged main","description":"A merged fix that never reaches `/Users/jack/.local/bin/lf` is invisible: nothing on any surface says the running fleet is older than main. Measured 2026-07-17, this silence made three Tasks unrecoverable and is still burning one.\n\n## The evidence\n\nTwo fixes for the review-gate deadlock merged on 2026-07-17:\n\n| fix | Linear | PR | merged | in the running binary? |\n| -- | -- | -- | -- | -- |\n| scope required reviews to active epochs | 7e91c1b7 | #1060 → `6232569e4` | 09:14:19Z | **NO** |\n| persist existing headless interaction policy | 5ce98bb9 | #1051 → `3be919fc0` | 03:39:51Z | yes |\n\nThe fleet binary's mtime is **09:00:12Z** — fourteen minutes before #1060 merged. Verified independently, not inferred: `strings /Users/jack/.local/bin/lf | grep -c \"newest gate should supersede\"` returns **0**, and `lf --version` still reports 0.11.3.\n\nCost, from the live store:\n\n| Task | status | human+require gates active | require+changes_requested |\n| -- | -- | -- | -- |\n| ENG-7 | **abandoned** | 0 | 1 |\n| W2-285 | **abandoned** | 0 | 1 |\n| W2-290 | running | 2 | 1 |\n\nENG-7 and W2-285 were abandoned holding merged work. W2-290 is the last one alive and has burned **7 body generations**; generation 6 stalled 1803s without durable progress and was superseded. Its ladder is the textbook loop tell: sequence 1 = #1041 merged (`f19f41aab`, the real work), sequence 2 = empty and unabandoned. It is at gate cycle 3 minting `human|require` reviews no agent may answer.\n\nEvery one of those bodies is remedied by code that is already on main.\n\n## Why this is the Task, and not \"just run the installer\"\n\nThe two fixes are not the defect. The defect is that **nothing observes the gap**. There is no surface — not `lf doctor`, not `lf status`, not the Task action model — that compares the installed binary against merged main. So:\n\n* A supervisor reads a Task's action model and gets recommendations computed by stale logic, with no hint they are stale.\n* Wave memory has to carry per-fix workarounds (\"check `strings lf | grep -c 'verifiably absent'` before trusting either rule\") because doctrine now depends on which binary is running. That is doctrine paying rent for a missing check.\n* The same reasoning was needed three times this session for three different fixes.\n\n## Done when\n\n* One supported read answers \"is the running `lf` older than merged main, and which merged fixes is it missing?\" `lf doctor` is the natural home; a warning on `lf status` is defensible. Read the answer — do not make it a rule someone must remember.\n* Compare something authoritative. Binary mtime vs merge timestamp is what I used by hand and it is a proxy: it is wrong across a rebuild that installs nothing new, and wrong if the clock moves. Prefer a build-stamped commit sha the binary reports about itself, compared against `origin/main` ancestry. If you use a version string, note that 0.11.3 has been the reported version across every fix above — **the version string is not evidence about the tree**, which this repo has already proven twice (the installed `lf auth --help` still lists `lf auth exec`, a command #1029 deleted).\n* Do NOT auto-install. The signal is the deliverable; the decision to restart the fleet mid-flight is not this Task's to make.\n* Regression: a fixture whose installed stamp predates a merged commit reports the gap, and one whose stamp is current stays silent. Sabotage the comparison — if both fixtures still pass, it is pinning the fixture.\n\n## Scope\n\nUnder KR1: \"Avoidable human-in-the-loop setup or repair steps found in agent runs fall to zero.\" Two abandoned Tasks and seven wasted bodies are exactly that, and none of it was visible from any machine surface.\n\nDo not fix W2-290 here. It needs a rebuild, which is an operator action; its exit already exists in merged code (#1060 drops the epoch-3 bar; #1051 converts its policy so the next gate mints `defer`). This Task is about seeing the gap, not closing that one.","rank":18,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"cf5c1579-b992-45db-bda1-3ec8dddb7cce","identifier":"LOO-23","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-23/make-repeated-pr-land-safe-while-a-pr-is-queued","name":"Make repeated pr land safe while a PR is queued","description":"Reproduction: land a PR, wait for all required checks to pass and GitHub to add it to the merge queue, then invoke lf pr land again. Current behavior rebases the local branch onto current main and attempts a force-with-lease push. GitHub rejects the update because queued branches are protected, leaving the local Task worktree diverged from the queued remote head. Done when lf detects queued state before rebasing or pushing, reports that landing is already in progress, preserves the local branch, and a deterministic test covers the repeated invocation.","rank":19,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"47880291-bc80-447a-8a47-d61077336913","identifier":"LOO-24","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-24/subsumed-by-loo-9-restore-the-verifier-host-from-sustained-syspolicyd","name":"[subsumed by LOO-9] Restore the verifier host from sustained syspolicyd pressure","description":"Re-measured 2026-07-17 ~11:10Z: syspolicyd (pid 5063) at 100.8% CPU and 497:08 elapsed CPU, up from 477:03 measured ~40min earlier. It is STILL ACCUMULATING, which sharpens the diagnosis: this is not a daemon that wedged once and went idle, it is actively burning CPU continuously. That distinction matters for the fix -- a stuck-then-idle daemon might be tolerable and worked around, but a continuously-burning one taxes every local build on this host for as long as it runs, and it is the measured cause of the earlier build hang (EXIT=143 = 128+15 = SIGTERM). Not a Gatekeeper denial. Restarting it is an operator action; this Project owns no host. Blocks KR3 (local pre-land verification budgets) directly, since the verifier cannot run here at all.","rank":20,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"2b7bad6d-db9b-40e8-aece-552d4c87ade7","identifier":"LOO-25","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-25/keep-unpublished-empty-proof-coherent-when-rebase-advances-the-branch","name":"Keep unpublished-empty proof coherent when rebase advances the branch","description":"## Incident\n\nW2-290 sequence 2 was minted at base f19f41aab. After main advanced, lf rebase classified the unpublished successor as disposable and reset its branch to current main 6232569e45, restoring scratch correctly. The TaskPr.base_commit stayed f19f41aab.\n\nThe branch then had no diff from origin/main, but unpublished_work classified the upstream-only base..branch interval as authored Range. A proven-empty successor became uncompletable solely because rebase advanced one half of the persisted branch/base pair.\n\n## Boundary\n\nThis is not W2-306. W2-306 owns mint-time M<B incoherence: a fresh base paired with a reused stale branch. This task owns post-mint B<M drift: rebase/reset advances an unpublished branch while its persisted base remains behind.\n\nThe publish path already heals base-behind-merge-base through heal_task_pr_base. Do not use an empty publish as an implicit store repair.\n\n## Done when\n\n* A deterministic reset/rebase of an unpublished empty successor cannot manufacture authored Task work from upstream-only commits.\n* The branch position and persisted TaskPr base advance atomically, or classification uses upstream authority so the pair cannot disagree.\n* A regression starts with an unpublished ProvenEmpty successor, advances main, runs the supported rebase/reset path, and still proves empty without publishing.\n* Genuine authored carry remains Range; the fix cannot fail open by recording the branch tip as the base.\n* Completion needs no manual store edit, empty publish, or Git surgery.\n\n## KRs\n\nAdvances zero manual Git surgery and no Task strands on lifecycle bookkeeping.","rank":21,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"a5dedd47-15f5-427d-9700-42558b931125","identifier":"LOO-26","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-26/the-review-handoff-ci-gate-deadlocks-every-pr-that-carries-its-own","name":"The review-handoff CI gate deadlocks every PR that carries its own design doc","description":"Live and systematic since #1054 landed 2026-07-17 06:58Z and the fleet binary was rebuilt 08:11:57Z. Two Tasks stalled within the hour, by two different mechanisms with one root.\n\n## The deadlock\n\ntask/runner.rs:995-1010 parks an Iterate Task with 'pull request #N is waiting for fresh passing required checks before Task review' whenever the PR is Open and !review_ready(). review_ready() (task/mod.rs:538) demands fresh_ci() == Some(CiState::Passing).\n\nFor a Task PR carrying its design doc that condition is UNSATISFIABLE before land:\n\n* scratch-clear fails whenever scratch/ holds anything but .gitkeep — true of every kickoff/iterate PR, which is exactly when review is open.\n* ci.yml:169-173 gives tests-result 'needs: scratch-clear', so the required roll-up is red by construction.\n* 'lf pr land' clears scratch/ as its first act and both go green — but land is the step review is gating.\n\nSo: review waits for CI, CI waits for land, land waits for review.\n\n## Measured, both mechanisms\n\nW2-283 / #1061 (head e5acba9d) — the DIRECT case:\n  gh pr checks <branch> --required  ->  [{'bucket':'fail','name':'tests-result'}]\n  ci_observation                    ->  {'state':'failing', ...}\n  full set: 12 checks, failing = ['tests-result','scratch-clear'], pending = none.\n  Every other leaf passes. review_ready() = false forever. Task parked.\n\nW2-297 / #1060 (head 58c47028c) — the INDIRECT case, same root:\n  gh pr checks <branch> --required  ->  'no required checks reported'\n  merge_gate_state (ops/pr.rs:511) returns None when the required set is empty/unparseable\n  ci_observation                    ->  EMPTY  =>  fresh_ci() = None  =>  review_ready() = false\n  full set: every leaf passes; only scratch-clear fails, tests-result fails as its roll-up.\n\nNote the two branches disagree about whether --required reports anything, which is itself worth understanding rather than assuming (I generalized from two branches and #1061 disproved it — do not repeat that). Either way review_ready() is false and the Task parks.\n\n## Cost\n\nThis blocks the Project's critical path right now: #1060 is 7e91c1b7's fix, which frees three permanently-uncompletable Tasks (W2-285, ENG-7, W2-290) and eight human require gates. Every Task PR is affected because every Task PR carries its design doc during review. It is KR1's subject exactly: an avoidable step no human asked for, blocking real runs.\n\n## Done when\n\n* A Task PR carrying its design doc reaches review handoff. Judge readiness on ACTIONABLE LEAF checks, never on a roll-up whose only red is a land-time precondition. ops/pr.rs already draws this distinction for ci-fix seeding (MergeGateReading::failing_leaves excludes the aggregate); the handoff gate should use the same authority rather than the raw --required verdict.\n* Decide the rule by reading, not by name-matching 'scratch-clear' — a literal name list rots.\n* Distinguish 'CI is unknown' (merge_gate_state None) from 'CI is failing'. Today both park the Task identically, which is why #1060 and #1061 stall the same way for opposite reasons. Unknown must not read as not-ready forever.\n* Regression, both directions and both FAIL on today's code: (1) a PR whose only red is scratch-clear reaches review handoff; (2) a PR with a genuinely failing leaf (e.g. rust-test) still parks. A test using only a real failure passes with this bug fully present.\n* Do NOT fix by deleting design docs earlier or changing scratch-clear. The check is correct.\n\n## Related\n\n## 232b91b5 (a ci-fix wake arms on scratch-clear) and W2-308 (a claimed CI wake preempts an interactive review wait) are the same family: the check is right; what the system does in response to it is wrong. W2-308's evidence is 4/4 scratch-clear incidents. Sequence these deliberately — W2-308 preempting a review to service an unserviceable wake would make this worse, not better.\n\n## CORRECTION 2026-07-17 (Project, iteration 33) — read this before designing\n\nTwo things above are wrong or stale. Both would cost a full design cycle.\n\n### 1. Do NOT reuse `MergeGateReading::failing_leaves`. It was suggested twice and disproved twice.\n\nThe \"Done when\" above says the handoff gate should use `failing_leaves`' authority. That was my suggestion and it is wrong. **W2-306 and W2-309 each independently disproved it by reading the code**, not by argument:\n\n`from_checks` separates the *required aggregate* from *leaf* checks. That is a structural required-vs-full test. Whether **land itself greens a check** is a different axis entirely, and it is invisible to that helper — `scratch-clear` is a genuine leaf, sitting right beside `rust-test`. Filtering there would leave a `failing` state with **no named checks**, which is worse than the deadlock.\n\n### 2. The primitive you need already exists — on W2-309's branch, not main.\n\nW2-309 (PR #1062, `a-ci-fix-wake-arms`, **approved**, awaiting land) introduces exactly the classifier this Task needs:\n\n```rust\n/// The required check `lf pr land` greens itself, by clearing `scratch/`.\nconst LAND_TIME_PRECONDITION_CHECK: &str = \"scratch-clear\";\n// -> check.land_time_precondition()\n```\n\npinned against `.github/workflows/ci.yml` so a rename fails at the source rather than rotting into a stale literal. That answers this Task's \"decide the rule by reading, not by name-matching\" requirement.\n\n**Sequencing: do not start until #1062 is merged.** Reuse `land_time_precondition()`; do not mint a parallel classifier. A second spelling of the same predicate is the defect, not the fix.\n\n### 3. This is the SIXTH asker of one question, and #1054 is the proximate cause.\n\nWave memory records five call sites re-deriving \"is this failing head actionable by a Task body?\" as `ci.state == Failing`. `review_ready()` (task/mod.rs:538) is the sixth, added by **#1054** `task: wait for green CI before review handoff` (merged 2026-07-16 23:58Z, `9f6bdd498`):\n\n```rust\npub fn review_ready(&self) -> bool {\n    self.phase() == PrPhase::Open\n        && self.fresh_ci().is_some_and(|o| o.state == CiState::Passing)\n}\n```\n\n#1054 is a correct fix for the problem it names and it introduced this deadlock in the same commit — that is the tell that the question needs **one** authority, not a seventh hand-rolled spelling. When you fix this, grep `CiState::Passing` **and** `CiState::Failing` across the tree and state in the design which askers you are and are not unifying. Grep the *shape* of the fact, not the name of the function you are standing on.\n\n### 4. Live cost, measured 2026-07-17 iteration 33\n\nThree Tasks are parked at \"PR is open for review\" with **every review disposed and zero gates open to anyone** — nothing is coming to free them:\n\n| Task | PR | reviews |\n| -- | -- | -- |\n| W2-283 | #1061 | 7 completed (last two approved) |\n| W2-300 | #1050 | 7 completed (last two approved) |\n| W2-306 | #1059 | 1 completed, approved |\n\nThey are not waiting on a reviewer. They are waiting on a condition that cannot occur.","rank":22,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"4974338a-e917-484a-8ff8-f9cac4abcf8c","identifier":"LOO-27","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-27/self-heal-pm-snapshots-when-a-referenced-project-disappears","name":"Self-heal PM snapshots when a referenced Project disappears","description":"Real Product Wave surface proof currently fails because the Wave references project product-performance, but the current PM snapshot omits it. Done when: (1) status/roadmap and Mac consumers can distinguish stale or missing Project data from an honestly empty portfolio; (2) a referenced Project missing from a snapshot triggers bounded targeted reconciliation or exposes one actionable recovery path, never a silent zero-project result; (3) successful sync restores the Project, KRs, open Task counts, and Task rows without restarting the Wave; (4) deterministic tests remove one referenced Project and prove recovery plus honest partial or unavailable behavior; (5) the real Product Wave capture renders its referenced projects from a fresh snapshot.","rank":23,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"c4515194-320b-4a05-ba6a-2d9b3a2dc118","identifier":"LOO-28","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-28/a-re-minted-successor-pairs-a-fresh-base-with-a-stale-branch-so-its","name":"A re-minted successor pairs a fresh base with a stale branch, so its emptiness is permanently unprovable","description":"## Measured live on W2-300, 2026-07-17 ~08:20Z\n\nA Task whose work has merged cannot complete, reopens itself, and re-mints its successor forever. Diagnosed to an incoherent (base, branch) pair created by the rotation — not by either shipped fix, both of which are individually correct.\n\n### The loop, measured\n\n1. `lf task complete W2-300` SUCCEEDED: W2-304's discardable-successor path (#1056) settled the proven-empty sequence 2 in the terminal write and removed it from the ladder. Only merged #1050 remained. Status: completed. PM writeback: current.\n2. Moments later the Session reopened itself:\n   `reopened: completion outran its gates (cannot prove unpublished pull request sequence 2 is empty: the recorded base is not an ancestor of the unpublished branch)`\n3. Sequence 2 was RE-MINTED, and the new row is incoherent:\n   * `base_commit = 3e9df06777297cd1cb83fccc1d0261fd3e74dfa8` (origin/main tip AT RE-MINT TIME — W2-283's #1053 advanced main to it at 08:02Z)\n   * `branch  jack-heart/do-not-complete-a-task-2` still points at `9f6bdd4986c29a111726758701d7729781680e4a` (the tip when the branch was FIRST minted)\n4. The gate asks 'is base an ancestor of branch?'. 3e9df0677 is a DESCENDANT of 9f6bdd498, so `is_ancestor` is false -> Unprovable -> fail closed -> refuse completion -> reopen -> re-mint against an even newer main. **It never converges**: every merge to main widens the gap.\n\n### Why this is not W2-300's or W2-304's bug\n\n* W2-300/#1050's fail-closed-on-Unprovable rule is CORRECT and must stay. Only ProvenEmpty may permit completion.\n* W2-304/#1056's settlement is CORRECT and proven — it worked on W2-280 (sequence 4 settled, Task stayed completed) and on the FIRST W2-300 completion.\n* The defect is the MINT: it records `base = current origin/main` while reusing an existing branch ref without moving it. On a first mint the branch is created at base, so they agree and the cut is ProvenEmpty. On a RE-mint after main advanced, the branch is stale and the pair is incoherent by construction.\n\n### Why it matters (KR1 + the dead-body KR)\n\nThis is the ENG-7 burn by a new mechanism: a Task that can never complete and re-mints a body per cycle, unbounded. It is worse now, not better: ENG-4's shipped lease release (#1055, installed 08:11:57Z) will free the revoked lease on the next launch attempt, which ENABLES the burn that a pinned lease was accidentally suppressing.\n\n### Done when\n\n* A re-minted successor's branch and recorded base agree by construction. Either reset the reused branch to the newly recorded base at mint, or record the base the branch actually sits at. Pick one authority; do not compute the pair in two places.\n* Regression that FAILS on today's code: mint a successor at base B1, advance origin/main to B2, force a re-mint, and assert the completion cut reads ProvenEmpty (not Unprovable) and the Task completes exactly once with no replacement row.\n* Sabotage proof: revert the mint fix and the regression must go red. A test that only exercises a first mint passes with this bug fully present — first mints are coherent, which is why this survived W2-300 and W2-304.\n* Do NOT fix this by loosening the Unprovable rule; that would re-open the fail-open hole #1050 closed.\n\n### Live repair still owed\n\nW2-300 is sitting in this loop now: status=waiting, sequence 2 re-minted, base 3e9df0677 vs branch 9f6bdd498. Its real work (#1050 / 0fef6d2ce) is on main. Do not abandon the successor — abandoning a rotation artifact mints its replacement (ENG-7).","rank":24,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"232b91b5-fcbe-43fa-b2cd-f4ec089bfca9","identifier":"LOO-30","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-30/a-ci-fix-wake-arms-on-scratch-clear-a-check-no-task-action-can-ever","name":"A ci-fix wake arms on scratch-clear, a check no Task action can ever green","description":"Measured twice on 2026-07-17, both times on a PR whose only red was structural. Each occurrence burns an agent body on a repair that cannot exist.\n\n## The defect\n\nci_incidents records failure_set ['scratch-clear'] and arms a ci-fix wake:\n\n* ENG-4 / PR #1055, head 4041c795e\n* W2-297 / PR #1060, head 58c47028c   <- while W2-297 was landing 7e91c1b7's fix\n\nscratch-clear asserts a LAND-TIME precondition ('scratch/ must be clear before landing'). It fails whenever scratch/ holds anything but .gitkeep — i.e. on EVERY PR carrying its own design doc, which is every Task PR by construction during kickoff/iterate. No code change can green it. 'lf pr land' clears scratch/ as its first act and the check passes.\n\nSo the wake asks a body to repair something that is not broken and cannot be repaired. The only action that would 'work' is deleting the reviewed design doc — destroying the artifact the reviewer reads, to green a check land greens anyway. Measured shape on #1060 at 58c47028c: rust-test, rust-lint, swift-test, e2e-smoke, python-test, website-test, loopflow-ui-test, migration-check ALL pass; scratch-clear fail; tests-result fail SOLELY as its roll-up (ci.yml:169-173 gives it 'needs: scratch-clear').\n\n## Why it costs\n\nEach arming spends a full body on a bounded repair turn that can produce nothing. It is the avoidable-repair-step KR's exact subject: an agent run doing work no human would ask for. Worse, the body's only route to 'success' is destructive.\n\n## Done when\n\n* A required check that asserts a land-time precondition never arms a ci-fix wake. Decide the rule by reading, not by name-matching a literal 'scratch-clear' string — a name list rots. Candidate: the incident classifier already distinguishes the merge-gate roll-up from actionable leaves (ops/pr.rs merge_gate_state / MergeGateReading::failing_leaves); a check that land itself resolves belongs in the same non-actionable category as the aggregate.\n* Regression: a head whose ONLY failing check is scratch-clear arms NO ci-fix wake, and a head where a real leaf (e.g. rust-test) fails still arms one. Both, or the fix is a mute button.\n* Sabotage: revert the classifier change and the first test goes red. A test using a head with a real failure passes with the bug fully present — that is why this survived two occurrences.\n* Do NOT 'fix' this by making scratch-clear pass earlier, and do NOT touch scratch/ handling. The check is correct; arming a repair against it is not.\n\n## Related\n\nSame family as ae4775c7 (a diagnostic that misreports its own cause). Both are 'the message/wake is wrong, the check is right'. See wave memory 2026-07-17 for the full ENG-4 measurement and the structural tests-result explanation.","rank":25,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"e625050f-174f-4841-b7f2-c5e817fd004e","identifier":"LOO-31","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-31/a-claimed-ci-wake-preempts-an-interactive-review-wait","name":"A claimed CI wake preempts an interactive review wait","description":"<!-- loopflow-task-start:3a1447c64089f5fdfda7dbeaa3f1056d585453fd6aa00075858ee8561c5f1df8 -->","rank":26,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"b9843f04-f093-4e3f-a2f7-c13695cd502c","identifier":"LOO-33","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-33/reap-orphaned-provider-processes-and-classify-their-children","name":"Reap orphaned provider processes and classify their children accurately","description":"User story\\n\\nAs an operator, a provider server and all of its language/tool subprocesses live no longer than the Task or Project body lease that owns them, and `lf top` attributes those children to that body instead of counting them as independent workers.\\n\\nDone when\\n\\n- Body completion, failure, interruption, replacement, and lost-lease reconciliation terminate the owned provider tree within a bounded grace period.\\n- Active replacement bodies and unrelated provider sessions are never killed.\\n- `lf top` groups language servers and provider children under the owning body.\\n- Cleanup and classification emit auditable receipts.\\n- A deterministic crash/resume test covers the current PID-1-adopted OpenCode-server shape.\\n\\nThis Task absorbs W2-242, whose narrower incident was an orphaned `opencode serve` process after W2-225 lost its historical body binary. Keep W2-242 open as the linked incident until this Task lands, then close it with the shared proof rather than implementing both.","rank":27,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"43036db1-5680-4180-befc-b62a3af7eb47","identifier":"LOO-34","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-34/make-lf-wt-ci-logs-fetch-the-failed-check-output","name":"Make lf wt ci logs fetch the failed check output","description":"User story\\n\\nAs an operator investigating a failed PR, `lf wt ci --logs` prints every failed check log with clear check attribution instead of constructing a malformed GitHub API path.\\n\\nObserved failure\\n\\nThe current path passes a full GitHub Actions job URL as a run identifier, producing paths shaped like `/actions/runs/https://github.com/...` and HTTP 404. PRs #978 and #983 provide the observed URL shape.\\n\\nDone when\\n\\n- Failed checks resolve their run and job ids from GitHub URLs or numeric ids.\\n- Logs for every failed check print with check attribution.\\n- Missing, expired, inaccessible, and private logs fail actionably.\\n- URL and numeric-id variants are covered.\\n- A deterministic integration test proves the #978/#983-shaped URL never becomes a malformed API request.","rank":28,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"69ad6b5c-f4ce-443d-9b47-c77ebfa98d3a","identifier":"LOO-35","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-35/settle-merged-work-over-a-proven-empty-successor","name":"Settle merged work over a proven-empty successor","description":"Observed on W2-280 after substantive PR #1048 merged as 047429047f6acd3b8926cfd71648cfcd5a7e24a6, all defer-policy reviews were approved, and the body finished. The lifecycle created sequence 4 as an unpublished successor. That successor has no publication and no authored commits, but lf task complete refuses until it is published and merged or abandoned. Publishing fake empty work would misstate the Task; abandoning can feed another empty rotation.\n\nScope:\n\n* At completion reconciliation, discard a lifecycle-created active successor only when its post-merge follow-up is ProvenEmpty, then settle the authoritative merged predecessor and Task without starting a body or minting another PR.\n* Range and Unprovable remain blockers and preserve every committed follow-up. Reuse the explicit tri-state from W2-300 after it lands or rebases; do not duplicate its committed-descendant work.\n* Do not weaken review eligibility or gates. W2-297 remains the owner of changes-requested eligibility and the permanent-review deadlock.\n\nProof:\n\n* Reproduce the W2-280 shape: a substantive merged predecessor plus an unpublished proven-empty successor completes, marks the successor terminal and non-authoritative, and mints neither a body generation nor a replacement PR.\n* A descendant Range refuses completion and preserves the work.\n* Unprovable refuses completion.\n* Once settled, later reconciliation cannot move the Task out of Completed.\n\nSequencing: filed only. Do not launch until W2-300 settles or its tri-state is available on the chosen base.","rank":29,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"9063d906-676a-4ea5-a97d-c4096d41b5cd","identifier":"LOO-36","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-36/never-instruct-a-task-to-reply-to-a-closed-review","name":"Never instruct a Task to reply to a closed review","description":"## Problem\n\nOn 2026-07-17 ENG-4 received Project review feedback for `ir_99e5e2d3275649fbbfd367f0071918e7` ending with an explicit `lf task review reply ir_99e5e2d3275649fbbfd367f0071918e7 \"<answer>\"` instruction. At delivery, the authoritative interaction-review row was already `project|completed|defer|changes_requested`, so the named command immediately refused with `interaction review is not open for this Task Session`. Task status still described the same review as requested.\n\nThis is an executable-instruction and read-model boundary, not review-gate policy. A machine-generated next step must be executable against the authoritative review state when it is delivered.\n\n## Done when\n\n* A deterministic fixture completes a defer-policy Project review before its feedback reaches the Task and proves the delivered guidance does not name `review reply` for the closed review.\n* Feedback still reaches the Task through a durable surface it can consume, and the Task can leave the review step without reopening or rewriting the completed review.\n* An active-review control proves `review reply` remains available when the authoritative row is genuinely open.\n* Task status and next-owner text stop describing a completed review as requested.\n* Sabotage that renders from the stale requested projection makes the fixture fail.\n* Human-review authority remains intact: no Task, Project, or Wave agent gains permission to answer a human review.\n\n## Not in scope\n\n* Require-policy `changes_requested` terminality or empty-successor burn; W2-297 owns those.\n* Post-merge action recommendations; W2-290 owns those.\n* Evaporated interrupt semantics; ENG-3 owns those.\n* Revoked-lease release or its CAS message; ENG-4 owns those.\n* Reopening or mutating completed interaction reviews.","rank":30,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"4875ce7c-c34c-46f6-8810-56041030bff4","identifier":"LOO-38","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-38/wait-for-green-ci-before-task-review","name":"Wait for green CI before Task review","description":"<!-- loopflow-task-start:d9ca5bbc5553f87f3ee4408e116e315cb5ceb1d4344a4c71a8fa4d299072396f -->","rank":31,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"b7638e2e-6101-42ee-b7be-9eaf2874a63d","identifier":"LOO-40","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-40/make-ambiguous-task-ancestry-refusal-actionable-and-fully-proved","name":"Make ambiguous Task ancestry refusal actionable and fully proved","description":"<!-- loopflow-task-start:3759cacf66a2bdab60d41d8e9f75d3eafce867a60bbdccec66243fb8be2c363e -->","rank":32,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"e796f903-aa86-481f-a7c0-f15eb0bde9d8","identifier":"LOO-41","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-41/require-an-authoritative-non-empty-range-for-every-task-pr-operation","name":"Require an authoritative non-empty range for every Task PR operation","description":"<!-- loopflow-task-start:95fde5845a4bcd72969c824a3c3793e65481af77d75573c4317f3b4eb73ab1ae -->","rank":33,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"d6318baf-19f7-4419-966b-3988b6775659","identifier":"LOO-42","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-42/fail-task-pr-operations-closed-without-durable-authority","name":"Fail Task PR operations closed without durable authority","description":"<!-- loopflow-task-start:73d3ea17f276258d8626df95a40c28b8df03f7a8cf0d89bf193c080dce04f9ee -->","rank":34,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"6ec7e9e8-74cb-42b8-8224-947a92bdeb1e","identifier":"LOO-43","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-43/compute-truthful-legal-task-actions-from-full-lifecycle-state","name":"Compute truthful legal Task actions from full lifecycle state","description":"LIVE MEASURED INSTANCE, 2026-07-17 ~11:25Z, W2-311 (PR #1065). This is the defect's full shape with every door checked, not an inference.\n\nW2-311 sat 'waiting', generation 1 finished (outcome=completed), directive 2/1 -- a replacement steer minted and nothing could consume it. Its action model offered exactly ONE recommendation: 'resume (required checks failed: scratch-clear, rust-test)'. That resume is refused for the only actor present. open_pr_bar answers a Project-issued 'lf task resume W2-311' with: 'Task W2-311 submitted pull request #1065 and is awaiting review; resume it explicitly with lf task resume W2-311' -- verbatim the command just run. Every other action was blocked and I read each: recover ('body is not dead; fix the checks'), review ('required checks failed'), start_next_pr ('PR is open, not merged'), complete ('PR is open, not merged').\n\nNO SELF-HEAL PATH EXISTS, verified rather than assumed: the incident at head cb28f3c22 ['rust-test','scratch-clear'] is already armed AND responded, and incident identity is (repo, PR, head, failure set), so dedup guarantees no second wake at that identity. The body was gone, the wake could not re-arm, and the one recommended action was barred. The Wave had to name an external operator to break it.\n\nTHE BAR'S REASON IS FALSE, and that is separable from the bar being right. W2-311's only interaction review, ir_a4e555ab, reads project|completed|approved, and the reviews-open-to-me gate query returns zero rows. 'Awaiting review' actually means 'a PR is open'. So the message sends a supervisor to answer a review that does not exist, via a command that supervisor cannot execute. The bar's SEPARATION (supervisor restart vs operator resume) is correct and must stay -- W2-129 is the regression it prevents. What is wrong is that one spelling means two things depending on ambient LF_PROJECT_SESSION_ID, and only the human reading is in the text.\n\nTWO CONSEQUENCES FOR THIS TASK'S FIX. (1) An action model that recommends exactly one action must not recommend one the caller provably cannot run; the caller's identity is already known (ChildCommandSource classification reads ambient LF_WAVE_ID/LF_PROJECT_SESSION_ID), so 'resume' should read as blocked-for-supervisor with the real next owner named. (2) A refusal on a machine-reachable path must name the actual next owner. Both my steer and my resume PERSISTED as durable commands while the CLI returned an error -- a refusal is not proof of no side effect -- so a machine that retries mints duplicates without ever launching.\n\nCost: a Task with an approved design and six non-scratch files of finished implementation ([doctor.rs](<http://doctor.rs>) +104, build_info.rs +292, bin/lf.rs, engine/git.rs, child_session.rs, store/mod.rs) parked on an ordinary serviceable rust-test failure it was fully equipped to fix. This is precisely Developer Efficiency KR1 ('avoidable human-in-the-loop repair steps in agent runs fall to zero'), so it is KR evidence and not merely a bug report.","rank":35,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"b830a461-6dfc-49d4-840b-0b33e75371c8","identifier":"LOO-44","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-44/validate-recovered-worktrees-before-committing-a-successor","name":"Validate recovered worktrees before committing a successor","description":"<!-- loopflow-task-start:d01e45a91f4c91c4c598bbec38c4ea10f47bb8fc740612375c6309e4109247b0 -->","rank":36,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"4d65cab8-0365-44b2-8ff2-77ef704567a0","identifier":"LOO-45","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-45/make-loopflow-performance-and-cost-visible-and-trustworthy","name":"Make Loopflow performance and cost visible and trustworthy","description":"Outcome: Humans and agents can see where ordinary Loopflow work spends time and resources, compare it with explicit budgets, and trust the numbers across every provider. Contract: publish one scorecard for Task launch-to-first-progress, pre-land verification stages, land-to-merge, avoidable repair, build and disk use, CPU, and agent spend; establish a 14-day baseline and named p50, p95, and resource budgets; use one authoritative per-turn usage path so missing usage is distinct from reported zero and no parser can overwrite accepted truth. Proof: provider conformance tests assert concrete token values, every landing emits the same bounded measures, and the scorecard can decide each Performance and Efficiency KR without manual reconstruction.","rank":37,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"7bccbbe2-39df-4d18-bb3f-ba1b000190c0","identifier":"LOO-46","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-46/do-not-complete-a-task-over-committed-follow-up-work","name":"Do not complete a Task over committed follow-up work","description":"Observed on W2-293/#1042: GitHub merged head b00405e, while the Task worktree had acknowledged directive v3 and committed follow-up 4d8d82e. Reconciliation marked the Task Completed because the PR was complete_task and no directive was pending, ignoring commits reachable from the Task branch beyond publication.github.head_sha. The work survived locally but fell outside Task ownership. Completion must block and preserve/rotate committed follow-up, using the same committed_follow_up_range already used for serial rotation. Prove a completing PR with a descendant local commit cannot complete or delete its work; no new schema or compatibility state.","rank":38,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"3cd6154a-82c3-40ca-8580-dbf9881a950b","identifier":"LOO-47","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-47/require-a-serviced-ci-fix-wake-before-counting-autonomy","name":"Require a serviced ci-fix wake before counting autonomy","description":"PR #1042 landed the trigger-attribution half, but its final local commit 4d8d82e8f added the missing responded_at requirement after the merge-group captured the earlier head. A persisted trigger alone does not prove ci-fix ran; normal Task work can make it green before service. Land the preserved one-predicate follow-up and its minimal cases: no trigger excluded, trigger without response excluded, trigger+response counted. No schema/DTO changes.","rank":39,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"eedf94f2-566b-4d6f-b043-85449d318474","identifier":"LOO-48","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-48/preserve-external-task-direction-across-session-succession","name":"Preserve external Task direction across Session succession","description":"<!-- loopflow-task-start:4a0e700bff85bbfd67edf732522e6dd92ce79f7de5c9e300681f892bce56c344 -->","rank":40,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"bd3d4f6d-4278-4103-9ed2-0c9a2e1bea2e","identifier":"LOO-49","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-49/resolve-the-current-task-attempt-consistently-after-recovery","name":"Resolve the current Task attempt consistently after recovery","description":"<!-- loopflow-task-start:350bd02556d30224ca27de8f99a4832ecc00d45c40f6964bbc2ebc5a72a9c992 -->","rank":41,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"38f9250a-4339-4c3d-85e0-6e24cf105956","identifier":"LOO-50","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-50/make-repeated-closed-pr-reconciliation-idempotent","name":"Make repeated closed-PR reconciliation idempotent","description":"W2-283/#1039 proves repeated reconciliation of an already-abandoned published PR is not idempotent. The closed arm assigns abandoned_at=now on every read; settle_task_pr_on then compares the already-settled row and rejects the new timestamp as \"already settled differently\", blocking status and resume while #1040 is open. Preserve the first abandoned_at when GitHub remains closed (set only if None). Add a focused repeat-reconcile proof; no schema, compatibility path, or new lifecycle state. After landing, resume W2-283 so its committed design work rotates through the supported serial-PR path.","rank":42,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"5ce98bb9-5285-4799-a602-fd3bc2d343b2","identifier":"LOO-51","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-51/lf-task-run-headless-silently-no-ops-on-an-existing-task","name":"lf task run --headless silently no-ops on an existing Task","description":"## Problem\n\n`lf task run <issue> --headless` on an **existing** Task Session is a silent\nno-op that reports success. The interaction policy is never persisted, so the\nTask keeps routing its interactive lifecycle steps to a human who, in an\nautonomous run, does not exist.\n\nThe three policy columns are **write-once at INSERT**. No UPDATE statement in\nthe store writes them:\n\n```\n$ grep -c \"interaction_policy=?\" rust/loopflow/src/store/sqlite/child_sessions.rs\n0\n```\n\n`TASK_SESSION_UPDATE` (child_sessions.rs:3356) sets ~30 columns — issue,\nproject, wave, status, worktree, agent, directives, lf_bin, abandon_* — and\nnone of `kickoff_interaction_policy`, `iterate_interaction_policy`,\n`gate_interaction_policy` (task_sessions cols 46/42/48).\n\n## Why it reports success\n\n`_defer_task_interactions` (ops/task.rs:415) mutates `session.lifecycle` in\nmemory, sets `session.updated_at = now`, and returns `Ok(true)`. `task_run`\n(ops/task.rs:537) then calls `update_task_session`, which writes `updated_at`\n— so the row visibly changes — executes with `changed == 1`, returns `Ok(())`,\nand drops the policy silently. No error path exists. The command prints the\nTask's status as if it had converted.\n\n`updated_at` moving while the policy does not is the tell, and it is what\nproves the defer function ran rather than being skipped by a guard.\n\n## Reproduction (live, 2026-07-17)\n\nW2-290 was launched without `--headless`, so its `review-design` gate minted\n`reviewer: {kind: \"human\"}` (ir_6cf5168b049c4731a55e7939bfa6252e). In a\nheadless run no human can answer it. The parent Project cannot answer it\neither — correctly, per #1034: `lf task review message` refuses with\n\"human review commands cannot run inside a Task, Project, or Wave agent\nsession\". The documented remedy is the flag, and the flag cannot work:\n\n```\n$ lf task run W2-290 --headless        # body alive\nError: Task W2-290 has an active body; interrupt or wait for it before marking the Task headless\n$ lf task interrupt W2-290             # body stops, status=waiting\n$ lf task run W2-290 --headless        # prints status, exit 0, \"succeeds\"\n$ sqlite3 ~/.lf/loopflow.db \"SELECT kickoff_interaction_policy, iterate_interaction_policy,\n    gate_interaction_policy FROM task_sessions WHERE issue_identifier='W2-290';\"\nrequire|defer|require                  # unchanged\n```\n\nContrast, same store, same moment — Tasks launched *with* `--headless` at\nINSERT are correct, which is why their gates route to the Project and are\nanswerable:\n\n```\nW2-280|defer|defer|defer|running\nW2-287|defer|defer|defer|failed\nENG-7 |require|defer|require|running   # launched without --headless\nW2-290|require|defer|require|waiting   # launched without --headless\n```\n\nSo the launch-time path works and only the conversion path is broken. The bug\nis invisible unless you compare two Tasks launched differently.\n\n## Why the tests miss it\n\n`ops/task.rs:5841` asserts `_defer_task_interactions(&mut session).unwrap()`\nreturns true, and `:5843` asserts the second call returns false. Both assert\nthe **in-memory return value**. No test round-trips a deferred session through\n`update_task_session` + re-read, which is the only thing that would catch a\nmissing SET clause. This is the wave's known \"a test pins the fixture, not the\nbehavior\" shape: the function under test is honest, and the layer beneath it\ndiscards the result.\n\n## Done when\n\n* Converting an existing Task to headless persists. Round-trip is the proof:\n  write, re-read from the store, assert all three policies are `defer`.\n* Sabotage it: delete the new SET clause and confirm the round-trip test goes\n  red. A test asserting `_defer_task_interactions` returns true passes with the\n  persistence still missing — that is the current state, and it is why this\n  shipped.\n* `--headless` cannot silently no-op. If a policy write is refused or dropped,\n  the command fails loudly rather than printing status. Decide whether any\n  *other* field on TaskSession is mutated-then-dropped by the same UPDATE — the\n  SET clause is hand-maintained against a growing struct, so audit it once\n  rather than patching three columns.\n\n## Bounds\n\nDo not weaken the #1034 authority boundary to work around this. An agent\nanswering its own human review gate is the thing that boundary exists to\nprevent; the fix is to make `--headless` work, not to let Projects dispose\nhuman reviews.\n\n## Scope correction (2026-07-17)\n\nPersisting the three lifecycle policies is necessary but does not by itself solve the live W2-290 reproduction. That Task already owns active review `ir_6cf5168b049c4731a55e7939bfa6252e`, whose row is `policy=require` and `reviewer=human`. `_defer_task_interactions` changes only `TaskSession.lifecycle`; `open_interaction_review` validation binds review policy and reviewer to the waitpoint, and no conversion path rewrites or supersedes the existing row.\n\nThe command must therefore make this boundary explicit. When converting an existing Task that already has an unanswered human review, it must either:\n\n* safely replace or supersede that active review with one Project-owned Defer review at the same waitpoint, preserving evidence and without letting an agent answer a human review; or\n* refuse before mutating any policy and name the outstanding human review as the blocker.\n\nIt may not report success while leaving the Task parked on the same human review. Add an end-to-end round-trip fixture for the W2-290 shape; asserting policy columns alone is insufficient. A completed review, especially `changes_requested`, is historical gate evidence and must never be rewritten by conversion.","rank":43,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"7e91c1b7-c877-4662-b1b9-54afd06eeecf","identifier":"LOO-52","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-52/a-changes-requested-review-permanently-kills-its-task-and-the-dead","name":"A changes-requested review permanently kills its Task, and the dead Task burns an agent turn per cycle forever","description":"Two defects COMPOUND here, and the second is the one that makes the first unsurvivable. Correction added 2026-07-17 after the Project reviewer caught my framing.\n\n== Defect A: changes_requested is terminal, so the gate can never open ==\n\nreview_gate (ops/task.rs:3836) blocks completion unless EVERY review whose own policy field == InteractionPolicy::Require is status=Completed AND disposition=Approved.\n_complete_interaction_review (store/sqlite/interaction_reviews.rs:449) makes the disposition WRITE-ONCE -- on an already-Completed review it returns 'interaction review already has a different completion' for any different disposition; only an identical (disposition, outcome) is an idempotent no-op.\nLive row: ir_5100a8fce1e64015b7e3d7e5b4ca9ecd | human | completed | changes_requested | require.\nA new approved review cannot rescue it, because the gate requires ALL required reviews approved and this one never can be.\n\n== Defect B: the gate is reviewer_kind=human in a headless fleet, so nobody was ever coming ==\n\nMeasured on the live store, fleet-wide:\n    SELECT reviewer_kind, status, COUNT(*) FROM interaction_reviews GROUP BY 1,2;\n    human   | active    | 8\n    human   | completed | 2\n    project | completed | 37\n    project | requested | 7\n\nEight ACTIVE human-kind reviews exist right now and no agent can dispose any of them -- 'lf task review message/complete' refuses inside a Task/Project/Wave session, which is #1034's shipped boundary and is CORRECT. Project-kind reviews are healthy (37 completed), so the machinery works; the reviewer KIND is the problem. A Task launched without 'lf task run <issue> --headless' mints human-kind gates, and --headless is write-once at INSERT and cannot be applied afterward (filed 5ce98bb9).\n\nSo: Defect B means a human gate exists that no agent can answer, and Defect A means that if a human ever does answer it with changes_requested, the Task is killed permanently. Either alone is survivable. Together they are a trap: the 8 human|active reviews are Tasks waiting for someone who is not coming, and any of them that gets changes_requested joins ENG-7 in being unopenable forever.\n\n== The burn: a dead gate does not park, it spins ==\n\nENG-7's ladder:\n  PR 1: merged     #1035  <- real work, on main as 5843a8cab\n  PR 2: abandoned  never opened, 0 commits vs main\n  PR 3: abandoned  never opened, 0 commits vs main\n\nCompletion is refused, so the runner re-enters iterate, mints an EMPTY serial PR, and runs a full agent body against it. I abandoned PR 2 using the exact exit the refusal recommends ('publish and merge it or run ') and that minted PR 3. Abandoning PR N mints PR N+1: abandon is the loop's feed, not its exit. Each cycle costs an agent turn and produces nothing. Severity is therefore not 'one Task stranded' but 'one Task burning an agent turn per cycle, indefinitely, per affected Task'.\n\nResolution for ENG-7 specifically: the Project reviewer decided to abandon the Task SESSION (decision cd_0104e6f0), since the work is merged and the Linear issue gets closed by hand -- the same reconciliation already used for W2-287 on #1034. That is the second instance in one night of merged work whose Session cannot record it, which is KR2 evidence ('No Task strands on a dead body'), not a workaround anyone is happy with.\n\nNote the store correction is NOT an available escalation: the running binary is release 0.11.3, dev builds are walled off from the production store, and #1032's reconciliation fix is not in it. I originally offered that as an option; it escalates to a capability nobody currently has.\n\n== Fix options (none chosen) ==\n\nFor A: make changes_requested non-terminal so the same review can be re-disposed once the fix lands (smallest); or scope review_gate to the CURRENT gate cycle rather than all history; or mint a fresh approvable gate when the Task re-enters Iterate and consult only the newest review per cycle.\nFor B: owned by 5ce98bb9 -- make the interaction policy convertible after INSERT, or refuse to mint a human-kind gate when no human surface is attached.\nFor the burn: a Task whose completion gate is permanently closed must park with a named blocker rather than mint another empty PR. Minting a serial PR should require actual follow-up work, not merely 'completion refused'.\nSeparately: arming a merge should be gated on outstanding dispositions, or a changes_requested disposition should disarm the queue entry -- otherwise merge and gate keep diverging, as they did when #1035 landed while the gate stayed changes_requested.\n\n== Interim guidance ==\n\nLaunch every Task with --headless. Under policy=require, request changes with a review MESSAGE/steer, never a changes_requested disposition, on any Task you intend to complete. Do not 'lf pr abandon' the empty successors of a gate-dead Task; abandon the SESSION.","rank":44,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"9af14990-e8bc-4a87-9a44-8e57637291f8","identifier":"LOO-53","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-53/migrate-legacy-ops-telemetry-without-dirtying-checkouts","name":"Migrate legacy ops telemetry without dirtying checkouts","description":"<!-- loopflow-task-start:d8e191bbb102d4c00109699194ee0dc7e306148cca637c4c44c74918795a43c6 -->","rank":45,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"66b1614b-f999-40b5-a98a-69376eda614a","identifier":"LOO-54","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-54/preflight-managed-provider-credentials-before-reserving-a-task-body","name":"Preflight managed provider credentials before reserving a Task body","description":"Observed on 2026-07-15 while recovering W2-235 from a Claude five-hour limit. `lf task resume W2-235 --model codex` reserved generation 2 and started Codex, then failed immediately because the managed Codex refresh token had been revoked. W2-240 also reserved a body before learning that every Claude account was cooling down until 09:00 PDT.\n\nUser-visible outcome\n\nTask dispatch chooses a credential that can start a turn before it reserves a generation or reports the Task running. Revoked refresh tokens and provider-wide cooldowns are detected at account-selection time, an available backup is selected deterministically, and no provider means one actionable blocked state rather than a burned body generation.\n\nProof\n\nCover a revoked Codex refresh token, an expired-but-refreshable token, all Claude accounts cooling down, and one healthy backup. Prove the unhealthy account is quarantined with a sanitized reconnect/status action, the healthy route starts exactly one generation, and an all-unavailable route stays resumable without provider churn. Never print or copy raw credentials.","rank":46,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"6bf90e67-c59f-43d0-a26f-2c9db04b6be9","identifier":"LOO-56","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-56/name-the-task-project-route-flag-for-what-it-reports","name":"Name the Task project-route flag for what it reports","description":"## Problem\n\n`lf task status --json` exposes `project_route_succeeded`, a boolean that is\n**false on the healthy path**. It does not mean \"routing works\" — it means\n\"this Task had to be rerouted to a successor Project Session.\"\n\n`resolve_task_project_route` (ops/project.rs:1249) returns `succeeded: false`\nwhenever the historical Project Session is **not terminal** — i.e. the normal\ncase, where the Session that launched the Task is alive and owns it. It returns\n`succeeded: true` only after the historical Session died and a live successor\nwas found. So the flag reads as failure precisely when nothing is wrong.\n\nShipped by #994 (W2-243, now closed); this is its read-surface follow-up, not a\ndefect in the routing itself, which is correct.\n\n## Evidence this costs real time\n\nOn 2026-07-17 the Developer Efficiency Project Session read\n`project_route_succeeded: false` on all six of its live Tasks (ENG-7, ENG-19,\nW2-280, W2-283, W2-287, W2-290) in the same iteration that delivered zero\nsupervised observations. The obvious reading — \"the supervision channel is\ndark, six bodies are unobservable\" — is wrong, and was one command away from\nbeing escalated as a fleet incident. Every one of those Tasks was routing\ncorrectly to the live parent (`routing_project_session_id` == the reading\nSession's own id). Only reading the source disproved it.\n\nThe human CLI is not exposed to this: `bin/lf.rs:671` prints \"routes to X\" when\n`succeeded`, prints \"no live successor\" when the target is `None`, and prints\n**nothing** in the healthy `succeeded: false` + `Some(target)` case. The trap\nexists only for `--json` consumers, which are agents. This Project's definition\ncounts that as equal friction.\n\n## Done when\n\n* No `--json` consumer can read a healthy route as a failure. Either the field\n  names what it actually reports (e.g. `rerouted`), or the route surfaces a\n  typed state (`owned_by_launcher` / `rerouted_to_successor` / `dead_chain`)\n  rather than a boolean that answers a different question than its name.\n* The healthy, rerouted, and dead-chain cases are distinguishable in `--json`\n  without reading Rust. Today the first two are distinguishable only by\n  correlating two fields, and the field that looks decisive is the misleading one.\n* A test pins the healthy case's wire value. Sabotage it: flip the healthy-path\n  return and confirm the test goes red — a test that asserts the field merely\n  *exists* passes trivially under either spelling.\n\n## Bounds\n\nDo not change routing behavior. #994's resolution logic is correct and is\ncarrying live traffic; this is a naming/shape fix on the read surface only.","rank":47,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"7261cb2e-1534-402b-8974-3e1e13eb1a87","identifier":"LOO-58","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-58/count-ci-autonomy-only-when-a-ci-fix-command-owns-the-repair","name":"Count CI autonomy only when a ci-fix command owns the repair","description":"lf ci currently counts any green or merged incident with human_assisted=false as autonomous. PR #1034 proves this is false: its normal Task body fixed scratch-clear, trigger_command_id is null, yet summary.autonomous became 1. Make autonomy attributable: count a green/merged incident as autonomous only when it has a durable ci-fix trigger (and remains not human-assisted). Add a focused summary test that #1034-shaped data is not autonomous and a triggered green incident is. Keep the DTO and schema unchanged; this is a predicate correction, not a new field.","rank":48,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"d568a7a4-b26b-4c4d-820e-c19e33bd7b79","identifier":"LOO-59","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-59/keep-ci-fix-subflow-playheads-out-of-the-durable-task-cursor","name":"Keep ci-fix subflow playheads out of the durable Task cursor","description":"## Problem\n\nAutomatic failed-PR repair can select the `ci-fix` subflow and start a body, then fail before repair because generic `record_task_flow_position` persists the transient `ci-fix` playhead into the durable parent Task cursor. The validator compares that playhead with the configured lifecycle flow and rejects it.\n\nThis is a live cross-phase defect, not a hypothetical Gate-only regression guard:\n\n* W2-280 generation 5 and W2-298 generation 3 originated in Gate. Their durable flow was `task-gate`; the transient playhead was `ci-fix`.\n* W2-303 generation 4 originated in Iterate. Its durable flow was `task`; the transient playhead was `ci-fix`.\n\nAll three selected and started the repair wake, then failed at the same cursor write before the repair could execute. This disproves the previous note that Gate-origin behavior was correct by construction.\n\n## Boundary\n\nTreat parent lifecycle cursor state and transient subflow state as separate authorities. When `ci_fix_wake` is armed:\n\n* run the bounded `ci-fix` flow without recording its transient playhead as the durable `task` or `task-gate` cursor;\n* preserve normal turn completion and Session reconciliation;\n* let `settle_ci_fix_turn` park the Task and accept the durable wake command;\n* preserve lifecycle phase, phase cursor, gate cycle, and gate proposal unless normal settlement independently changes them;\n* never mint a second body for the same failure set.\n\nKeep one Task cursor model. Do not add a parallel durable ci-fix cursor or a compatibility shim.\n\n## Done when\n\n* A deterministic Iterate fixture reproduces the W2-303 shape and completes one bounded ci-fix turn without `iterate flow is task, but its playhead is ci-fix`.\n* A deterministic Gate fixture reproduces the W2-280/W2-298 shape and completes one bounded ci-fix turn without `gate flow is task-gate, but its playhead is ci-fix`.\n* Both fixtures prove the parent cursor remains valid, lifecycle and gate state are not accidentally advanced, the Task parks after settlement, and the durable command is accepted exactly once.\n* Sabotage that routes the transient playhead back through generic parent-flow persistence turns the fixtures red.\n* An unchanged failed wake is never repaired by Resume; the fix is proven through a fresh deterministic lifecycle.\n\n## Not in scope\n\n* Failed-head trigger, deduplication, or attribution; ENG-20 and W2-293/W2-299 own those.\n* Waiting for green CI before review; W2-303 owns that policy.\n* Proven-empty successor settlement; W2-304 owns it.\n* Revoked body-lease release; ENG-4 owns it.","rank":49,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"76f42191-8cbd-4b78-85af-a4abe0401514","identifier":"LOO-60","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-60/loopflow-has-no-stable-machine-identity-gethostname-is-not-one","name":"Loopflow has no stable machine identity; gethostname is not one","description":"Filed out of W2-283's design review (do not absorb into W2-283).\n\n## What is true today\n\n`HostId::local()` is `gethostname()` and nothing more (`profile.rs:81`) — no stable machine id, no persistence. Renaming a Mac silently changes the identity of the machine as far as loopflow is concerned. It cannot distinguish 'this store was restored onto a different machine' from 'this machine was renamed', and any guard built on it refuses identically in both cases.\n\n## What W2-283 does to it\n\nW2-283 PR 2 **deletes** `HostId` **outright**. All 22 of its references were Chrome-binding-related (`chrome_profile_bindings`, keyed `(profile_id, host_id)`), and that table is dropped when the account -> ordered-venues inversion lands. The review specifically rejected keying the new default route on `gethostname`, because a rename would then silently drop the machine default and send every unrouted repo back to ambient credentials.\n\nSo after W2-283, the instability has **no load-bearing consumer**. The only remaining `gethostname` caller is `wave/playhead.rs:140`, which stamps a descriptive `host` label onto a body record; grep shows nothing reads it as a key. A renamed Mac just labels new bodies differently. Nothing resolves through it.\n\n## Why this is still worth a task\n\nThis is a **capability gap, not a live bug** — and it should be closed before something needs it, not after.\n\nThe infrastructure wave's own roadmap has 'Cron host bootstrap — bring up the first maintained `lf cron` host (Mac mini default)' and 'Replicate intentionally'. The moment a second host exists, something will want to say 'this row belongs to that machine' — host-scoped credential homes, per-host cron ownership, per-host health. The next person to reach for that will reach for `gethostname`, because it is what is there, and they will rebuild the same coin-flip guard W2-283 just deleted.\n\n## Done when\n\n* Loopflow has a persisted, stable machine identity, minted once and stored (not derived from a hostname), surviving a rename.\n* `gethostname` is either gone or demonstrably confined to human-readable labels, with the distinction stated where it is used.\n* A test proves a renamed host keeps its identity.\n\n## Do not\n\nDo not reintroduce host scoping speculatively along with it. W2-283 established that the store is host-local — the one cross-host path (ssh forwarding) leases a throwaway `router.db` from `mktemp -d`, deleted on exit, so no account row ever reaches another host's durable store. A stable machine id is worth having; a shared store is not proven to exist, and scoping rows to hosts should wait for the second host that actually needs it.","rank":50,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"dd837933-019c-419a-900e-e98718f3f75f","identifier":"LOO-61","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-61/route-existing-task-sessions-to-the-successor-project-session","name":"Route existing Task Sessions to the successor Project Session","description":"<!-- loopflow-task-start:df239ae0213ce869042fe588cc996f6e76888c51238d8b3d6c51521a82460f39 -->","rank":51,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"219c195d-bd2c-4188-bf16-c4c77a8e5bee","identifier":"LOO-63","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-63/retire-the-parallel-spend-recorders-one-per-turn-store-answers-every","name":"Retire the parallel spend recorders: one per-turn store answers every usage question","description":"PR #1022 unified the *grain* (per-boundary deltas everywhere, one aggregation rule, subscription-first lf usage) but deliberately deleted no store: coverage was asymmetric, so removing any recorder lost data. This task follows the merger to its conclusion — one store records token spend, everything else derives.\n\nCurrent state (measured 2026-07-16):\n\n* run_events carries five usage columns (input/output/cache_read/cost/duration + provider/model) with complete coverage: every provider, every engine run.\n* agent_turns carries the richest per-turn evidence (reasoning, cache writes, context pressure, prompt paths) but misses opencode entirely, and task/project session runners drop ConversationEvent::TurnUsage on the floor (task/runner.rs, project_session/runner.rs) so their spend reaches agent_turns via capture only, never the ledger.\n* The wave journal narrates per-turn usage; it is a log, not a query source, and stays.\n\nEnd state, as proof a maintainer can check:\n\n1. Every agent-backed spend writer produces agent_turns rows: session runners record turn usage; opencode launches capture turns.\n2. run_events has no token/cost columns; journal's PendingUsage thread-local and the drain-at-boundary machinery are deleted; a migration drops the columns after verifying the turn-derived totals match the ledger totals on the live store.\n3. lf usage, lf top, and lf trace answer from agent_turns ⋈ agent_launches; lf runs is unchanged; lf doctor's uncaptured-spend check inverts into a coverage check on turns.\n4. lf usage output before and after the cut is identical on a migrated copy of a production ledger (same totals, same rows), and the Mac dashboard still reconciles with lf usage.\n5. Net LOC is negative.\n\nDecision recorded, not reopened: agent_turns wins as the surviving store (natural per-turn grain, richest fields); run_events remains the process/lineage spine without spend columns.","rank":52,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"7ac28a8b-fcf9-432a-8eb1-634a0d547cf8","identifier":"LOO-64","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-64/stop-restoring-xcode-module-caches-across-sdk-changes","name":"Stop restoring Xcode module caches across SDK changes","description":"Observed on PR #1026, workflow run 29543428054, job 87770345162: loopflow-ui-test restored swift/DerivedData, then SwiftShims failed because the macOS SDK module.modulemap mtime changed since the cached PCM was built. The same commit passed minutes earlier and contains no Swift changes. Make the cache boundary deterministic: exclude compiler module caches or key the cache to the exact Xcode/SDK identity, preferring the smallest reliable cache. Do not add retries. Prove a stale SDK module cache cannot make an unrelated PR red.","rank":53,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"d5f732f1-2227-4278-b38f-88dd33574346","identifier":"LOO-65","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-65/reconcile-a-reopened-task-pr-before-rotating","name":"Reconcile a reopened Task PR before rotating","description":"Observed on ENG-20 after GitHub PR #1026 was reopened at the same head: the local PR row retained abandoned_at, lf ci omitted the live failure, Task resume created empty serial branch sequence 2 at main, and gate requested a demo with identical base/head. Reconciliation must treat authoritative GitHub open state as reopening the same Task PR when identity/head still match, or explicitly stop for an operator; it must never silently rotate to an empty successor while the published PR is open. Prove a reopened red PR remains active, is visible to ci incident detection, and can receive its bounded ci-fix wake without creating a second branch.","rank":54,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"81f28db6-795e-4cac-9f62-9b5f5385d4ec","identifier":"LOO-66","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-66/recommend-only-executable-task-actions-after-merge","name":"Recommend only executable Task actions after merge","description":"## Observed 2026-07-17 across three merged Tasks.\\n\\nW2-285 merged PR #1037, had no active PR, and status recommended resume. A next-turn command was accepted, generation 3 started, and the body failed with \"Task Session ... has no active PR\". W2-286 merged PR #1032 and remains parked with the same impossible resume recommendation. Running complete gives the real outstanding gates instead: W2-285 has a completed-but-unapproved Project review; W2-286 has directive v2 unincorporated. Status instead says \"implementation not finished\". W2-284 is the control: its merged PR completed normally while its body was alive.\\n\\nThe defect is one action model producing mutually inconsistent claims. Status availability, next-owner recommendation, process-start preconditions, and command refusals do not share an evaluator. A supervisor can therefore follow a recommended action that the runner accepts and only rejects after spawning a doomed body.\\n\\nDone when:\\n\\n- Task status recommends only actions that can pass the runner's preconditions.\\n- A merged Task with no active PR never starts a resume body merely to discover that no PR exists.\\n- The next move names the real outstanding gate and owner: Project review, directive incorporation, or completion.\\n- Status and the corresponding command share refusal reasons; \"implementation not finished\" is never emitted for a merged implementation.\\n- Recover liveness agrees with the recorded body outcome.\\n- Regression fixtures cover W2-285's review gate, W2-286's directive gate, and W2-284's successful merged completion.\\n\\nDo not bypass completion gates. Make the action projection truthful and route the existing gate to its actual owner.\n\n## Live fixtures (added by the parent Project, 2026-07-17 01:45Z)\n\nDelivered here rather than as a directive or a review message: v2 is already\nincorporated and must not be superseded, and the kickoff gate is a `human`\nreview the parent is correctly forbidden from answering (#1034). Nothing below\nchanges the design; it supplies fixtures whose expected values are fixed by\nGitHub rather than generated by the code under test.\n\n### W2-287 — every evaluator disagrees with the ledger row it reads\n\nIts stored PR row (`pr_9d6a35ded82f4c1d97c0b9ac6dbad0df`):\n\n```\nmerge_commit: 34bc6af890571f33f49d212e860bad66f7f8abd7\nafter_merge:  \"complete_task\"\nabandoned_at: null\n```\n\nGitHub agrees: #1034 `state=closed merged=true merged_at=2026-07-17T01:31:35Z`,\nsha on origin/main. Against that row:\n\n```\ncomplete      -> false, \"implementation not finished\"    <-- the hardcoded literal, verbatim, on merged work\nstart_next_pr -> false, \"no merged PR to advance from\"   <-- merge_commit is populated in the same struct\nrecover       -> false, \"body is not dead; the session is parked\"\nresume        -> AVAILABLE, \"resume the parked session\"  <-- recommended, and it is the action that just killed it\n```\n\nwith `status=failed`, `process_alive=false`, lease finished `outcome=failed`.\nStill true 12 minutes after the merge. Because the fixture's expected values are\nexternally fixed (merged=true, sha 34bc6af89), a test built on it cannot pass by\ngenerating both sides — which is the failure mode the v2 summary flags for the\nagreement test.\n\n### The merge kills the live body; the recommendation is the second victim\n\nW2-287's body was **running** and died at ~01:31 with \"Task Session ... has no\nactive PR\", within seconds of the 01:31:35Z merge. ENG-19 shows the identical\nshape at its own merge (#1018 at 01:27, same failure string). So\n`PrPhase::is_active()` excluding `Merged` is not only a read-surface defect\nfeeding `derive_task_actions` — the same exclusion reaches a running body's\nprecondition and terminates it. The title (\"Recommend only executable Task\nactions after merge\") may be scoped narrower than the defect. Decide explicitly\nwhether the planned shared `resume_refusal` also covers the in-body path at\n[runner.rs:1019](<http://runner.rs:1019>), or whether that is a separate blocker.\n\n### Divergence worth explaining, not just fixing\n\nENG-19 failed with \"has no active PR\" and then **completed** (\"pull request\n#1018 merged and completed the Task\"). W2-287 — whose row literally says\n`after_merge=complete_task` — never completed and still recommends resume. Both\nhad merged PRs and identical failure strings. The visible difference is\n`lifecycle_phase`: ENG-19 in `iterate`, W2-287 in `kickoff`. If the\nmerge->complete path is phase-scoped the way the v2 summary found the\nreview-gate query to be phase-scoped (ir_d191 at an older coordinate), that is\nthe same bug class twice in one lifecycle. The parent reconciled W2-287's Linear\nissue by hand; the Session remains `failed` holding merged work.\n\n### Liveness control — do not read W2-284 as a strand\n\n## W2-284's generation-3 lease went `revoked -> finished` with `outcome=lost` on a\n\nTask that had **already completed** via merged #1038. So `lost` marks completed\nwork too. Any liveness predicate (Failed|Lost|LegacyStopped) must not read\nW2-284's lost lease as a recoverable strand.\n\n## Parent review findings for ir_6cf5168b — RECORDED, NOT DISPOSED. **DO NOT COMPLETE AS CHANGES-REQUESTED**\n\n> ### CORRECTION (2026-07-17, parent Project) — READ BEFORE DISPOSING\n>\n> An earlier revision of this section said \"Disposition would be:\n> changes-requested\". **That was wrong and would permanently wedge this Task.**\n> Retracted.\n>\n> `ir_6cf5168b` is `policy=require`. Two facts, both verified in the tree:\n>\n> * `required_reviews_for_task` (ops/task.rs:3816) filters **only** on\n>   `task_session_id` and `policy == Require` — no phase, epoch, cycle, or\n>   recency scoping. Every require-policy review ever created for this session\n>   must be `Approved`, forever.\n> * `_complete_interaction_review` (store/sqlite/interaction_reviews.rs:449)\n>   returns `InvalidData(\"interaction review already has a different completion\")` for any completion that differs from the recorded one. A\n>   `changes_requested` review can therefore **never** become `Approved`, and a\n>   new approved review does not help because the gate requires *all* required\n>   reviews approved.\n>\n> So completing this gate as changes-requested bars W2-290 from ever completing\n> — the exact wedge ENG-7 is in right now (its `ir_5100a8fce1e6` is\n> `human|completed|changes_requested|require`; its work merged as #1035 and it\n> burned an agent turn per cycle minting empty serial PRs until the parent told\n> it to abandon). Filed as `7e91c1b7`. Until that lands, **changes-requested is****\n> ****terminal on a require-policy review.**\n>\n> **Do this instead.** The three findings below are test-boundary and scope\n> constraints, not design flaws — the approach is sound and I recorded what is\n> approved as-is. Either:\n>\n> 1. **Preferred** — send the findings to the Task first while the review is\n>    still `active`: `lf task review message ir_6cf5168b049c4731a55e7939bfa6252e \"<findings 1-3>\"`, let it update the design doc, then complete\n>    `--disposition approved`. The findings land *and* the Task stays alive.\n> 2. Or complete `--disposition approved --outcome \"<findings 1-3 verbatim, as binding implementation constraints>\"`. Approval is not a waiver: the\n>    findings are durable in this task body and in the outcome text.\n>\n> Approving does not weaken Finding 1 — it must still be satisfied before the\n> implementation PR merges. It just refuses to trade a permanent wedge for a\n> disposition label.\n\nThe Wave directed the parent Project to conduct this gate. **The parent cannot****\n****disposition it**:* `ir_6cf5168b049c4731a55e7939bfa6252e` *is* `reviewer_kind=human`\n*(the sole review row for this session), and both* `lf task review message` *and*\n`lf task review complete` *refuse with \"human review commands cannot run inside*\n**a Task, Project, or Wave agent session\"* — #1034's authority boundary, working as\ndesigned. The findings are recorded here so the human who disposes the gate can\napply them verbatim. **Disposition: approved**, with Findings 1-3 as binding implementation\nconstraints — see the correction above for why not changes-requested.\n\n### Finding 1 — BLOCKING. The abandoned shape does not prove the preflight\n\nThe design already argues the defect is *settled*-specific, not merge-specific\n(\"Problem\", W2-283 paragraph: `is_active()` excludes `Abandoned` too). The\n**done-when does not test that claim where it matters.**\n\n* Done-when 3 (\"No doomed generation\") is scoped to *\"*`lf task resume` *on a**\n  ****merged** Task with no active PR\"*. Merged-only.\n* Done-when 4's W2-283 line asserts only *\"never recommends* `resume`*\"* — a\n  **model projection** assertion. It never exercises `resume_task_async`.\n\nSo the shared `resume_refusal` preflight is proven for the merged shape only.\nFor the abandoned shape, nothing requires `resume_task_async` to refuse **before**\n`resume_session` **mints a generation**. Since §4 deliberately demotes\n`runner.rs:1043`'s `bail!` to an \"unreachable\" defensive invariant, a regression\nthat lets the abandoned shape past the preflight would be caught *only* by that\n`bail!` — i.e. after the body exists. That is precisely the defect this Task\nexists to remove, surviving in the one shape the tests do not cover.\n\n**Required:** W2-283 becomes an abandoned-PR **regression fixture for the shared****\n****preflight**, not only for the projection. With `active_pr` absent and the last PR\n`Abandoned` (not merged), assert both:\n\n1. `resume_task_async` refuses with **no** `ChildProcessGeneration` **row reserved**\n   (assert the generation count is unchanged across the refused command, as\n   done-when 3 already does for merged), and\n2. `lf task status`'s `resume` reason and that refusal are the **same value**.\n\nSabotage it the way the design sabotages the rest: make `resume_refusal` return\n`None` for the abandoned arm specifically and confirm this fixture goes red. The\nexisting \"`resume_refusal` returns `None` always\" sabotage does not prove it —\nthe merged fixture would catch that first and mask an abandoned-only regression.\n\n### Finding 2 — BLOCKING. Name the two exclusions the fixture drags in\n\nOut-of-scope names \"reopening/rotating PRs, or any durable PR mutation\n(W2-286/#1032's territory)\". That does not name the two things a W2-283 fixture\nactually walks into, because W2-283 is both the fixture *and* a Task whose live\nstate is a split-brain:\n\n* **#1039/#1040 identity reconciliation** — W2-283's ledger PR and the PR holding\n  its work are permanently different objects (#1039 auto-closed, #1040\n  unregistered). Explicitly out of scope. Build the fixture from a constructed\n  abandoned-PR state, not from reconciling W2-283's real ledger.\n* **The scratch-only publish classifier** — the cause of that split-brain, and\n  unrelated to action derivation. Explicitly out of scope.\n\nBoth belong to other owners. Name them so an implementer who reads W2-283's real\nstate does not adopt its incident.\n\n### Finding 3 — Sharpening, not blocking. The agreement test\n\nThe design is already alert here (\"The agreement test is the one most at risk of\npassing for free... asserting `x == x` unless the two renderers are genuinely\nseparate call sites reading one value\"), and done-when 1 compares\n`model.status(a).reason` against *\"the error text* `task_complete` */*\n`resume_task_async` *actually produces\"* — two distinct call sites, which is the\nright shape. Two constraints to record explicitly:\n\n* The command-side string must be obtained by **invoking the command entry****\n  ****point**, not by re-rendering `CompletionEvidence::refusal` a second time in the\n  test. A test that builds the evidence once and calls `.refusal()` twice is two\n  calls into the same evaluator and passes unconditionally — that is not an\n  agreement test, and it must not be accepted.\n* Sabotage row 1 perturbs the **model** side only. The sabotage must show\n  **either** projection can diverge: also perturb the command side (make\n  `task_complete` format its own refusal) and confirm the same test goes red.\n  One-directional sabotage leaves the command-side renderer unpinned.\n\n### Approved as-is (recorded so implementation does not relitigate)\n\n* Keeping **both** review queries (phase-scoped for \"answer now\", required-set\n  for Complete). Collapsing them would drop Defer-policy in-flight gates — the\n  design's reasoning is correct and the \"verify then prune\" instinct cuts the\n  other way here.\n* `latest_settled_pr` routing merged PRs to `merged_pr_model`, and rewriting the\n  dead `Some(Abandoned)` arm rather than re-feeding it.\n* Liveness from recorded outcome with `Completed|Superseded|Interrupted` as\n  settlements. **W2-284 is the binding control** — its generation-3 lease is\n  `outcome=lost` on an already-completed Task, so `Lost` must not read as a\n  recoverable strand there.\n* Q1: recommend `no_action` after an abandon, leaving `start_next_pr` available.\n  Rotation is a durable mutation; the conservative reading is right.\n* No auto-merge (`lf pr publish`, never `lf pr land`).\n\n## Additional event-order fixtures (2026-07-17)\n\nThese two Tasks are terminal now; do not resume or reconcile them. Preserve the event order because the final projection alone hides the defect.\n\n* W2-299 completed after verifying merged PR #1045 and incorporating directive v2. Closing duplicate PR #1047 then emitted `completed -> waiting` with reason `pull request #1047 closed without merge`. A later reconciliation restored `completed` / `no_action`. Terminal completion must dominate a later duplicate-PR closure; the intermediate waiting/resume projection is invalid even if another pass repairs it.\n* W2-296 completed after verifying merged PR #1046 and incorporating directive v4. Closing duplicate PR #1044 produced the same terminal-status overwrite before reconciliation restored `completed` / `no_action`. The Task had no active PR and no remaining directive work.\n\nRequired regression: drive the real evidence builder through completion followed by abandonment/closure of a duplicate published PR. Assert the Session never leaves terminal status, no resume action becomes available, and no new body generation can be reserved. Test the transition, not only the eventual corrected snapshot.","rank":55,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"e4721eab-4b49-4cd0-8eb5-72aadc8095f1","identifier":"LOO-67","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-67/let-top-level-task-review-commands-pass-authority","name":"Let top-level task review commands pass authority","description":"<!-- loopflow-task-start:491dbadbc72423440e01d028ff16b45c4b34495fca703564c38776e7741fdbb2 -->","rank":56,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"18c805e2-bf9e-4d87-af42-0d3f3c67a26a","identifier":"LOO-68","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-68/initialize-an-existing-but-unmigrated-database-instead-of-wedging-on","name":"Initialize an existing but unmigrated database instead of wedging on it","description":"PR #1030 (store: skip no-op migration transactions, 1e064a47b) fixed the ENG-7 contention incident by consulting requires_migration_sqlite before opening the migration transaction. That gate conflates two different databases: a schema that is current, and a database that was never initialized. requires_migration_sqlite returns Ok(false) for BOTH -- it answers Ok(false) when there are no user tables at all, and when schema_migrations is empty and is the only table.\n\nSqliteStore::new picks apply_sqlite (initialize) vs apply_sqlite_with_backup (migrate) on `existing_database`, which only means 'the file has bytes'. So a database file with a header but no tables now takes the migrate path, the gate says 'nothing pending', and the schema is never created. Every subsequent open fails permanently with 'no such table: run_events'. Before #1030 the same open fell through to apply_set and created the schema.\n\nReachability: a process killed mid-migration leaves exactly this state -- BEGIN EXCLUSIVE rolls back, the file keeps its header, the tables are gone. That is not hypothetical under the ~51-process fleet this incident came from. The result is a permanent wedge whose only recovery is a manual 'rm loopflow.db' -- an avoidable human-in-the-loop repair step, which is this Project's first KR.\n\nVerified by bisection, not inferred: this test passes on 1e064a47b^ and fails on 1e064a47b with 'no such table: run_events'.\n\n```\n#[test]\nfn an_existing_but_empty_database_still_gets_its_schema() {\n    let dir = tempfile::tempdir().unwrap();\n    let path = dir.path().join(\"loopflow.db\");\n    let conn = rusqlite::Connection::open(&path).unwrap();\n    conn.execute_batch(\"PRAGMA user_version = 1;\").unwrap();  // header, no tables\n    drop(conn);\n    assert!(std::fs::metadata(&path).unwrap().len() > 0);\n\n    loopflow::store::sqlite::SqliteStore::new(&path)\n        .expect(\"an existing but unmigrated database must still get its schema\");\n}\n```\n\nFix shape (deliberately not chosen here): the gate must skip only when the schema is provably current, never merely when it fails to prove it stale -- or 'existing_database' must ask whether the database is initialized rather than whether the file has bytes. Either way the predicate is the bug, and requires_migration_sqlite's second caller (backup_before_migration) shares it, so change its meaning with care.\n\nFound while verifying that #1030 owns the ENG-7 incident. Filed rather than absorbed into ENG-7: different code path, different defect.","rank":57,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"0134332f-6343-4cb2-a8d8-68637f6852dc","identifier":"LOO-69","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-69/preserve-queued-task-direction-when-interrupting-an-active-body","name":"Preserve queued Task direction when interrupting an active body","description":"## Observed on 2026-07-16\\n\\nENG-19 generation 12 was alive on a stale task_mutate turn while replacement directive v8 was durably persisted and explicitly required the current rewrite to stop. The v8 command did not reach applied within a bounded 30-second receipt wait.\\n\\nRunning the supported Task interrupt command persisted a control-only interrupt, immediately changed the queued v8 receipt to Superseded, and left generation 12 alive. The stop command had erased the instruction that explained what the next body must do. A subsequent Task steer restored the same semantic content, but that replacement also remained Persisted after a bounded receipt wait.\\n\\nThis is distinct from ENG-3, which owns an interrupt landing after the process is already dead, and from completed W2-172, which covered replacement direction racing Task completion.\\n\\n## Done when\\n\\n- Interrupting an active Task body cannot supersede its queued current replacement direction.\\n- A control-only interrupt and a semantic direction have distinct durable identities and transitions; stopping a body preserves the direction the successor must consume.\\n- Task interrupt and Task steer reach a truthful applied or failed receipt within a bounded interval while the Task harness is alive.\\n- Tests reproduce the ENG-19 shape: active generation, persisted replacement, interrupt, successor generation, and exactly-once incorporation of the replacement.\\n- Status names the real owner when a live harness has not consumed a control command instead of indefinitely reporting the body as working.","rank":58,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"92e0f253-f551-47d8-b74d-21a37e5dd551","identifier":"LOO-70","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-70/subsumed-by-loo-45-retire-the-parallel-usage-parsers","name":"[subsumed by LOO-45] Retire the parallel usage parsers","description":"Found while designing W2-280 (2026-07-16), which retires the parallel spend *store* and deliberately leaves the parallel *parsers*. That is the deeper duplication and the root cause of W2-280's finding #3.\n\nTwo parser stacks both feed [trace.rs](<http://trace.rs>) under conflicting semantics, verified in the tree on 2026-07-17:\n\n* engine/stream.rs emits StreamEvent::Usage; [trace.rs:1260](<http://trace.rs:1260>) ACCUMULATES it (self.usage.input_tokens += ...).\n* harness/*_mapping.rs emits ConversationEvent::TurnUsage; [trace.rs:1298](<http://trace.rs:1298>) CLOBBERS with it (self.usage = usage.clone()).\n\nOne consumer, two producers, opposite rules. That is what let opencode's real parsed usage be overwritten with zeros: opencode_mapping::complete_turn unconditionally pushes TurnUsage { usage: usage.unwrap_or_default() }, and the clone semantics faithfully cloned the default over the accumulated truth. W2-280 fixes that one source and keeps the clone honest, which is the right narrow fix -- but it leaves the shape that makes the next mapping gap silent in exactly the same way.\n\nDo not start this before W2-280 lands both PRs; it would collide with its readers and its migration. Scope it as a shape question, not a bug hunt: decide whether one stack subsumes the other or whether both are needed at different layers, and if both survive, make the conflicting semantics impossible to express rather than merely correct today.\n\nDone when one parse path owns usage end-to-end, [trace.rs](<http://trace.rs>) consumes a single producer under a single documented rule, a provider that reports no usage is representable distinctly from a provider that reports zero, and a conformance test per provider asserts concrete token values rather than event shape.","rank":59,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"4843ade1-d927-4273-b84f-5d3ffc9bfb6b","identifier":"LOO-71","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-71/resolve-task-completion-state-from-the-issues-owning-linear-team","name":"Resolve Task completion state from the issue's owning Linear team","description":"## Observed after PR #1011 merged on 2026-07-16. Running lf task complete ENG-12 correctly completed the local Session, but Linear writeback stayed pending with this error: Discrepancy between issue team and state, cycle or project. A dry-run of lf pm reteam --wave infrastructure proves the wave creation binding is the legacy W2 team while current ENG issues belong to the ENG team; applying that plan would renumber 20 open tasks and is not the repair. Existing-item updates are documented as team-agnostic, but LinearClient complete_item and reopen_item call resolve_team_id from the wave binding before choosing a workflow state. Done when completion and reopen resolve the target issue owning team, select a state from that team, preserve creation-team behavior for new issues, retry the ENG-12 pending writeback successfully, and tests cover a wave binding that differs from an existing issue team without bulk reteam or identifier changes.\n\n## Live reproduction, 2026-07-17 (parent Project)\n\n`lf pm task done` **fails to close any** `ENG-*` **issue** in this Project, with:\n\n```\n$ lf pm task done --id 25178a6b-4979-4e1b-b08a-dd0790c05d24 \\\n    --pr https://github.com/loopflowstudio/loopflow/pull/1035\ncommenting PR link on linear task 25178a6b-4979-4e1b-b08a-dd0790c05d24\nError: Discrepancy between issue team and state, cycle or project.\n```\n\nNote the partial failure: **the PR-link comment succeeds and only the close****\n****fails**, so the issue is left open carrying a comment that says it shipped.\n\n### Cause, confirmed by the split\n\nDeveloper Efficiency spans **two** Linear teams:\n\n```\nteam_ids: ['08c8d501-791d-4db0-a14c-15599d365955',   # ENG-*\n           '60558c53-2169-49f8-a76a-1f4586705aa9']   # W2-*\n```\n\n`wave/infrastructure/GOAL.md` frontmatter pins exactly one:\n`pm.linear_team: 60558c53-2169-49f8-a76a-1f4586705aa9`. The workflow state is\nresolved from the **wave's configured team**, not from the issue's own owning\nteam, so the state id handed to Linear belongs to a team the `ENG-*` issue is\nnot in, and Linear rejects the mutation.\n\nEvidence from three closes in one hour, same command, same session:\n\n| Issue | Team | `lf pm task done` |\n| -- | -- | -- |\n| W2-243 | 60558c53 (wave's) | closed OK, PR #994 linked |\n| W2-287 | 60558c53 (wave's) | closed OK, PR #1034 linked |\n| ENG-7 | 08c8d501 | **rejected** — PR #1035 comment landed, close refused |\n\nThe Project's own `team_ids` array already carries the correct team per issue,\nso the fix reads the owning team from the issue rather than from wave config.\n\n### Why it costs more than a stale row\n\nBoth closes above were manual reconciliations of Sessions that shipped merged\nwork and could not record it. ENG-7 is the sharper case: its work merged as\n#1035 (`5843a8cab`), its completion gate can never open (`7e91c1b7`), and the\nparent abandoned the Session to stop a per-cycle agent burn — so the Linear\nissue is now the *only* place its state can be made true, and that is the one\nwrite this bug blocks. The issue stays open indefinitely with no owner and no\nprocess that will retry it.\n\n## Done when\n\n* `lf pm task done` closes an issue in **any** team the Project spans, resolving\n  the workflow state from the issue's owning team rather than `pm.linear_team`.\n* A close that cannot complete does not leave a \"shipped\" comment behind — the\n  comment and the state transition succeed or fail together, or the comment\n  follows the state.\n* Regression: close one `ENG-*` and one `W2-*` issue in this Project. Sabotage\n  by forcing the wave-config team and confirm the `ENG-*` case goes red — a test\n  that only closes a `W2-*` issue passes today with the bug fully present.","rank":60,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"750bcd02-50c9-40e6-bea8-e4fc15c16a58","identifier":"LOO-72","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-72/make-task-command-caller-authority-explicit-across-wave-project-and","name":"Make Task command caller authority explicit across Wave, Project, and operator paths","description":"## Problem\n\nTask command authority currently changes with ambient process environment. During ENG-19 recovery, the same lf task resume spelling was refused as a Wave supervisor command while LF_WAVE_ID was present, then accepted as a Human or operator command when only that variable was removed. This made an environment edit cross the open-PR control boundary.\n\nENG-12 and PR #1011 shipped truthful lifecycle actions, but that Task is terminal and does not own this newly observed caller-identity gap.\n\n## Design constraint\n\nEnvironment configures a process; it must not decide what the process or caller is. Start with a scratch design before changing the worker or Wave runtime.\n\n## Done when\n\n* Task command authority is an explicit typed input from the invocation surface, not inferred from mutable ambient LF_WAVE_ID or LF_PROJECT_SESSION_ID presence.\n* Wave, Project, and operator commands derive legal actions and refusals from the same authority model.\n* A supervisor blocked by the open-PR bar is told the real next owner or given an explicit typed recovery path; repeating the same command cannot self-loop.\n* Removing an environment variable cannot convert a Wave or Project command into a Human command.\n* Tests cover Wave, Project, and operator resume against an open-PR Task, including the ENG-19 stranded-body shape.\n\n## Sequencing\n\nFiled from the infrastructure Wave on 2026-07-16. Do not launch while ENG-18, ENG-19, and ENG-20 own the active delivery queue; start after one reaches a terminal or external gate.","rank":61,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"9d7c2023-a0a7-4248-8f34-ac31772a7f97","identifier":"LOO-74","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-74/accounts-are-the-first-order-primitive-profiles-shrink-to-login","name":"Accounts are the first-order primitive; profiles shrink to login ceremony","description":"Proposed by Jack 2026-07-16 after a day of live account operations. Guiding constraint, his words: enforce simplicity as long as we make no mistakes or incorrect assumptions.\n\nThe model, stated precisely:\n\n* An ACCOUNT is the provider credential/subscription. It spends. It owns the credential home, provider-reported plan, rate-limit windows, health/cooldown, login_email. Accounts are more fundamental than profiles.\n* A PROFILE is a Chrome browser profile — a venue where you EXPECT an account's identity to be signed in. The expectation is not an identity: the venue's actual login can drift, so every ceremony verifies who is signed in and refuses on mismatch (the existing Chrome-login checks are the pattern; nothing may assume).\n* OWNERSHIP DIRECTION (the inversion): per provider, a list of accounts; each account carries an ordered list of profiles usable to gain access to it. Today's store owns the opposite arrow — profiles own per-provider account mappings — and while both directions can be represented, account → profiles is primary. Profiles have no run-time role; they are consulted only at auth ceremonies, in order, verified.\n\nTarget mechanics:\n\n* Repo routing: repo → per-provider ordered account list, replacing repo → profile-order → per-provider mapping. What you order is what spends. Asymmetric fleets (2 claude, 4 codex today) stop pretending to be symmetric bundles.\n* Machine-wide default route: one host-level per-provider account order applying when a repo has no route, so unrouted repos fail over across managed accounts instead of silently using ambient credentials.\n* Failover: ordered candidates + observed health (cooldowns, limit windows) select the account; a window near its cap demotes an account before the provider refuses. lf --account (PR #1029) is the explicit escape hatch above all of it; lf auth exec (PR #1027) is the same principle for interactive vendor use.\n* Ceremonies: lf auth connect <provider> <account> tries the account's access profiles in order — open venue, verify signed-in identity, proceed or fall through; exhausted list = actionable error, never a silent wrong-identity login.\n\nWhere today's profile-shaped state goes:\n\n1. ProfileProviderAccount (profile owns accounts) → account_access_profiles (account owns ordered venues).\n2. Cross-provider bundling in RepoProfileRoute → explicit per-provider account lists.\n3. ChromeProfileBinding (per host) → the profile primitive: chrome directory + expected login, per host, referenced by accounts.\n4. ForwardedProfileBundle → rekeys to accounts+credentials per repo route.\n5. Provider session pinning (profile, account) → account alone.\n\nKnown wrinkles to resolve in design (no hand-waving past these):\n\n* Account credential homes are host-local paths while account rows live in a shared store; homes (and possibly health) may need explicit HostId scoping, which today rides implicitly on the chrome binding's host.\n* Migration derives per-provider account routes from each repo's current profile order, and access-profile lists from today's reverse mappings; profile tables shrink rather than drop until forwarding is rekeyed.\n* Multiple accounts may list the same Chrome venue; ceremony verification is what keeps that safe.\n\nDone means: routing (repo and machine default), connect, import, exec, usage, forwarding, and failover all address accounts; each account lists the profiles that can log it in, tried in order and verified — never assumed; no command requires inventing a profile to use an account.","rank":62,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"6702bbf4-4e9b-40a4-bdb6-ecfc9a73318f","identifier":"LOO-75","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-75/fix-the-linear-pr-attachment-argument-and-make-a-wrong-mutation","name":"Fix the Linear PR-attachment argument and make a wrong mutation argument fail CI","description":"## Problem\n\nEvery published PR fails to link to its Linear issue. Loopflow sends an argument Linear does not accept:\n\n```\nLinear link degraded: linear request failed with status 400 Bad Request:\nUnknown argument \"subtitle\" on field \"Mutation.attachmentLinkURL\". Did you mean \"title\"?\n```\n\nSource: `rust/loopflow/src/pm/linear.rs:258` —\n\n```\nattachmentLinkURL(issueId: $issueId, url: $url, title: $title, subtitle: $subtitle)\n```\n\nShipped by **#1010** (\"task: link published PRs to their Linear issue\", merge f93267651, 2026-07-16), which closed task `f994b5e1` **\"Link every published PR on its Linear Task\" as done**. The task is closed; the capability does not work. Observed live on ENG-20's PRs #1019 and #1021, ENG-12's #1011, and every other published PR in the queue — each shows `Linear link degraded` in `lf task status`.\n\n## The deeper defect: the test cannot fail\n\n`pm/linear.rs:1937` mocks the Linear response:\n\n```\njson!({ \"data\": { \"attachmentLinkURL\": { \"attachment\": { \"id\": \"att-1\" } } } })\n```\n\nThe mock answers whatever we send, so an argument Linear rejects parses as success. **CI is structurally incapable of catching this class**, which is why a 400 on every call shipped green. This is the trap CLAUDE.md names directly: *\"Don't mock to verify internal wiring... The test will break when you refactor, even if the feature still works\"* — here the inverse, the test passes while the feature is broken.\n\nFixing only the argument leaves the hole: the next argument drift ships the same way.\n\n## Done when\n\n* Published PRs link to their Linear issue. `lf task status <id>` shows no `Linear link degraded` on a PR whose publication succeeded.\n* **A wrong argument to a Linear mutation fails a test.** Whatever the mechanism — asserting the request shape rather than the mocked response, pinning each mutation's argument set, or a contract check against Linear's schema — the proof is that reintroducing `subtitle` turns something red. A mock that echoes success is not coverage.\n* `f994b5e1` (\"Link every published PR on its Linear Task\") is only truly done once the above holds; it is currently closed on an untested claim.\n\n## Related, same shape\n\nWave memory already records one of these: Linear takes the same id as `String!` on mutation inputs and `ID!` inside a filter, and `lf pm task done` silently failed to close issues until each position was pinned separately — with the note that *\"a blanket 'no ID! anywhere' guard test is the trap — pin each position separately.\"* This is the argument-name version of that same drift, and the same lesson applies: pin the real request, not a convenient stand-in.\n\n## KR\n\nAdvances 'Avoidable human-in-the-loop setup or repair steps found in agent runs fall to zero' — a human currently has to notice a degraded link and cross-reference the PR by hand — and it protects the observable-evidence surface the ci-fix work (ENG-19/ENG-20) is building on, which reads `CiIncident` and command evidence alongside these links.\n\n## Sequencing\n\nFiled, not started, per the standing directive to drain ENG-12/19/20 before new work. ENG-12 has merged; ENG-19 and ENG-20 remain in flight.","rank":63,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"c1285d06-5852-4858-938f-dc0e4bee6c07","identifier":"LOO-76","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-76/make-the-wave-idle-message-test-deterministic","name":"Make the wave idle-message test deterministic","description":"Observed on PR #1026, GitHub Actions run 29542967146 attempt 1: flowloop:👋:tests::message_while_idle_starts_a_pass_answering_it failed after its 3.114s polling deadline with \"condition not met in time: pass spawned\"; the identical head was immediately rerun. Remove the flake rather than institutionalizing retries. Find the scheduler/notification race, then either prove the user behavior with deterministic synchronization or delete the test if it adds no durable value. Do not lengthen sleeps or add retries. Proof: the focused test passes under a high-count stress run and the normal Rust suite remains green.","rank":64,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"c1deb1ff-7b3a-406a-b4bd-32b6f9b78cd1","identifier":"LOO-77","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-77/prove-the-complete-cli-wave-resolution-matrix-for-reads-and-mutations","name":"Prove the complete CLI Wave-resolution matrix for reads and mutations","description":"Discovered after Product W2-151 merged. Its tests prove a central resolver and several read consumers, but the task closed without the required full read-plus-mutation parser matrix.\\n\\nUser outcome\\n\\nAdding or changing an lf command cannot silently invent a different rule for Wave context.\\n\\nDone when\\n\\n- One table-driven integration harness enumerates every Wave-scoped read and mutation command.\\n- Each command is exercised under registered UUID, registered hand-set name, explicit valid override, stale UUID, stale name, explicit unknown, and absent context.\\n- Expected outcomes are shared classifications, not per-command snapshots that bless divergence.\\n- The matrix runs cache-only with deterministic local stores and proves mutations target exactly the resolved Wave.\\n- CI fails when a new Wave-scoped command is not registered in the matrix.\\n\\nSource: W2-151 post-merge done-when audit.","rank":65,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"f9649051-f5ce-4dfc-9d49-449cef88872f","identifier":"LOO-78","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-78/reject-unknown-explicit-wave-names-consistently-across-all-consumers","name":"Reject unknown explicit Wave names consistently across all consumers","description":"Discovered after Product W2-151 merged. Live merged-branch proof under LF_WAVE_ID=product: lf memory show --wave definitely-unknown exits 0 with empty output, while lf status and lf pm show reject the same explicit name differently. engine/wave_context.rs names UnknownExplicit but currently permits any nonempty explicit name.\\n\\nUser outcome\\n\\nAn explicit unknown Wave is never accepted as an empty or synthetic target. Every command rejects it with one classified actionable error.\\n\\nDone when\\n\\n- The shared resolver validates every nonempty explicit Wave name against the local registry.\\n- Memory, status, PM, chat, radio, trace, home, cron, Task, and Project consumers return the same UnknownExplicit classification and safe next action.\\n- Explicit valid names still override stale ambient context.\\n- Empty explicit input remains a distinct invalid-input error.\\n- A subprocess integration test preserves the live W2-151 reproduction and the full consumer matrix.","rank":66,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"c098765f-d10a-42e8-95fb-1e92dc98c20d","identifier":"LOO-79","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-79/preserve-stale-ambient-wave-identity-failures-in-trace-and-run","name":"Preserve stale ambient Wave identity failures in trace and run attribution","description":"Discovered after Product W2-151 merged. engine/wave_context.rs currently swallows StaleIdentity and registry errors during trace/run attribution and falls back to worktree inference, turning an explicitly stale durable identity into silent misattribution.\\n\\nUser outcome\\n\\nA stale managed Wave identity is visible and actionable everywhere; trace and run records never quietly attach to a different Wave inferred from the worktree.\\n\\nDone when\\n\\n- Trace/run attribution propagates classified stale identity and registry failures.\\n- Worktree inference is used only when no managed identity was supplied, never after supplied identity failed validation.\\n- Error text names the stale source and safe explicit recovery.\\n- Tests cover stale UUID, stale name, absent identity, valid UUID/name, and explicit override.\\n- Existing traces are not rewritten and wire DTOs remain honest.","rank":67,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"f0750ef9-9119-4518-8cd7-61e142754e21","identifier":"LOO-80","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-80/resolve-ambient-wave-names-and-uuids-uniformly-in-task-and-project","name":"Resolve ambient Wave names and UUIDs uniformly in Task and Project controls","description":"Discovered after Product W2-151 merged. rust/loopflow/src/ops/task.rs and rust/loopflow/src/ops/project.rs still parse LF_WAVE_ID only as a UUID, while the shared command resolver accepts both registered UUIDs and hand-set Wave names.\\n\\nUser outcome\\n\\nTask and Project lifecycle controls work identically whether managed context carries the registered Wave UUID or a hand-set registered Wave name.\\n\\nDone when\\n\\n- Task and Project control entry points use the shared Wave identity resolver rather than local UUID parsing.\\n- A registered name, registered UUID, explicit override, stale UUID, stale name, and absent context produce the same classified result across Task and Project operations.\\n- Explicit Wave selection wins over ambient context.\\n- Focused integration tests exercise both controls with real store rows and no network.\\n- No compatibility parser or second identity model remains.\\n\\nSource: W2-151 post-merge done-when audit.","rank":68,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"00089929-a36b-4e3b-8951-895c8fd1e99f","identifier":"LOO-81","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-81/never-complete-a-linear-task-before-its-pr-and-review-gates","name":"Never complete a Linear Task before its PR and review gates","description":"Incident evidence: Product task W2-151 is marked completed in the refreshed Linear snapshot while durable Task Session ts_f0e... remains at required Human review ir_987b16aa8cce417087f41f53cdf2029b and PR #979 is still open. Completion writeback outran the Task's merge and review gates.\\n\\nUser outcome\\n\\nA Linear Task never appears done while its implementation PR or required review is still open. The PM row, durable Task Session, PR state, and review state converge monotonically.\\n\\nDone when\\n\\n- PM completion writeback is impossible until every active Task PR is merged or explicitly abandoned and every required Task review is approved.\\n- A Task that is waiting on review or an open PR remains open in Linear with an actionable reason.\\n- Reconciliation detects and repairs premature completion without losing the Session, PR, directives, or review transcript.\\n- Out-of-band merge, delayed review, writeback retry, restart, and duplicate observation are deterministic and idempotent.\\n- An integration test reproduces the W2-151 ordering and proves Linear cannot close early.\\n- Dogfood W2-151 through review and merge, then verify its final PM completion happens exactly once.\\n\\nDo not weaken the review gate or infer merge from a green head.","rank":69,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"7935f721-ce2c-46e3-a93e-a13cc694e758","identifier":"LOO-82","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-82/keep-run-lineage-valid-across-trace-boundaries-and-retention","name":"Keep run lineage valid across trace boundaries and retention","description":"Observed on 2026-07-15 while verifying W2-225 with the published v0.11.2 binary. `lf doctor --json` reported lineage=fail: 7 parent process ids were missing or belonged to another trace.\n\nUser-visible outcome\n\nEvery retained `run_events.parent_process_id` resolves to a retained parent or to an explicit typed external/pruned boundary. Cross-trace parentage and retention never masquerade as corruption, while truly missing parents remain a focused actionable failure.\n\nProof\n\nClassify the seven production-shaped failures, identify the writer or retention path that breaks lineage, correct it at the ownership boundary, and add regression coverage for nested runs, cross-trace handoff, and retention. `lf doctor --json` must report a healthy lineage check on the fixture without weakening detection of a genuinely missing parent.","rank":70,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"daf7f6cc-98ec-45c9-82dd-30749c4206d4","identifier":"LOO-83","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-83/make-trace-capture-references-survive-cleanup-and-interrupted-runs","name":"Make trace capture references survive cleanup and interrupted runs","description":"Observed on 2026-07-15 while verifying W2-225 with the published v0.11.2 binary. `lf doctor --json` reported capture=fail: 235 failures because trace metadata referenced missing `conversation.jsonl` files. This makes the Home health surface permanently red and hides new capture loss among historical damage.\n\nUser-visible outcome\n\nEvery retained capture record either resolves to its conversation artifact or carries an explicit terminal unavailable/pruned state with a reason. Cleanup, interrupted provider runs, and worktree/session rotation cannot leave a live-looking reference to a missing file. `lf doctor` distinguishes historical/intentional pruning from fresh capture loss and gives an actionable count.\n\nProof\n\nReproduce the deletion/interruption path that created the current dangling references, make metadata/artifact lifecycle atomic or explicitly tombstoned, add regression coverage, and run doctor against a copied production-shaped store. Do not rewrite or delete the production database by hand.","rank":71,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"bb2a9aa3-a6bf-4c43-9302-8d8c5a09a1bb","identifier":"LOO-84","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-84/keep-ops-telemetry-out-of-tracked-worktree-state","name":"Keep ops telemetry out of tracked worktree state","description":"Observed while launching W2-230 on 2026-07-15. Canonical main was clean except for generated .lf/metrics/ops.jsonl, so lf task run refused to dispatch. The implementation in rust/loopflow/src/lf/commands/ops/mod.rs still writes repo/.lf/metrics/ops.jsonl while Infrastructure memory and the ignored-path contract name .lf/tmp/metrics/ops.jsonl. Historical journals show the same generated file repeatedly blocking rebases and forcing agents to discard it manually.\\n\\nUser outcome\\n\\nRead-only planning, status, rebase, and task dispatch never dirty a source worktree merely by recording local telemetry.\\n\\nDone when\\n\\n- Ops telemetry writes to an ignored local-state path, preferably .lf/tmp/metrics/ops.jsonl, with one source of truth.\\n- Existing generated telemetry can be preserved or migrated without committing it.\\n- Canonical main, Wave homes, and Task worktrees remain clean after representative lf rebase --plan, status, PM, commit, and land flows.\\n- A regression test fails if telemetry targets a tracked path or makes task dispatch refuse a previously clean checkout.\\n- Documentation and Infrastructure memory agree with the implementation.\\n\\nDo not disable telemetry; fix its ownership and path.","rank":72,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"8ffcd0c6-8b84-4fe9-9786-f838906de837","identifier":"LOO-85","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-85/settle-one-bounded-ci-fix-turn-without-entering-the-generic-task-gate","name":"Settle one bounded ci-fix turn without entering the generic Task gate","description":"## Outcome\n\nOne failed PR head wakes at most one bounded repair body. After that body pushes, settlement judges the authoritative remote head, not a pre-turn cached observation, before declaring that the body did not repair anything.\n\n## Live evidence: W2-311 / PR #1065\n\nOn 2026-07-17, system command cc_25913faa6aa94ba29a664c5588553cc2 woke Codex generation 3 for failed head 709046f64477aebff648211daf2957e0455e993d. The autonomous body diagnosed the Rust stack overflow and pushed 02527e291885ad6305921bff54c23dc69065502b. Every substantive hosted check passed on the new head; only the structural scratch-clear and tests-result roll-up remained red.\n\nSettlement still used the pre-turn PR observation at 709046f. It marked the original wake failed, blocked the incident, and proposed that the body did not repair the head even though GitHub and the Task PR publication both held 02527e291. This is a settlement observation race, not an agent repair failure. The system command and generation remain the repair actors; no supervisor should claim the push.\n\n## Done when\n\n* After a CI-fix body returns, settlement either reads the authoritative remote PR head directly or waits for a PR observation known to be newer than the repair turn before evaluating head advancement.\n* If the remote head differs from the incident head, settlement records the response against that new head, rearms hosted CI observation, and never blocks the old incident as unrepaired.\n* Only an authoritative read proving the remote head is still the original failed head may produce the did-not-repair outcome.\n* Duplicate, stale, concurrent, and restart deliveries for one failed head still launch at most one repair body.\n* Durable evidence ties the incident, system command, generation, old head, and repaired head together so attribution remains automatic.\n* A deterministic regression holds a stale pre-turn observation while the fake body pushes a new remote head, then proves no false block and no second body.\n\nKeep this at the CI-fix settlement boundary. Do not add another PR observation cache or a generic gate body.","rank":73,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"f0e5e4bd-9aa6-4d2d-a4e0-1b2068a2cf51","identifier":"LOO-86","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-86/make-ci-fix-infrastructure-failures-block-the-task-actionably","name":"Make ci-fix infrastructure failures block the Task actionably","description":"Source: Product task W2-156, [https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/W2-156/wake-a-waiting-task-into-ci-fix-when-its-pr-fails\\n\\nUser](<https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/W2-156/wake-a-waiting-task-into-ci-fix-when-its-pr-fails%5Cn%5CnUser>) outcome\\n\\nWhen a ci-fix body cannot repair a PR because infrastructure is unavailable, the Task turns red with an actionable reason instead of quietly returning to Waiting.\\n\\nDone when\\n\\n- A ci-fix result can distinguish code-repair completion from an infrastructure blocker without relying on worktree churn.\\n- Infrastructure failure transitions the Task to Blocked with the failing capability and safe next action.\\n- The same unchanged PR remains attached and resumable after the infrastructure recovers.\\n- Waiting is reserved for a healthy PR observation or human-review boundary, not a failed repair environment.\\n- Tests cover provider outage, GitHub observation failure, and no-change agent report paths.","rank":74,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"73581397-4626-4638-b31e-a9524b629061","identifier":"LOO-87","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-87/route-automatic-ci-fix-wakes-through-the-durable-task-command-ledger","name":"Route automatic ci-fix wakes through the durable Task command ledger","description":"Source: Product task W2-156, [https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/W2-156/wake-a-waiting-task-into-ci-fix-when-its-pr-fails\\n\\nUser](<https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/W2-156/wake-a-waiting-task-into-ci-fix-when-its-pr-fails%5Cn%5CnUser>) outcome\\n\\nAn automatic failed-PR wake is as inspectable and deduplicated as a human Task direction, not an unaudited direct runner call.\\n\\nDone when\\n\\n- Failed-head observation enqueues a typed ci-fix Task command carrying canonical PR number, head SHA, failed checks, and delivery identity.\\n- The existing durable command/event path owns acceptance, deduplication, incorporation, and restart behavior.\\n- The Project runner does not bypass that ledger with a direct wake-only call.\\n- Task status and events expose which failure set woke which body.\\n- Tests prove duplicate observation, restart, and delayed incorporation without launching two bodies.\\n\\nKeep one command model; do not add a parallel ci-fix queue.","rank":75,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"7d540011-842d-4cab-bacf-1caabf1266ce","identifier":"LOO-88","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-88/prove-failed-pr-ci-fix-recovery-as-one-deterministic-lifecycle","name":"Prove failed-PR ci-fix recovery as one deterministic lifecycle","description":"Source: Product task W2-156, [https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/W2-156/wake-a-waiting-task-into-ci-fix-when-its-pr-fails\\n\\nUser](<https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/W2-156/wake-a-waiting-task-into-ci-fix-when-its-pr-fails%5Cn%5CnUser>) outcome\\n\\nA maintainer can trust that one failed PR head wakes exactly one ci-fix body, that the same PR is repaired, and that a new head rearms observation until the PR is green.\\n\\nDone when\\n\\n- A deterministic integration harness drives pending to failed head to exactly one ci-fix body to same-PR push to new-head rearm to green waiting.\\n- Duplicate and concurrent failed-head deliveries still produce one body.\\n- Restart between observation, wake, and settlement preserves the same outcome.\\n- Infrastructure-blocked behavior is exercised explicitly.\\n- The proof uses local deterministic fakes rather than live GitHub or provider traffic and runs in normal CI.\\n- CiObservation wire fields are explicit in Rust, fixtures, and mirrored consumers; no serde default hides schema drift.\\n\\nThis is the final behavioral proof for W2-156. Keep W2-156 open until this and its three implementation prerequisites hold.","rank":76,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"52c79f90-a441-44a1-90e3-229f2d11ff2a","identifier":"LOO-89","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-89/verify-v0112-migration-recovery-from-a-fresh-task-body","name":"Verify v0.11.2 migration recovery from a fresh Task body","description":"<!-- loopflow-task-start:21fe5c57c1a3d3f82a9a55b214fa30eeaf35f71b021b6283a7d506837e4bf2ec -->","rank":77,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"fab60eae-27c8-4c53-bb99-ca58e21f711a","identifier":"LOO-90","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-90/make-glm-52-loopflows-opencode-default","name":"Make GLM-5.2 Loopflow's OpenCode default","description":"Bare `opencode` must resolve inside Loopflow to `opencode/glm-5.2` for both one-shot CLI launches and durable Session harness payloads. Explicit `opencode:<provider>/<model>` selections still win. Loopflow must send the model explicitly so OpenCode configuration cannot silently fall back to a lower-capability model such as `big-pickle`.\n\nDone when:\n\n* `parse_agent(\"opencode\")` returns harness `opencode` and model `opencode/glm-5.2`.\n* CLI command construction includes `--model opencode/glm-5.2`.\n* OpenCode Session turn payloads contain provider `opencode`, model `glm-5.2`.\n* Documentation names the Loopflow-owned default.\n* OpenCode-focused Rust tests and `cargo fmt --check` pass.","rank":78,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"d00be282-bd3e-40a1-9290-7d74ddc3ca01","identifier":"LOO-91","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-91/resolve-session-bodies-through-the-current-home-lf","name":"Resolve Session bodies through the current Home lf","description":"User-visible outcome\n\nA durable Project or Task Session resumes after Loopflow upgrades without depending on the executable file that happened to launch its first body. Shared-store schema evolution cannot strand otherwise resumable work.\n\nOwnership and invariant\n\nThe shared SQLite store is the compatibility boundary. A Session row lives in that store, so it must not persist or launch through a historical lf binary, database path, or LF_HOME. The current Home resolves all three at body-launch time.\n\n* Remove the persisted ChildExecutionContext triple from Project and Task Session domain state and new-session writes.\n* Resume and supervisor relaunch resolve the current Home lf, current store connection, and current LF_HOME at the launch boundary.\n* Do not add a compatibility fallback to the old persisted columns. Stop reading them; historical SQLite columns may remain until an earned table rebuild.\n* Every ChildProcessGeneration records immutable binary provenance for audit: at minimum build version and Git/source identity available from build_info. Provenance says what ran; it never selects what runs next.\n* Worktree, Wave/Home identity, provider, provider session id, directives, commands, body generation, and PR history remain durable and unchanged.\n* Resolve and validate the current executable before reserving a generation. A missing or incompatible current lf fails visibly without burning a reservation.\n* The same rule applies to Project and Task Sessions and to handoff completion waking a parent.\n\nRegression evidence\n\nW2-177, W2-178, W2-218, and W2-224 are presently stranded because their stored lf_bin paths are missing or their binaries reject the shared database migration 0.11.016_task_linear_observations. Use these as the real recovery proof; do not rewrite their Session rows manually.\n\nProof\n\nTests create a Session under binary provenance A, advance the shared store/current launcher to provenance B, remove A, and resume the same Session through B with its worktree, provider history, commands, and generation sequence intact. Cover Project and Task Sessions, live handoff, restart after a failed reservation, and status DTO provenance. After building the fix, use it to resume W2-177, W2-178, and W2-224 successfully.\n\nScope\n\nKeep this a narrow launch-ownership correction. Do not redesign migrations, introduce a daemon dependency, copy the database, or create new Session successors.\n\nDelivery\n\nPR #986: [https://github.com/loopflowstudio/loopflow/pull/986](<https://github.com/loopflowstudio/loopflow/pull/986>)","rank":79,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"5585e65c-c2ed-41ad-8607-ae7f5a1206b4","identifier":"LOO-92","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-92/reintroduce-lfd-as-the-home-subscription-ingress","name":"Reintroduce lfd as the Home subscription ingress","description":"User-visible outcome\n\nOne maintained Loopflow Home accepts signed Linear and GitHub events even while every Wave is black/off, routes each event to the owning repository, Wave, Project, or Task Session, and makes the resulting direction or observation durable before acknowledging delivery.\n\nOwnership\n\n* Run one lfd per LF_HOME/machine. Repositories and Waves register routing context with that Home; they do not each run a public webhook server.\n* lfd owns external subscription registration, the minimal public HTTP ingress, signature verification, provider delivery receipts, durable deduplication, routing, and best-effort nudges to live owners.\n* lf and the current domain libraries remain the only implementation of Task direction, Wave observations, PM state, and GitHub/PR behavior. lfd calls those typed operations; it does not grow parallel business logic or arbitrary argv execution.\n* lfd never owns agent execution, Wave residency, chat, cadence, Project/Task lifecycle decisions, worktrees, or PR landing.\n* Provider credentials belong to Home profiles/accounts. A Wave may supply routing identity, but --wave must not masquerade as webhook scope.\n\nHistorical implementation to mine\n\n* d6c00459c and adbb7ab13 define lfd as a relay listener and access gate, with lf as the single implementation.\n* f3fcb3a7f and the parent of 309575f8e contain the old GitHub hook router, signature checks, request limits, service lifecycle, and tests.\n* Reuse the launchd/systemd service rendering and status shape where it still fits, but never persist raw secrets in service files.\n* Reuse the bounded hook-only router, non-loopback bind checks, HMAC verification, error redaction, and plan-then-deliver test shape.\n* Do not restore /v0/exec, the read API, global session/process supervision, worktree janitors, provider token loops, WebSockets, Docker deployment, the Swift BundledDaemonManager, or the old generic runtime.\n* Do not restore the old in-memory CI dedupe or detached lf spawning after a success response. Persist a provider delivery and its routed command/observation transactionally before returning 2xx.\n\nShape\n\nLinear or GitHub -> signed provider adapter -> durable Home delivery inbox -> typed router -> existing Task/Wave/store operation -> nudge if live.\n\nKeep provider adapters explicit rather than inventing a generic plugin framework. Linear support may build on W2-206. GitHub may initially prove routing and durable receipt with one useful event type rather than restoring every old hook behavior.\n\nService and security\n\n* Native launchd on macOS and systemd user service on Linux; one stable health/status surface and actionable logs.\n* Default private/local control surface. Public ingress exposes only provider hook paths and health required by deployment; it must not expose Wave chat, store reads, or an exec door.\n* Load secrets through the current profile/Doppler boundary. Never print them or copy them into generated service definitions.\n* Bound bodies and request time, reject invalid signatures before parsing, use provider delivery ids for durable idempotency, tolerate retries and reordering, and expose stale/degraded subscription state without disabling lf.\n\nProof\n\nStart one lfd with two registered repositories and multiple Waves. Deliver valid and invalid signed fixtures for Linear and GitHub. Prove valid events resolve only to the owning target, are persisted before 2xx, arrive exactly once across duplicate delivery and daemon restart, and remain pending while the target Wave is off. Prove unmatched events are handled deterministically and visibly. Prove the public router has no arbitrary exec or broad read routes. Verify launchd install/start/restart/status on the maintained Mac and deterministic service-render tests for Linux.\n\nDelivery\n\nStack this Task on W2-206 while its Linear adapter PR is open. Keep the first PR narrow enough to explain in one screen: daemon lifecycle, durable ingress envelope/router, and Linear adapter integration. Follow-on provider behaviors should be separate Tasks once the boundary is proven.","rank":80,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"c66d3fb6-47ed-4d00-93c2-6aee8752db5a","identifier":"LOO-93","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-93/make-migration-histories-safe-under-continuous-dogfooding","name":"Make migration histories safe under continuous dogfooding","description":"# 5 Whys: migration history repeatedly disables `lf`\n\n## The Problem\n\nWithin roughly 30 hours of launching release-namespaced migrations, normal Loopflow dogfooding produced incompatible histories in the durable local database more than once. The installed `lf` now rejects that database, and the rejection has blocked work unrelated to schema changes, including agent-assisted PR landing.\n\nThis has not been an isolated rough edge after launch. The operating model and the migration model disagree, so the same class of failure keeps returning under ordinary use.\n\n## Evidence\n\n| Time / state | Evidence | What it establishes |\n| -- | -- | -- |\n| 2026-07-14 12:20 PDT | `c09708dbf` introduced release-scoped migration names, strict prefix validation, and release-tag immutability checks. | The new model launched less than two days before this analysis. |\n| 2026-07-14 12:52–15:07 PDT | `v0.11.0` and `v0.11.1` were tagged. Subsequent migrations continued to use the `0.11.*` namespace while the package version remained `0.11.1`. | Semver stopped distinguishing binaries even as their accepted schemas diverged. |\n| Since launch | 42 commits across local refs touched migrations. Parallel branches assigned different meanings to the same ordinals, including `0.11.006`, `.007`, `.008`, and `.014`. | The collision is a normal consequence of concurrent work, not a singular authoring mistake. |\n| Production backups | One historical chain used `.008_context_pressure`, `.009_context_input_normalization`, `.010_profiles`, `.011_provider_account_lifecycle`. | A branch-local ordering reached durable production data before merge or release made it canonical. |\n| Current production ledger | The current database instead contains `.008_interactive_handoffs`, `.009_profiles`, `.010_provider_account_lifecycle`, `.011_context_pressure`, `.012_context_input_normalization`. | The database has since passed through a second, noncanonical convergence attempt. The exact writer is not yet known. |\n| Current `lf doctor` | Reports `applied 0.11.012_context_input_normalization / known 0.11.012_provider_account_lifecycle`, then rejects unknown `0.11.009_profiles`. | The installed binary cannot identify or recover the history, even though both writers call themselves `0.11.1`. |\n| PR landing | Journaling degrades when the store is unavailable, but implicit trace capture for the PR-copy agent hard-fails on the same store open. | An observability dependency can disable an otherwise unrelated command. |\n\n### Incident-specific recovery proof\n\nThe current ledger has two orders. SQLite row insertion order is\n`.008_interactive_handoffs`, `.011/.012_context_*`, then\n`.009_profiles/.010_provider_account_lifecycle`; the stored timestamps sort\nthose same rows as `.008`, `.009/.010_profiles`, then `.011/.012_context_*`.\n`applied_versions` validates by `(applied_at, rowid)`, so a repair has to\ncanonicalize the ordered history, not only rename rows.\n\nTwo copies of the live database proved the boundary without touching production:\n\n1. Renaming the four semantic migrations to main's ordinals while retaining\n   each migration's original timestamp still failed exact-prefix validation.\n2. Renaming them and assigning the already-existing timestamp set in main's\n   canonical order opened successfully with installed `lf 0.11.1`. Current main\n   then opened the same copy, applied `.013_task_review_state` and\n   `.014_task_lifecycle`, and passed SQLite integrity checking.\n\nThis establishes that the product schema and data are compatible; the outage is\nthe ledger's identity/order. A safe immediate repair can therefore be narrow:\nmatch the exact suffix, verify the full product schema, take a fresh backup, and\nrewrite only those four migration identities and their ordering timestamps.\n\nThe live semantic order matches the Context Lab-era branch history and was\nwritten at 12:46:56–12:47:00 PDT, after dev/release store isolation merged and\nbefore migration convergence PR #940. That makes Context Lab the strongest\nlineage lead, but not a proven writer: `schema_migrations` records no build,\ncommit, source checkout, command, or provenance. The incident record should not\nclaim the exact writer until another durable log supplies it.\n\nNo production data was changed during this investigation. The database and its backups were inspected read-only, and `lf doctor` was run as a diagnostic.\n\n## Chain\n\nMigration mismatch → exact-prefix rejection → durable branch-local histories → ordinal reuse across parallel work → protection begins only at release tags → releases are treated as the deployment boundary → real dogfooding deploys untagged branch schemas → one eagerly migrated store is an availability dependency for unrelated capabilities\n\n**Problem**: Normal `lf` use has repeatedly made the durable local database unreadable by another current `lf` binary, and one mismatch can disable broad portions of the CLI.\n\n↳ *Could we have caught this earlier?*  \nAn end-to-end test needed to exercise the real lifecycle: branch A opens a persistent database, branch B adds a concurrent migration, main merges both, and the installed client then opens the database. Existing tests validate one checkout in isolation.\n\n### Why 1: the installed binary rejects the database's migration ledger\n\n`SqliteStore::new` requires the applied migration sequence to be an exact prefix of the binary's registry. The current database contains migration names in an order that is not a prefix of current main. Strict rejection is reasonable once identities are trustworthy; here it is surfacing an identity collision the authoring system allowed.\n\nThe diagnostic cannot name the writer or a safe recovery path because `schema_migrations` records only `version` and `applied_at`. It records no migration checksum, parent/history fingerprint, Git revision, source identity, or writer build.\n\n↳ *Could we have caught this earlier?*  \n`lf doctor` could have compared history fingerprints and named the first fork. A ledger with writer provenance could have identified the exact binary that created it. Today the error suggests “a newer release or matching divergent local build,” but both incompatible binaries identify as `0.11.1` and the matching build is not discoverable from the database.\n\n### Why 2: more than one valid-looking migration history reached durable data\n\nThe production backups show a branch-local order that differs from canonical main. A later repair, manual intervention, or different branch binary produced the current second divergence. The exact action that rewrote the current ledger remains unanswered, but it is not necessary to explain the first failure: the original divergent chain was already created by supported migration execution.\n\nCommit `7cee5853f` added a hardcoded repair for one exact divergent lineage. That repair cannot recognize the current lineage. Each hardcoded branch history therefore turns convergence into another shipping event and leaves the next permutation unsupported.\n\n↳ *What process allowed this?*  \nFeature binaries are regularly exercised against real profiles, provider accounts, tasks, and run state. Before dev-store isolation landed, branch builds defaulted to the shared store. After isolation, the only supported way to test a branch against that real control-plane state is a break-glass environment variable. A path necessary for normal dogfooding is being modeled as exceptional.\n\n### Why 3: parallel branches can assign different schemas the same migration identity\n\n`scripts/new_migration.py` scans only the current worktree and chooses `max(used ordinal) + 1`. It has no reservation, fetch/rebase step, merge-base comparison, globally unique identity, or dependency declaration. Two branches created from the same main therefore choose the same next number by construction.\n\nObserved collisions include:\n\n* `0.11.006`: `context_launch_work` and `task_pr_stack`\n* `0.11.007`: `task_pr_parent`, `task_session_successors`, and `profiles`\n* `0.11.008`: `profiles`, `provider_account_lifecycle`, `context_pressure`, and `interactive_handoffs`\n* `0.11.014`: merged `task_lifecycle` and branch-local `task_linear_observations`\n\nThe open `task-interaction` branch has already allocated `.015_interaction_reviews`, so the mechanism remains active.\n\n↳ *Could tooling have prevented this?*  \nYes. Migration identity does not need to encode a final total order at authoring time. Globally unique, immutable identities plus explicit dependencies—or a serialized reservation performed against the shared integration branch—would make two branches unable to create different migrations with the same identity.\n\n### Why 4: CI protects release history, not deployed history\n\n`scripts/check_migrations.py` checks registry/file alignment, local ordering, namespace/version compatibility, and immutability relative to the most recent release tag. It does not compare a PR with its merge base, query the current integration branch, detect another meaning for an unshipped ordinal, or simulate convergence from databases written by sibling branches.\n\nIts tests faithfully prove that tagged migrations do not change. They do not test concurrent authoring or a persistent database moving between branch, main, and installed binaries.\n\n↳ *What assumption was wrong?*  \nThe check assumes a migration becomes durable history when it ships in a tagged release. In Loopflow, an untagged migration becomes durable history as soon as a dogfood binary opens the live control-plane database.\n\n### Why 5: release identity is being used as deployment identity\n\nMultiple main, branch, and installed binaries all report `0.11.1` while carrying different migration registries. The migration namespace says they are one release line; the database compatibility contract says they are different products. There is no build identity or publication state between “authored in a worktree” and “released in semver.”\n\nThe result is a dangerous gap: Git considers an ordinal unshipped and safe to renumber while a live database already treats that exact string as irreversible history.\n\n↳ *Why was that assumption encoded?*  \nThe design optimized for a conventional application lifecycle: migrations merge, a release ships, and production then applies them in release order. Loopflow's creator dogfoods branches continuously against long-lived local state. The conventional release sequence does not exist here.\n\n### Why 6: branch isolation does not provide a complete dogfood workflow\n\nSource-specific dev stores now prevent accidental writes to `~/.lf/loopflow.db`, which contains the immediate blast radius. They do not let a feature branch exercise real authenticated profiles and control-plane state without either rebuilding that state or bypassing the guard. There is no supported snapshot/clone workflow, no explicit promotion boundary, and no compatibility service between a branch client and the production store.\n\n↳ *What feedback loop failed?*  \nThe system treats use of production data from a dev build as misuse, but the product workflow rewards it because that is where the configured accounts and useful state live. The break-glass flag records intent; it does not make the database transition safe.\n\n### Why 7: the store has one schema-compatibility fate\n\nProfiles and provider accounts, wave/project/task state, run journaling, trace capture, and other control-plane data share one eagerly migrated SQLite schema. Opening `SqliteStore` applies all known migrations and validates the full product schema before a caller can use any portion.\n\nThis turns an incompatible task migration into an authentication or observability outage, and an incompatible profile migration into a PR workflow outage. The `open_run_ledger_read_only` path demonstrates that narrower access is possible, but it is not the general capability boundary.\n\n↳ *Could architecture have contained this?*  \nYes. Data with different compatibility and availability requirements can have separate stores or explicit schema capabilities. At minimum, commands that do not need the store must not open it, and optional telemetry must remain optional through the entire launch path.\n\n### Why 8 (Root): migration ownership does not match Loopflow's operating model\n\nThe system models migrations as a single release-ordered chain owned by the repository. Loopflow operates as multiple concurrent, same-version binaries acting on durable creator state before merge and release. There is no immutable identity at authoring time, no publication boundary before a branch can mutate durable state, no provenance for recovery, and no containment when one subsystem becomes incompatible.\n\nThe root cause is therefore not that migrations were numbered incorrectly or that someone used the wrong binary. The system permits ordinary parallel work to create ambiguous irreversible identities, then makes exact recognition of those identities a prerequisite for unrelated work.\n\n## Blast-Radius Chain\n\nThe migration collision explains why the store is incompatible. A second chain explains why that incompatibility has felt like the whole new system is unstable:\n\nStore history diverges → every `SqliteStore::new` validates the whole chain → run journaling warns and degrades → implicit trace capture independently opens the same store and fails closed → internal agent launch fails → `lf pr land` cannot generate PR copy → an unrelated release operation stops\n\nThe availability contract is inconsistent inside one command. Journaling treats telemetry as best effort, while implicit trace establishment treats it as required. The strictest incidental caller wins.\n\n## Why Existing Safeguards Did Not Hold\n\n| Safeguard | What it covers | Gap exposed in practice |\n| -- | -- | -- |\n| Release-namespaced ordinals | Human-readable grouping by release | Does not make IDs unique across branches within a release. |\n| Exact-prefix validation | Prevents an unknown binary from silently mutating a database | Correctly fails, but only after the authoring system has created ambiguous histories. |\n| Release-tag immutability check | Prevents edits to migrations already in a tag | The live deployment boundary occurs before the tag. |\n| Dev/release store isolation | Prevents a default dev build from touching the release store | Provides no smooth way to dogfood a branch with real control-plane state. |\n| Hardcoded divergent-history repair | Converges one known ordering | Every new ordering requires new code; the current database is already outside the recognized case. |\n| Migration backup | Provides a pre-migration SQLite copy | Filename and reuse are keyed by latest version, not a complete history/data fingerprint; freshness is unproven. |\n| `lf doctor` | Reports migration failure | Cannot identify the writer, find the right binary, preview repair, or perform a safe recovery. |\n| Best-effort run journal | Lets some commands continue without telemetry | A second implicit tracing path still fails closed before agent launch. |\n\n## Unanswered Whys\n\n| Branch Point | Unexplored Question | Priority |\n| -- | -- | -- |\n| Why 2 | What exact binary or repair action rewrote the database from the backup lineage into its current second divergent ordering? | Medium—important for the incident record, not required to establish the systemic cause. |\n| Why 2 | Does backup reuse ever restore a stale pre-migration snapshot after data changed without the migration version changing? | High |\n| Why 2 | Can two different histories with the same latest version reuse or overwrite the same backup filename? | High |\n| Why 2 | Did any repair change only `schema_migrations`, or did it also transform product data? | High before recovery |\n| Why 4 | Which open branches currently own unpublished migrations, and which have already applied them to durable stores? | High |\n| Why 4 | What is the earliest reliable publication event: migration creation, PR publication, merge queue entry, or merge to main? | High |\n| Why 6 | Which production state is truly required for branch dogfooding, and can it be safely cloned without provider session secrets? | High |\n| Why 7 | Which commands can operate store-free, read-only, or against a subset of schema capabilities? | High |\n| Why 7 | Should auth/profile state, operational control-plane state, and observability have separate schema lifecycles? | Medium |\n| Why 7 | Does `product_schema` need to compare types, indexes, triggers, constraints, and foreign keys before any generalized convergence repair can trust schema equivalence? | High |\n\n## Fixes\n\n| Level | Fix | Prevents |\n| -- | -- | -- |\n| Immediate | Add a tested, forward-only repair for the exact current production history, taking a fresh backup identified by a full history fingerprint before changing anything. | This database remaining unusable, or recovery destroying the only trustworthy snapshot. |\n| Immediate | Add a sanitized regression fixture containing the exact production migration rows and schema shape. | Releasing another binary that cannot recognize the known incident state. |\n| Immediate | Make implicit trace capture best effort for internal agent launches; preserve strict capture only when a user explicitly requests a trace. | Store/telemetry failures blocking PR landing and other unrelated agent-assisted commands. |\n| Immediate | Keep mechanical PR operations usable without a store or agent-generated copy. Surface copy generation as the degraded capability. | A control-plane outage turning into an inability to ship its repair. |\n| Structural | Replace order-derived identity with immutable globally unique migration IDs and explicit dependencies, or serialize allocation against one authoritative integration registry. Never renumber an authored migration. | Parallel branches assigning different schemas the same irreversible identity. |\n| Structural | Record content checksum, parent/history fingerprint, writer Git revision, source identity, and binary version for every applied migration. | Circular “find the matching build” diagnostics and unsafe guess-based repairs. |\n| Structural | Gate PRs by simulating convergence from latest release, merge-base, current main, and each migration-bearing branch history into the proposed merge result. | Tests proving only isolated local validity while cross-branch upgrades fail. |\n| Structural | Define publication before durable application: either forbid unpublished branch migrations from production stores or publish/reserve their immutable identities before they can run there. | Git and SQLite disagreeing about whether a migration may still change. |\n| Structural | Provide `lf store clone` or equivalent to create a branch-owned, history-fingerprinted snapshot of production state for dogfooding, with explicit secret/session handling. | Routine dependence on the production-store break-glass bypass. |\n| Structural | Make backup names and reuse depend on the complete migration-history fingerprint and snapshot freshness, not only the latest migration version. | Stale or wrong-lineage backups being mistaken for valid recovery points. |\n| Systemic | Define store capability boundaries: commands declare whether they need no store, read-only state, a specific subsystem, or a fully current schema. | One incompatible table family disabling the entire CLI. |\n| Systemic | Separate auth/profile registry, operational state, and telemetry when their compatibility and availability contracts differ materially. | Unrelated subsystems sharing one migration fate indefinitely. |\n| Systemic | Treat merge/publication and build identity—not semver tags alone—as part of the deployment protocol for continuous dogfooding. | Repeating this class of failure whenever branches run before a formal release. |\n\n## Changes to Implement\n\n- [ ] Preserve the current database and all backups; record file hashes and inspect the full schema before any repair.\n- [ ] Add a sanitized fixture for the exact current ledger and product schema, then make recovery pass from that fixture first.\n- [ ] Implement one forward-only, idempotent recovery for the current lineage with a fresh history-fingerprinted backup and a dry-run explanation.\n- [ ] Change implicit internal-agent trace capture to warn and continue when the ledger is unavailable.\n- [ ] Prove `lf pr land` can complete its mechanical path with an incompatible or absent store.\n- [ ] Choose an immutable migration identity/dependency format and remove worktree-local `max + 1` allocation.\n- [ ] Extend `schema_migrations` with checksum, parent/history fingerprint, and writer build/source provenance.\n- [ ] Replace latest-tag-only CI with a convergence matrix covering release, merge-base, current main, and migration-bearing branches.\n- [ ] Define and enforce when a migration becomes published and therefore immutable.\n- [ ] Add a supported production-to-branch store snapshot workflow so live dogfooding does not require a production write bypass.\n- [ ] Audit every store opener and classify it as required, optional, read-only, or subsystem-scoped.\n- [ ] Decide whether auth/profile state, operational state, and telemetry need separate databases after the capability audit supplies evidence.","rank":81,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"f994b5e1-c5ac-46e7-ac89-46bce2637f95","identifier":"LOO-94","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-94/link-every-published-pr-on-its-linear-task","name":"Link every published PR on its Linear Task","description":"<!-- loopflow-task-start:a9a5fe2b94e18b1361daf9d0c0692f949b374311eb75b080a2471590a647eef0 -->","rank":82,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"4005a921-3cac-4a18-bd6e-f652e7bce460","identifier":"LOO-95","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-95/stream-human-linear-edits-into-task-sessions-via-webhooks","name":"Stream human Linear edits into Task Sessions via webhooks","description":"Replace the planned Linear polling and resident sweep with webhook-driven ingestion.\n\nLinear issue title/description edits and new human comments must arrive through a verified webhook endpoint, then reuse the existing durable child_directives and child_commands writer and exactly-once ledger. Validate Linear webhook signatures, deduplicate deliveries by event identity, handle retries and out-of-order events, and never auto-launch a dormant Task. Live Tasks should react promptly; stopped Tasks persist direction for delivery on resume.\n\nInclude the operational setup needed to create/configure the Linear webhook and expose health or degraded delivery state. Preserve the useful read/store foundation already in PR #944, but remove polling-specific code and design.","rank":83,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"bbe9a6ff-9cf8-4970-8f47-e182ccccc706","identifier":"LOO-96","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-96/separate-headless-pr-publication-from-review-presentation","name":"Separate headless PR publication from review presentation","description":"User-visible outcome\n\nHumans and agents can create or refresh a well-written pull request without presenting it. An explicit human review action uses lf pr open to present the pull request in the preferred review surface.\n\nBehavioral contract\n\n* Add lf pr publish as the headless publication command. It pushes as needed, creates or refreshes the current pull request with the generated or supplied title and body, prints the resulting state and URL, and never invokes a review surface.\n* Make lf pr open the explicit review-presentation action. Today its default presentation is the GitHub pull-request URL in the browser.\n* Keep presentation behind one boundary so a later preference can open another review surface, such as a terminal diff or file browser, without changing publication semantics or duplicating presentation logic across callers.\n* The Swift Mac app may call lf pr open for an explicit user review action. Background app work must use publication without presentation.\n* lf pr submit and lf pr land use the headless publication path and never present or open anything.\n* Agent-facing skills, flows, help, and examples direct agents to publish, submit, or land. They do not direct agents to call pr open unless the human explicitly requested review presentation.\n* Failure to launch a review surface can fail only pr open; it cannot make a successfully published, submitted, or landed pull request look failed.\n\nEnd-to-end proof\n\nIn CLI integration tests with recorded publication and presentation boundaries, lf pr publish creates or updates the expected title and body with zero presentation attempts; submit and land also make zero presentation attempts; lf pr open makes exactly one presentation attempt after a PR URL exists. The default presenter opens GitHub. A Swift test proves the explicit review action delegates to pr open rather than opening a URL itself. Help and user documentation describe the same split.\n\nSource of truth and consumers\n\nGitHub remains the source of truth for pull-request state. One shared publication operation owns push plus create/update behavior. One presentation boundary owns how review is opened. The CLI commands, Swift Mac app, builtin PR skills, Loopflow instructions, and automation are consumers of those operations.\n\nAbsent and error states\n\nPublishing without an existing PR creates one. Publishing with an existing PR refreshes it. GitHub or push failure returns an actionable error without attempting presentation. pr open reports presentation failure only after publication succeeded and preserves the PR URL in its output. An absent presentation preference uses the GitHub browser default.\n\nExclusions\n\nDo not implement the terminal diff or file-browser presenter in this Task. Do not change submit versus land commitment levels, readiness behavior, assignment, auto-merge, merge gates, or title/body generation policy. Do not add presentation behavior to any other command.","rank":84,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"3c008cfc-1388-4519-9a7e-fba30302694b","identifier":"LOO-97","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-97/keep-auto-merge-revocation-compatible-with-current-github-cli","name":"Keep auto-merge revocation compatible with current GitHub CLI","description":"Landing PR #1133 on 2026-07-20 exposed command drift: Loopflow invokes gh pr merge NUMBER --disable, but GitHub CLI 2.86.0 accepts --disable-auto and rejects --disable. Update the single revocation implementation and its behavioral fixtures to the supported flag, then prove replay can revoke and re-arm an exact-head auto-merge request.","rank":85,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"9c6903d3-7834-4c1a-8476-680692124f7f","identifier":"LOO-98","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-98/preserve-serial-task-work-and-local-control-through-pr-observation","name":"Preserve serial Task work and local control through PR observation failures","description":"User story\n\nA Task worker can keep its durable work and accept local control even when GitHub observation is stale, rate-limited, or an earlier PR merged out of band. Resuming or rotating never silently drops committed or uncommitted follow-up work.\n\nDogfood failures\n\n* W2-166 committed unique follow-up work on its sequence-1 branch after PR #907 merged. The shipped lf pr next path in PR #913 carries dirty edits only and would rotate from origin/main without the already-committed range.\n* On 2026-07-15, exhausted GitHub GraphQL quota killed W2-93, W2-151, and W2-170 at flow boundaries and made lf task follow-up unusable because reconciliation unconditionally called gh pr list. Durable Task control became dependent on a remote read.\n\nEnd-to-end contract\n\n* Compute and preserve the full unique post-merge commit range plus dirty edits when rotating to a serial PR. Prove it with a W2-166-shaped integration test and recover W2-166 without manual history loss.\n* When a persisted GitHub PR number exists, reconcile it with a bounded REST read. Do not enumerate remote PRs for unpublished working PRs.\n* On quota, network, or GitHub failure, preserve cached durable PR state, keep local follow-up/steer/interrupt/status commands usable, and surface observation freshness plus the exact degraded reason.\n* A replacement directive accepted before auto-merge must either be incorporated before completion or remain visibly pending on the successor/follow-up path; completion cannot silently erase it.\n* Tests cover out-of-band merge plus committed continuation, dirty continuation, GraphQL exhaustion, REST failure, unpublished working PR, and the completion/directive race.\n\nKeep one durable Task/PR state model. Do not add a second cache or compatibility shim.","rank":86,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"9bc1029c-cd8a-496d-838b-53bb43e4abdd","identifier":"LOO-99","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-99/reconcile-out-of-band-merges-and-un-ignored-scratch-stash-so-landing","name":"Reconcile out-of-band merges and un-ignored scratch-stash so landing never needs manual repair","description":"Two post-merge friction bugs hit while landing W2-116 (PR #905), both avoidable-repair-step regressions against developer-efficiency:\n\n1. Out-of-band auto-merge leaves the Task's PR row open. PR #905 was merged by GitHub auto-merge (armed via 'lf pr land'), not via 'lf pr land -c'. Afterward 'lf pr status' correctly says 'No open PR for the current branch', but 'lf task complete W2-116' refuses with 'Task has an open pull request; merge it or run lf pr abandon first' — it reads a stale local Task->PR row that was never marked merged because loopflow didn't observe the out-of-band merge. There is no non-destructive lever to fix it: no --force on 'lf task complete', and 'lf pr abandon' would delete the live worktree/branch (wrong for an already-merged PR). Fix: when completing (or on PR status/sync), reconcile the Task's PR row against GitHub/git — a merged PR should mark the row merged so completion proceeds. This currently strands a fully-shipped Task's completion and re-cycles the flow into pursue with nothing to do.\n2. '.lf/scratch-stash/' is not gitignored. 'lf pr land' restored scratch to '.lf/scratch-stash/<branch>-<ts>/', which sits OUTSIDE the ignored '.lf/tmp/' (siblings .lf/log,logs,prompts,journal,summaries,tmp are ignored; scratch-stash is not). It dirties the worktree and blocks 'lf task complete' ('Task worktree has uncommitted changes'). Wave memory documents the stash path as '.lf/tmp/scratch-stash/<branch>-<ts>/' (which IS ignored) — so the code drifted out from under the ignored prefix. Fix: either restore under '.lf/tmp/scratch-stash/' (matches the documented, already-ignored path) or add '.lf/scratch-stash/' to .gitignore. One line either way.","rank":87,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"23094a3b-f8fa-43a4-a04d-4390d351945f","identifier":"LOO-100","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-100/verify-the-required-ui-host-gate-55-on-the-maintained-permissioned","name":"Verify the required --ui-host gate 5/5 on the maintained permissioned macOS host","description":"W2-116 shipped the bounded+honest gate and the separately named required --ui-host gate (PR #905). The one proof item that cannot run headlessly remains: on the maintained permissioned macOS host, run 'uv run python scripts/test.py --ui-host' 5 times and confirm 5/5 clean runs with the hosted LoopflowUITests actually executing. Grant Automation + Accessibility to the test runner if absent (release/UI_HOST_GATE.md). The headless simulation path (LF_UI_HOST_SIMULATE_NO_PERMISSION=1) and classification are already proven in CI/tests; this is the real-host execution proof only.","rank":88,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"dd9ca7a7-09ed-4253-a0d1-69005b57b659","identifier":"LOO-101","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-101/make-discord-authority-survive-home-and-ssh-boundaries","name":"Make Discord authority survive Home and SSH boundaries","description":"## Outcome\n\nDiscord chat works in local development, foreground remote development, and durable remote Wave operation without leaking connector authority or depending on the origin laptop.\n\n## Contract\n\n* Discord is an external messaging provider like Linear or GitHub, not an agent provider.\n* Doppler is the only secret source. Foreground remote work may use `lf ssh --secret LF_DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN <home> ...`.\n* Durable detached work continues to scrub forwarded authority. Installed `lfd` uses the credential installed on its own Home.\n* Wave listeners may use the token; resident provider children and their descendants may not inherit it.\n* Missing, expired, or rejected authority degrades Discord delivery with an actionable reason and does not kill unrelated Wave work.\n* Status distinguishes absent local installation, borrowed foreground authority, authenticated provider, permission failure, and Discord unavailability.\n\n## Done when\n\nA foreground remote Wave demo succeeds with an explicitly forwarded secret. The same command cannot leave a detached resident holding borrowed authority. A Home-installed `lfd` survives SSH exit and machine service restart while Discord remains usable. Process-environment tests prove the provider child lacks the token, logs contain no credential, and every failure state names the supported recovery.","rank":89,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"b4c6ccf2-78aa-4b49-b009-d48d84693ca9","identifier":"LOO-103","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-103/make-local-lf-schema-complete-across-migrations","name":"Make local lf schema-complete across migrations","description":"Incident: a clean-main lf binary was promoted with runtime queries for draft schema but only the released migration registry, causing agent_invocations launch failures. Rollback to tagged v0.12.4 exposed a released durable-input migration defect: 1,818 legacy nanosecond timestamps were copied into Unix-second columns. The local database was backed up at ~/.lf/loopflow.db.backup-incident-timestamp-units-1784521517 and repaired in place by dividing only out-of-range epoch_revisions.created_at, steers.issued_at, and tool_responses.responded_at values by 1,000,000,000; no rows were discarded. A checkpoint named incident-local-lf-schema-closure is in git stash and contains the installer guard, regression test, and 5 Whys. Apply that checkpoint in the Task worktree. Add a durable repair migration and behavioral proof for timestamp units. Resolve schema closure at the build/promotion boundary so local main can never replace lf with code newer than its embedded schema. Use incident first, ship-5whys for loop work, and ship finally.","rank":90,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"7f57cd70-107f-404a-888c-c3c6b2262a19","identifier":"LOO-106","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-106/stop-partial-trace-captures-from-accumulating-after-terminal-pruning","name":"Stop partial trace captures from accumulating after terminal pruning","description":"## Evidence\n\nThe 2026-07-18 telemetry-daily run used published revision 1a3079a94e04cb31cecafa6f8b10f4e94c910f7f against ~/.lf/loopflow.db at migration 0.11.029_ci_incident_repaired_head. Doctor failed capture integrity with 787 failures across 2,262 launches and 2,272 turns; sampled records were still classified partial.\n\nENG-85 / PR #1017 is terminal and claimed that retained captures would resolve or carry an explicit pruned state, with doctor distinguishing historical pruning from fresh loss. Its original evidence was 235 failures on 2026-07-15. The count has more than tripled, so either new runs still create live-looking partial references or reconciliation does not cover the current store shape. Keep ENG-85 closed; this fresh Task owns the regression.\n\nDoctor also reported the running binary 14 merged commits behind origin/main. Establish whether any later merged change already fixes the regression before writing another implementation. Do not treat binary freshness alone as proof: reproduce against the published revision and current main on copied production-shaped stores.\n\n## Outcome\n\nA retained capture is either resolvable or explicitly terminal with an actionable reason. Fresh interrupted/failed runs cannot grow the partial count, historical rows reconcile idempotently, and telemetry-daily exits zero unless it observes genuinely new capture loss.\n\n## Proof\n\n* Partition the 787 failures by creation time, terminal outcome, provider, and artifact state to identify the post-ENG-85 path.\n* Reproduce at least one current path that creates a partial record, or prove current main already prevents it and only published-binary drift remains.\n* Run doctor on copied production-shaped state before and after reconciliation; preserve the real database untouched.\n* Prove a second reconciliation is a no-op and a fresh interrupted run becomes explicit terminal state rather than partial.\n* Keep the fix inside capture lifecycle/reconciliation and doctor classification; do not add a parallel telemetry store.\n\n## KR\n\nAdvances zero avoidable human repair and zero stranded dead bodies: capture integrity must expose fresh loss without leaving the health surface permanently red.","rank":91,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"cb148bbb-d55e-46f8-89e1-4f629089a02c","identifier":"LOO-107","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-107/separate-exact-frontier-cli-repair-from-migration-bearing-promotion","name":"Separate exact-frontier CLI repair from migration-bearing promotion","description":"## Incident\n\nOn 2026-07-17/18, ~/.local/bin/lf remained on migration frontier 0.11.027 while the shared store was at 0.11.029. Every agent-backed `lf` command failed before launch. A compatible exact-main candidate existed, but `lf install promote` refused because 34 Task/Project bodies were active or reserved. This machine runs resident waves continuously, so a zero-body window is not an operationally available repair path.\n\n## 5 Whys\n\n1. Why did `lf code` fail? The installed global binary did not recognize the shared database frontier.\n2. Why was the compatible binary not installed? Promotion rejects every replacement while any body is live.\n3. Why did the bodies not drain? Tasks, Projects, and resident waves are intentionally continuous; several leases live for hours or days.\n4. Why does an exact-compatible symlink repair require a full drain? Promotion uses one strongest-possible fence for both executable repointing and schema-mutating migration advancement.\n5. Why could this become a lasting outage? There is no supported exact-frontier repair operation or invariant that restores the globally resolved CLI while preserving pinned running bodies.\n\n## Root cause\n\nThe promotion model conflates two different risks. Applying a migration can invalidate running binaries and requires exclusive ownership plus a drained body set. Atomically repointing the global CLI to a published candidate that already exactly recognizes the current frontier does not mutate the database; existing processes retain their loaded/pinned binary, while new processes need the repaired one. Requiring the migration fence for the latter turns continuous residency into permanent unavailability.\n\n## Outcome\n\nSplit promotion policy by operation. An exact-frontier published candidate may atomically repair the global CLI target under the promotion lock without draining bodies or writing the database. Any pending migration, divergent lineage, checksum mismatch, unreadable evidence, or app/helper bundle replacement with incompatible evidence remains fail-closed; migration-bearing promotion still requires zero live/reserved bodies.\n\nPin running child control binaries explicitly so a CLI repoint cannot silently change their executable mid-session. Retain the prior global target as rollback evidence.\n\n## Proof\n\nA deterministic regression holds multiple active and reserved Task/Project leases, places the global CLI on an older unknown-frontier binary, and presents a published exact-frontier candidate. Exact repair succeeds atomically, performs no database write, new invocations open the store and establish trace capture, running bodies retain their pinned executable, and rollback bytes remain. The same fixture proves a pending-migration or divergent candidate still refuses before changing the target. Add an operational health signal when the globally resolved CLI cannot recognize the shared frontier.","rank":92,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"99b7927f-43fe-4e71-8440-bc4ba97fef94","identifier":"LOO-108","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-108/let-reconciliation-trust-the-settled-pr-set-plus-actual-linear","name":"Let reconciliation trust the settled PR set plus actual Linear completion","description":"<!-- loopflow-task-start:f636ca25b6bdb590fd9b5c57db9ff59f6aba2dce8fc129af9316ea103ab3e5bc -->","rank":93,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"cb9b0e72-122e-40e6-9db2-1e8c1c71d129","identifier":"LOO-109","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-109/duplicate-stop-sending-removed-subtitle-in-linear-pr-attachments","name":"[duplicate] Stop sending removed subtitle in Linear PR attachments","description":"Duplicate of ENG-79, which already owns removing the obsolete Linear PR-attachment argument and adding schema validation in CI. Keep one implementation task.","rank":94,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"19b48108-b719-419b-9252-1258468dae17","identifier":"LOO-110","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-110/duplicate-project-pursuit-successor-path-shipped-by-w2-140","name":"[duplicate] Project pursuit successor path shipped by W2-140","description":"Closed as a duplicate of W2-140. PR #894 shipped append-only terminal Project Session history, exactly one current successor, task-launch adoption, and predecessor/successor audit identity. Keeping a second open Task would describe work already on main.","rank":95,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"dfbdcf52-4cb6-4d01-a3bd-c3e0560f490d","identifier":"LOO-111","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-111/make-shared-db-migrations-exclusive-to-promoted-lf","name":"Make shared DB migrations exclusive to promoted lf","description":"## The problem\n\nOn 2026-07-17, `~/.local/bin/lf` (0.11.3, latest known migration `0.11.027_accounts_first`) stopped launching runs because the shared store had advanced to `0.11.029_ci_incident_repaired_head`. The exact-main candidate at `target/release/lf` works, but its install preflight correctly refuses global promotion while 31 Task/Project bodies are live.\n\n## 5 Whys\n\nProblem → an unpromoted candidate advanced the shared DB → the installed binary could no longer read it → store migration and binary promotion are separate operations → promotion authority is enforced only when replacing the global target → no single owner controls the shared migration frontier.\n\n**Why 1:** The exact-main candidate opened `~/.lf/loopflow.db` and automatically applied migrations through `.029`.\n\n**Why 2:** The globally installed binary still knows only through `.027`, so journal/trace setup fails before every run.\n\n**Why 3:** The promotion fence refused to replace the global binary while 31 bodies were live, but nothing prevented that candidate from migrating the shared DB first.\n\n**Why 4:** Candidate provenance and authority are checked at `lf install promote`; ordinary store open still treats migration application as an unconditional local side effect.\n\n**Why 5 (root):** Binary publication and shared-store frontier advancement have no single transactional owner. A development/candidate runtime can revoke compatibility for the installed runtime without becoming the installed runtime.\n\n## Unanswered whys\n\n* Which command first opened the shared store with `.029`, and can the ledger identify it deterministically?\n* Should non-promoted binaries be read-only against the shared store, refuse it entirely when migrations are pending, or automatically use an isolated dev DB?\n* What exact ordering lets promotion switch the binary and migrate the store under one exclusive boundary after live bodies drain?\n\n## Fixes\n\n* **Immediate:** keep live bodies intact; use the exact-main candidate explicitly until the safe promotion boundary opens, then promote through `lf install promote`. Never downgrade or edit migration rows.\n* **Structural:** only an authorized promoted runtime may advance the shared DB frontier. Candidate/dev binaries must refuse pending shared migrations or use an isolated fresh DB.\n* **Systemic:** make global binary publication plus shared-store migration one owned release/install operation under the promotion lock and live-body fence. Dev mode assumes a fresh isolated DB between releases.\n\n## Done when\n\n* Reproduce installed-known `.027` + candidate-known `.029` + live bodies.\n* Running the candidate cannot advance the shared DB or break installed `lf`.\n* Safe promotion owns the frontier transition and leaves the newly installed `lf` usable.\n* Candidate/dev workflows use an isolated fresh DB by default or fail with one actionable command.\n* Regression proves the old installed binary remains usable until promotion commits.\n* Document the exact release boundary and remove any alternate migration path.","rank":96,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"7675a87f-02a7-433a-8ffb-9bd7c97fffcb","identifier":"LOO-112","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-112/allow-an-abandoned-legacy-project-session-to-be-recreated","name":"Allow an abandoned legacy Project Session to be recreated","description":"Closed as a duplicate of W2-140. W2-140 owns append-only Project Session successors after terminal intent, convergence between project/task launches, traceable predecessor history, and the lifecycle tests. Do not implement a second successor model here.","rank":97,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"d61ce343-d957-4279-aeeb-e34acf6a622c","identifier":"LOO-113","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-113/obsolete-treating-the-ambient-wave-uuid-as-a-launcher-bug","name":"[obsolete] Treating the ambient Wave UUID as a launcher bug","description":"Closed because the premise is obsolete: managed Wave/Project/Task processes intentionally carry the durable Wave UUID in LF_WAVE_ID, and current Task/Project launch succeeds with that identity. The remaining defect is inconsistent command-side resolution, now owned end to end by W2-151, “Make every CLI command resolve managed Wave context consistently.”","rank":98,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"7b21c637-7f75-47cd-b2c7-eacdcb4b53bf","identifier":"LOO-114","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-114/asana-oauth-creds-self-serve-from-doppler","name":"Asana OAuth creds self-serve from Doppler","description":"oauth_client_credentials (rust/loopflow/src/lfd/provider_auth.rs:1834) reads ASANA_CLIENT_ID/ASANA_CLIENT_SECRET only from std::env::var and errors 'set X and Y to enable Asana OAuth' when absent. Result: lf op pm / auth only works when a human hand-wraps it in 'doppler run', so the unattended wave loop can't read or write its roadmap. Fix: when the env vars are missing, fall back to fetching them from Doppler (the codebase already shells out to doppler in extract_doppler_token / Command::new(\"doppler\")). Then the CLI self-serves the credential; no doppler-run wrapper, no human. Serves the 'agents run unattended' metric.","rank":99,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"a2aa07c7-897b-4d9a-9c0a-3fca1f027b61","identifier":"LOO-115","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-115/obsolete-asana-doppler-fallback-asana-removed-as-pm-provider","name":"[obsolete] Asana Doppler fallback — Asana removed as PM provider","description":"Obsolete: Asana removed entirely; Linear is the only supported PM provider (lfd/pm/mod.rs rejects non-linear). Nothing to wire.","rank":100,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"c9885380-4738-4c4b-b72a-1b7d24cf079e","identifier":"LOO-116","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-116/obsolete-asana-token-expiry-asana-removed-as-pm-provider","name":"[obsolete] Asana token expiry — Asana removed as PM provider","description":"Obsolete: Asana no longer a PM provider (Linear only). Unattended-agent token lifetime is a non-issue now.","rank":101,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"23b01f30-a58f-4887-9cdc-3d965c50fa18","identifier":"LOO-117","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-117/lf-op-next-fails-in-worktrees-resolved-by-removing-nextadvance-in","name":"lf op next fails in worktrees — resolved by removing next/advance in worktree stage 3b","description":"Not a checkout-collision fix: the worktree redesign (PR #818) removed land rotation, and stage 3b (Worker-mind runtime) retires `lf op next`/`advance_branch`/`next_wave_handler` entirely — Rust + wire + Swift. Ephemeral rotation is replaced by workers that fork-from and target parent(). Close this when 3b lands. See wave/systems/MEMORY.md → Next.","rank":102,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"54f17e72-f1e9-40a9-ab75-b3ea58a077d9","identifier":"LOO-118","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-118/make-the-full-local-release-gate-trustworthy-and-bounded","name":"Make the full local release gate trustworthy and bounded","description":"User story\n\nBefore a human or worker opens or lands a Loopflow PR, one local command should produce a definitive, timely release signal across Rust, Python, Swift, website, smoke, and UI behavior. Nobody should have to interpret a hung runner as success, retry until green, or remember which suite is secretly excluded.\n\nScope\n\n* Reproduce and classify the LoopflowUITests-Runner hang on a CI-like headless Mac, including the macOS Automation and runner-connection boundary.\n* Make `uv run python scripts/test.py --all` terminate inside a named budget with phase-level progress and an actionable failure.\n* If UI execution genuinely requires a permissioned host, make the split explicit: the ordinary gate must say what it proved, while a separately named required host gate owns the real UI run. Do not silently skip it.\n* Preserve failure artifacts and a one-command reproduction so a worker can repair the first red signal without opening Xcode by hand.\n* Measure the full gate on the living repository and keep that budget visible in release evidence.\n\nProof\n\nTen consecutive headless invocations complete with an honest pass or actionable fail; the permissioned UI gate completes 5/5 times on its maintained host; no phase can hang indefinitely; and a simulated runner bootstrap failure names the missing capability and next action.","rank":103,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"3ddc333b-5e0a-4edc-8c6a-41205f3be7da","identifier":"LOO-119","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-119/obsolete-label-migration-replaced-by-native-linear-hierarchy","name":"[obsolete] Label migration replaced by native Linear hierarchy","description":"The project:<slug> label migration and lf op PM commands no longer exist. Loopflow now uses Wave to Linear Initiative, Project to Linear Project, and Task to Issue through top-level lf pm commands. The live infrastructure wave is already bound to Initiative 218967b6-a760-4b7c-9a46-11d9d61a42c2 and the cache resolves all tasks to native projects. Close this obsolete migration task; diagnose future drift with lf pm sync --plan.","rank":104,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"7c8f66d0-6918-4d6f-a20f-488945c91366","identifier":"LOO-120","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-120/align-lf-op-pm-with-waveprojecttask-planning","name":"Align lf op pm with wave/project/task planning","description":"Implement the API pass in scratch/pm-api-wave-projects.md. The new model is wave = durable context, project = measured bet in wave/<wave>/projects/<project>.md, task = Linear issue. Add project-aware task creation/filtering, PM space rename/sync/doctor, stranded-space detection, and migration support for the current Product/Intelligence/Infrastructure roster. This belongs under Infrastructure: Technical Architecture + Developer Efficiency.","rank":105,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"eff79124-a06a-4a83-ba49-bc95917094d1","identifier":"LOO-121","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-121/duplicate-persist-child-base-commit-for-stacked-rebase","name":"[duplicate] Persist child base commit for stacked rebase","description":"Collapsed into W2-93, which now owns the current lf rebase behavior for both merged and pruned parents. Keep one implementation task and one future PR; the retired queue and lf op surfaces are not coming back.","rank":106,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"914003d9-a99e-4f91-bf22-cd6eff4aabae","identifier":"LOO-122","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-122/deliver-stacked-tasks-after-parent-merge-with-zero-manual-git-repair","name":"Deliver stacked Tasks after parent merge with zero manual Git repair","description":"User story\n\nA human or agent can stack a Task on unfinished work, merge or prune the parent, and continue shipping the child without rebasing by hand, reconstructing branch ancestry, or accidentally reintroducing commits already represented by a squash merge.\n\nEnd-to-end contract\n\n* Persist the child's actual base commit and parent relationship at placement; branch names remain readable hints, never the source of truth.\n* After a parent squash-merges, move the child onto `origin/main`, omit parent changes already represented upstream, and preserve only child-authored work.\n* After a local parent ref is pruned or diverges, use durable placement metadata to choose the safe deterministic outcome or stop with the exact repair required.\n* Clear obsolete parent metadata when the stack collapses, while preserving audit history.\n* Make `lf rebase --plan`, execution, Task status, and PR range agree about the chosen base.\n\nProof\n\nIntegration tests cover merged, squash-merged, pruned, and divergent parents. In a dogfood stack of at least three serial PRs, every child lands with the expected diff and zero manual Git commands; unsafe ancestry stops before rewriting history and names the conflicting commits. This Task absorbs the earlier narrow “persist child base commit” diagnosis.","rank":107,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"a3d1cb8f-8cf2-4f2b-b0d4-7c15fcf75fd2","identifier":"LOO-123","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-123/assign-canonical-migration-order-at-release-cut-not-on-parallel","name":"Assign canonical migration order at release cut, not on parallel branches","description":"## Outcome\n\nParallel schema work never contends for, reserves, or leaks a canonical migration ordinal. Developers author schema changes under stable non-ordinal identities against disposable development databases. The release PR is the single publication boundary that orders the accumulated changes, assigns release-scoped ordinals, proves the upgrade from the previous release, and makes the resulting migration chain immutable.\n\n## Live evidence\n\nThe current allocator chooses the next `<major>.<minor>.<ordinal>` while a branch is active and treats `origin/main` as durable publication because creator dogfooding runs ahead of tags.\n\nThat has produced the failure shape this Task removes:\n\n* main currently contains `0.11.027_accounts_first` followed by `0.11.029_ci_incident_repaired_head`;\n* `0.11.028_task_gate_settlement` is still isolated on open PR #1052 / W2-302;\n* if #1052 lands after 029 has already been applied by a main build, the canonical source order and a live ledger can disagree even though each branch was locally reasonable;\n* allocating and renumbering ordinals during rebase makes independent schema work coordinate on a serial number rather than on its real data dependency.\n\nThe migration mismatch that broke `lf code-review` on 2026-07-17 is related evidence: a branch-selected global binary encountered a shared store whose migration frontier came from another build. [W2-319](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/W2-319/authorize-global-lf-promotion-against-the-shared-migration-frontier) owns promotion safety. This Task changes when migration identities become canonical.\n\n## Existing foundation\n\nDevelopment builds already default to `~/.lf-dev/worktrees/<source-identity>/loopflow.db`, may migrate that isolated store, and refuse the production `~/.lf/loopflow.db`. Published builds own the shared release store.\n\n`lf release run` already creates a dedicated release worktree and PR, waits for it to merge, then tags that exact commit. Today it only verifies the already-numbered chain before preparing the release. Move canonicalization into that existing release boundary rather than creating a second release mechanism.\n\n## Required behavior\n\n### Development\n\n* A schema-changing PR adds a stable, collision-free draft migration identity that does not contain a canonical ordinal. Prefer a content/task identity plus an explicit human-readable name; never coordinate through \"next number.\"\n* Development and test runs assume a disposable per-source database. A schema frontier change recreates that development database or otherwise proves a fresh initialization without touching the release-owned store.\n* The fresh schema and any data-transform logic remain testable before release. If draft migrations depend on one another, dependency metadata—not merge timing or an ordinal—must express that ordering.\n* Main and branch development binaries remain unable to advance the shared release database.\n\n### Release\n\n* The release PR freezes exactly the draft migration set included in the release.\n* It rejects missing, cyclic, or ambiguous data dependencies before any tag or shared-store write.\n* It deterministically orders the frozen set, assigns the next canonical ordinals in the release namespace, rewrites/registers the canonical files, and leaves no included drafts behind.\n* A retry of the same release produces the same canonical identities and diff.\n* The release gate migrates a fixture at the previous released frontier through the newly canonical chain, validates checksums/schema/foreign keys, and also proves fresh initialization.\n* Only the merged release commit is published migration authority. After that merge, canonical migration names and bytes are immutable.\n* Patch and minor releases choose namespaces from the version being cut; a minor release starts its own ordinal sequence.\n\n## User-visible contract\n\n```\nuv run python scripts/new_migration.py add_wave_colour\n# creates a uniquely named draft; no ordinal allocation and no fetch/rebase race\n\ncargo test\n# uses a fresh per-source database and proves current-head schema behavior\n\nlf release run patch\n# release PR orders drafts, assigns canonical ids, proves last-release upgrade,\n# merges, then tags the exact canonical commit\n```\n\nA development command that points explicitly or indirectly at `~/.lf/loopflow.db` fails closed and tells the user to use the installed release. Deleting/recreating state is allowed only inside the identified development-store universe.\n\n## Proof\n\n* Two independent branches create schema changes concurrently with no shared ordinal and merge in either order without renaming.\n* Two drafts with a real dependency are ordered by that dependency; a cycle fails the release before publication.\n* A release containing multiple drafts assigns a deterministic contiguous tail after the last released migration and migrates the previous-release fixture successfully.\n* Re-running release preparation yields no migration diff.\n* A failed/abandoned release PR does not publish its assigned ordinals; a later release can deterministically regenerate them.\n* Development schema changes recreate only their disposable source-owned store and can never delete, initialize, or migrate the shared release store.\n* The release artifact opens the prior release database, applies the canonical tail once, and a second open is a no-op.\n* `scripts/check_migrations.py`, runtime provenance, doctor output, release docs, and tests all describe the same publication boundary.\n\n## Design pressure\n\nKeep one migration model. Do not retain branch ordinals as a second quasi-canonical namespace and do not add a central ordinal reservation service. Stable draft identity plus release-time canonicalization should replace branch-time allocation.\n\nData migrations are the hard case: \"fresh DB in development\" must not mean their transformation logic goes untested until release. The design must provide a deterministic pre-release proof from the last released fixture without making ordinals the dependency graph.\n\n## Exclusions\n\n* Do not weaken W2-319's shared-store promotion fence.\n* Do not make production databases disposable.\n* Do not infer migration order from filename sorting, PR number, or wall-clock time when a data dependency exists.\n* Do not retroactively rename already released migrations.\n* Repairing the current 028/029 live history is separate operational cleanup; this Task prevents recurrence and may specify the forward-compatible transition.","rank":108,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"c998b775-13e1-4378-ba6c-0013e2be829a","identifier":"LOO-124","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-124/finish-global-install-cutover-and-verified-rollback-after-w2-319","name":"Finish global install cutover and verified rollback after W2-319","description":"## Outcome\n\nComplete the promotion boundary left incomplete when W2-319 PR #1077 merged as 88b295ff16d9eb7f6c21218bde5bbdf5450996ef during a dequeue/review race. Every supported machine-global install mutation must use the merged Rust promotion transaction, and any advertised rollback binary must be proven compatible with the current live migration frontier.\n\n## Exact-head findings\n\n* PR #1077 adds a safe low-level lf install promote command and reservation fence, but no supported caller routes scripts/install.py local --use, refresh/no-pull/custom-dir paths, app or bundled install helpers, or post-install skill sync through it. Those paths can still bypass the live-body and migration-frontier checks.\n* preserve_prior_binary copies the current target and labels it prior compatible without validating that executable against the current store frontier. The incident that created W2-319 began with an incompatible active global binary, so retained bytes alone are not a safe rollback candidate.\n\n## Done when\n\nRoute every supported global mutation entry point through one atomic Rust promotion boundary and delete direct Python/app copy or symlink swaps. Validate a retained prior executable against the current store before advertising or activating it; incompatible bytes may remain historical evidence only. Add behavioral tests that fail if any supported entry point bypasses promotion and prove incompatible rollback rejection. Preserve the #1077 reservation fence, candidate-first activation, immutable staging, and post-failure compatible-command guarantee. Run full CI, receive exact-head Project review, land, and verify terminal state.","rank":109,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"e7533602-4d94-457a-b871-c778d214f70e","identifier":"LOO-125","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-125/move-all-legacy-issues-before-narrowing-linear-project-teams","name":"Move all legacy issues before narrowing Linear Project teams","description":"## Outcome\n\nComplete the existing-item portion of the per-wave Linear team migration without stopping healthy agents.\n\nNew task creation is already cut over by PR #1072: Infrastructure creates ENG issues, Intelligence creates SCI issues, and Product creates PRD issues. ENG-22 itself is the live Infrastructure proof. Existing W2 bodies may finish naturally; no active agent needs to be killed merely to stop new W2 creation.\n\n## Live failure\n\nOn 2026-07-17, after wave/intelligence was bound to SCI:\n\n* `lf pm reteam --wave intelligence` said 16 completed W2 issues would stay historical while both Projects would narrow from [SCI, W2] to SCI.\n* `lf pm reteam --wave intelligence --apply` failed on the first Project update: `loopflowstudio/loopflow cannot be removed from the project as they own issues that are part of the project.`\n* No issue moved because reteam updates Projects first.\n\nLinear will not remove a team from a Project while issues from that team remain in it. The current promises—retain historical W2 issues but require exactly one Project team—cannot both hold. The user wants the team migration completed and stale W2 labels removed.\n\n## Required behavior\n\n1. Reteam every foreign-team issue under the wave Initiative, including completed issues. Preserve stable issue UUIDs and record the previous identifier in a traceability comment.\n2. Protect any issue whose Task Session still has a writing lease. Apply remains fail-closed before the first provider mutation when any protected body exists.\n3. Move issues before replacing each Project's teamIds, so Linear permits legacy-team removal.\n4. Make interrupted/partial provider runs safely rerunnable. Reconcile cached Task Session identifiers for moved and already-moved issues.\n5. Remove the misleading historical-retention result/output and tests. Add behavioral tests for completed issue migration and provider mutation order.\n6. Keep scope in reteam/Linear PM. Do not touch active Task worktrees and do not mutate live provider state from the implementation body.\n7. Run targeted tests, cargo fmt, and cargo clippy -- -D warnings. Publish, receive Project review, land, and complete.\n8. After merge, the orchestrator will apply Intelligence immediately, then Product and Infrastructure as their active legacy writers naturally become terminal.\n\n## Proof\n\n* Intelligence apply succeeds and a second dry-run is a no-op.\n* Product and Infrastructure dry-runs continue to name protected live writers without creating new W2 work.\n* After those bodies settle, apply leaves all Projects on exactly one wave team, all issues on that team, local Task identifiers reconciled, and pm doctor without stranded-team diagnostics.","rank":110,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"3d9f4996-1824-471c-afc3-dd30415fe651","identifier":"LOO-126","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-126/complete-release-boundary-migration-canonicalization-after-eng-23","name":"Complete release-boundary migration canonicalization after ENG-23","description":"## Outcome\n\nRepair the incomplete ENG-23 implementation merged in PR #1076. The release PR must be the executable, atomic publication boundary for collision-free draft migrations and must prove the canonical tail against real databases.\n\n## Findings from exact-head review\n\n* `lf release run` has no caller for `scripts/canonicalize_migrations.py`; the standalone script and docs claim an integration that does not exist.\n* `scripts/new_migration.py` tells authors to append `DraftMigration` to a `DRAFTS` slice, but neither the type nor registry exists. Draft SQL therefore has no executable pre-release registration path.\n* Draft identity is only a human snake_case name, so concurrent same-name branches still collide and share a registry edit if the printed instruction is followed.\n* A draft dependency must resolve against either the frozen draft set or an already-canonical migration. The merged code rejects a dependency once its upstream draft crossed an earlier release boundary.\n* Canonicalization writes files and deletes drafts before all validation succeeds; a graph, registry, or write failure must leave the tree byte-identical.\n* Tests only exercise filesystem rewriting. They do not migrate a versioned previous-release fixture through the generated canonical tail, prove fresh initialization, preserve the prior input fixture in the release PR, or require Rust CI before merge/tag.\n\n## Done when\n\nUse an immutable task/content identity plus readable name with generated registration, integrate validate-then-atomically-install canonicalization into the existing release worktree after version bump and before commit, support dependencies across release boundaries, and add two-generation prior/target fixtures plus real upgrade and fresh-init proof. Prove two branches have no shared registry edit, invalid graph/write failure is byte-identical, the release PR retains its prior input fixture, and the merged release path—not a manual script invocation—is the only publication authority. Preserve W2-319 promotion safety.","rank":111,"completed":true,"project_id":"3778bc54-6fcb-4f38-b262-8eb68bcaa602","project":"performance-efficiency","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null}]}
